export const TOPICS = {
  ...IN_A_HURRY_TOPICS,
  ...QUESTION_BREAKDOWN_TOPICS,

  // ── CORE CONCEPTS ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  'networking-essentials': {
    slug: 'networking-essentials',
    title: 'Networking Essentials',
    subtitle: 'DNS, TCP/IP, HTTP, WebSockets — the foundation of every system you will ever design',
    duration: '30 min', difficulty: 'Beginner',
    sections: [
      { type: 'text', heading: 'Why Networking Matters', body: `Every system design interview involves data moving between machines. When someone says "the app is slow," understanding networking is how you figure out whether the problem is your server or the road to get there.\n\nYou will not be asked to implement TCP from scratch. But you will be asked things like "how does the client connect to the server?" or "should we use WebSockets or polling here?" or "why is this request taking 500ms?" Networking gives you the vocabulary and mental model to answer these confidently.` },
      { type: 'text', heading: 'The Networking Stack: OSI Model in 60 Seconds', body: `Networks are built in layers, each handling a different concern. You do not need to memorize all seven OSI layers, but understanding three of them is essential for system design.\n\nThe Network Layer (Layer 3) is where IP lives. It handles addressing and routing — getting packets from one machine to another across the internet. Every device gets an IP address, and routers use these addresses to forward packets toward their destination.\n\nThe Transport Layer (Layer 4) is where TCP and UDP live. This layer provides end-to-end communication between applications. TCP adds reliability and ordering on top of IP. UDP skips those guarantees for speed.\n\nThe Application Layer (Layer 7) is where HTTP, DNS, WebSockets, and gRPC live. These are the protocols your application code actually interacts with. When you hear "Layer 7 load balancer," it means the load balancer understands HTTP and can make routing decisions based on URLs, headers, and cookies. A "Layer 4 load balancer" only sees TCP connections and IP addresses — faster but less intelligent.\n\nWhy does this matter? When you draw a load balancer in your system design, knowing whether it operates at L4 or L7 determines what it can do. When you choose between TCP and UDP for a feature, you are making a Layer 4 decision. When you pick between REST and WebSockets, that is a Layer 7 decision.` },
      { type: 'diagram', variant: 'osi-layers', heading: 'The OSI Model', caption: 'Seven layers — but only three matter for system design interviews (highlighted in color).' },
      { type: 'text', heading: 'IP Addressing: Public, Private, and NAT', body: `Every device on a network has an IP address. There are two types that matter for system design.\n\nPublic IP addresses are routable on the internet. When you deploy a server on AWS, it gets a public IP that anyone in the world can reach. These are scarce (IPv4 has only about 4.3 billion addresses) and expensive.\n\nPrivate IP addresses (like 10.x.x.x or 192.168.x.x) are only reachable within a private network. Your application servers, databases, and caches should all live on private IPs inside a Virtual Private Cloud (VPC). They cannot be reached from the internet directly — which is exactly what you want for security.\n\nNAT (Network Address Translation) bridges the gap. It allows devices with private IPs to access the internet by translating their private address to a public one. This is how your home router works — all your devices share one public IP.\n\nIn system design, the key principle is: only load balancers and API gateways need public IPs. Everything else — application servers, databases, caches, message queues — should be on private networks. This is a basic security practice that interviewers expect you to know.` },
      { type: 'animation', id: 'vpc-architecture', heading: 'VPC Architecture: Public vs Private', body: 'Only the load balancer is exposed to the internet. Everything else lives in a private network with no direct internet access.' },
      { type: 'text', heading: 'The Networking Stack: Layers That Matter', body: `Networks are built in layers, each one abstracting away the complexity below it. The full OSI model has 7 layers, but for system design interviews, you only need to care about three:\n\nThe Network Layer (Layer 3) is where IP lives. It handles addressing and routing — given a destination IP address, the network figures out how to get your data there by hopping through routers across the internet. Every device on the internet has an IP address. IPv4 addresses look like 192.168.1.1 (32 bits, about 4.3 billion possible addresses — we are running out). IPv6 addresses are much longer and provide essentially unlimited addresses, but adoption is still gradual. In system design, you will encounter public IPs (routable on the internet) and private IPs (used inside your network, like 10.x.x.x or 192.168.x.x). Your application servers typically have private IPs, and only your load balancer has a public IP.\n\nThe Transport Layer (Layer 4) is where TCP and UDP live. This layer provides end-to-end communication between applications. TCP gives you reliable, ordered delivery. UDP gives you speed without guarantees. When you hear "Layer 4 load balancer," it means the load balancer makes routing decisions based on IP and port without looking at the HTTP content.\n\nThe Application Layer (Layer 7) is where HTTP, DNS, WebSockets, and gRPC live. This is where most developers spend their time. When you hear "Layer 7 load balancer," it means the load balancer can inspect HTTP headers, URLs, and cookies to make smarter routing decisions.\n\nThese layers work together like nesting dolls. Your HTTP request (Layer 7) is wrapped in a TCP segment (Layer 4), which is wrapped in an IP packet (Layer 3), which is wrapped in an Ethernet frame (Layer 2) and sent as electrical signals or light pulses over a wire or fiber (Layer 1). At the other end, each layer is unwrapped in reverse order until the server gets your HTTP request.` },
      { type: 'animation', id: 'http-request', heading: 'The Journey of a Request', body: 'When you type a URL, a chain of events happens before you see a single pixel. Each step can fail. Each step can be slow.' },
      { type: 'text', heading: 'What Actually Happens When You Visit a Website', body: `When you type "google.com" into your browser, here is what happens behind the scenes:\n\nFirst, your browser needs to figure out where google.com actually lives. It asks DNS — the internet\'s address book — to translate "google.com" into an IP address like 142.250.80.46. This is like looking up a contact in your phone instead of memorizing their number.\n\nOnce the browser has the IP address, it opens a TCP connection. Think of this as a polite introduction — your browser says "Hey, want to talk?" (SYN), the server says "Sure, let\'s talk" (SYN-ACK), and your browser says "Great, here we go" (ACK). This three-way handshake guarantees both sides are ready, but it costs one full round trip before any actual data flows.\n\nIf the site uses HTTPS (and it should), there is an additional TLS handshake to set up encryption. TLS 1.3 does this in just one round trip. TLS 1.2 takes two.\n\nFinally, the browser sends the actual HTTP request — "GET /search?q=cats" — and the server responds with the page content. The browser parses the HTML, discovers it needs CSS, JavaScript, and images, and fires off more requests for those.\n\nFor a server in the same datacenter, this whole process takes a few milliseconds. For a server across the world? Each round trip adds 150-300ms. That is why understanding where latency comes from matters so much.` },
      { type: 'diagram', variant: 'url-journey', heading: 'What Happens When You Type a URL', caption: 'The complete journey from keystroke to rendered page — 8 steps, each adding latency.' },
      { type: 'animation', id: 'dns-resolution', heading: 'DNS: The Internet\'s Address Book', body: 'DNS translates human-readable domain names into IP addresses through a chain of nameservers. Results are cached at every level.' },
      { type: 'text', heading: 'DNS Deep Dive', body: `Think of DNS as the contact list on your phone. You do not memorize phone numbers — you save "Mom" and your phone knows to dial the right number. DNS works the same way. Humans remember names (google.com). Computers need numbers (142.250.80.46). DNS translates between them.\n\nWhen your browser needs to resolve a domain, it checks several caches first: the browser cache, then the operating system cache, then your ISP\'s recursive resolver. If none of them have the answer, the resolver walks up the DNS hierarchy — asking the root nameserver, then the TLD nameserver (.com), then the authoritative nameserver for that domain — until it gets the IP address.\n\nA fresh DNS lookup typically takes 10-100ms, but cached lookups are nearly instant. This is why TTL (Time To Live) matters. A high TTL (24 hours) means fewer DNS queries but slower propagation when you change your IP. A low TTL (60 seconds) means faster failover but more DNS traffic. For critical services, 1-5 minute TTLs give you the ability to redirect traffic quickly when things break.\n\nDNS also plays a role in system design beyond simple lookups. GeoDNS returns different IP addresses based on where the user is located — a user in India gets routed to your Mumbai datacenter, while a user in the US gets your Virginia datacenter. This reduces latency by keeping users close to their servers. However, DNS-based load balancing has serious limitations: it cannot do health checks (it will happily return the IP of a dead server), and caching means changes take minutes to propagate.` },
      { type: 'animation', id: 'tcp-handshake', heading: 'TCP: The Reliable Handshake', body: 'TCP\'s three-way handshake ensures both sides are ready before data flows. It costs one round trip of latency.' },
      { type: 'text', heading: 'IP Addressing, NAT, and Private Networks', body: `Every device on the internet needs an address. IPv4 gives us about 4.3 billion addresses (32-bit numbers like 192.168.1.1), which sounds like a lot until you realize there are far more devices than that. IPv6 solves this with 128-bit addresses, but adoption is slow.\n\nTo work around the IPv4 shortage, most networks use NAT (Network Address Translation). Your home router has one public IP address visible to the internet, but all your devices (phone, laptop, smart TV) have private IP addresses (like 192.168.x.x) that only exist inside your home network. When your laptop sends a request to google.com, the router translates the private IP to its public IP before sending it out, and translates back when the response comes in.\n\nIn system design, the same pattern applies at a larger scale. Your application servers, databases, and caches all sit in a private network (a VPC in AWS terms) with private IPs. Only your load balancer has a public IP. This is a fundamental security practice — your database should never be directly accessible from the internet. Traffic flows: Internet → Load Balancer (public IP) → App Servers (private IP) → Database (private IP, no internet access at all).` },
      { type: 'text', heading: 'TCP vs UDP: Reliability vs Speed', body: `TCP is like a phone call. Before you can talk, there is a ritual — you say hello, they say hello back, you confirm you can hear each other. Only then does the actual conversation begin. TCP guarantees that every piece of data arrives, in order, without corruption. If a packet gets lost, TCP detects it and resends it. This reliability comes at a cost: the three-way handshake adds one round trip of latency before any data flows, and retransmissions add more if the network is lossy.\n\nUDP is the rude alternative. No handshake, no guarantees. It just starts sending data and hopes for the best. Packets can arrive out of order, get duplicated, or not arrive at all. But it is significantly faster because there is no setup overhead and no waiting for acknowledgments.\n\nSo when do you use which? TCP is the default for almost everything on the web — HTTP, email, file transfers, database connections. You want reliability. UDP is for situations where speed matters more than perfection: video calls (a dropped frame is better than a delayed one), online gaming (you want the latest position, not a retransmission of an old one), and DNS queries (small, single-packet requests where the overhead of TCP setup is not worth it).\n\nThe key insight for system design: TCP trades latency for reliability. UDP trades reliability for speed. Most of the systems you will design use TCP (via HTTP). Real-time features like live video or gaming might use UDP.` },
      { type: 'text', heading: 'Latency vs Bandwidth', body: `These two concepts confuse people constantly, so let us make it simple with an analogy.\n\nLatency is how long it takes for data to travel from point A to point B. Think of it as the length of a highway. A 100-kilometer highway takes time to drive, no matter how many lanes it has.\n\nBandwidth is how much data can flow at once. Think of it as how many lanes the highway has. More lanes means more cars at the same time, but each car still takes the same time to reach the destination.\n\nSome numbers to internalize: same datacenter is about 0.5ms, same region is 5-20ms, cross-continent (New York to London) is about 50-100ms, and around the world (New York to Tokyo) is 150-300ms. Light in fiber travels roughly 200km per millisecond. New York to London is about 5,500km, which means 27ms one way, 55ms round trip. No amount of money or engineering beats physics.\n\nThe critical insight: more bandwidth does not reduce latency. They are different problems with different solutions. If your users in India are experiencing 300ms latency to your Virginia server, buying a bigger pipe will not help. You need to put a server closer to them (CDN, multi-region deployment).` },
      { type: 'text', heading: 'HTTP: How Browsers Talk to Servers', body: `HTTP is the language your browser uses to talk to servers. Think of it as a formal letter exchange. The browser sends a request ("I would like the user profile for user 123") and the server sends a response ("Here it is, along with a 200 OK status code").\n\nHTTP has evolved significantly over the years. HTTP/1.1, released in 1997, allows only one request at a time per connection. Browsers work around this by opening 6 parallel connections, but it is still a bottleneck. HTTP/2, released in 2015, introduced multiplexing — multiple requests can fly over a single connection simultaneously. It also compresses headers (which were surprisingly large in HTTP/1.1) and is roughly 2x faster for most workloads. HTTP/3, released in 2022, replaces TCP with QUIC (a UDP-based protocol) to eliminate a subtle problem called head-of-line blocking and reduce connection setup time. It is especially good on lossy networks like mobile.\n\nIn system design interviews, you do not need to go deep on HTTP versions. But mentioning that your system uses HTTP/2 for performance, or that HTTP/3 helps mobile clients, shows awareness.` },
      { type: 'text', heading: 'Real-Time Communication: When HTTP Is Not Enough', body: `Standard HTTP is request-response: the client asks, the server answers. But many features need the server to push data to clients without being asked — chat messages, live scores, stock prices, collaborative editing.\n\nThere are three main approaches, and knowing when to use each is a common interview question.\n\nWebSockets create a persistent, full-duplex connection. Once established, both the client and server can send messages at any time. Latency is very low (around 10ms) and the protocol is efficient for frequent messages. The trade-off is complexity: WebSocket connections are stateful, meaning a user is connected to a specific server. If that server goes down, the connection is lost. Scaling WebSockets requires sticky sessions or a pub/sub layer (like Redis Pub/Sub) so any server can route messages to any connected client. Use WebSockets for chat, collaborative editing, gaming, and live dashboards.\n\nServer-Sent Events (SSE) are simpler. The server pushes data to the client over a persistent HTTP connection, but only in one direction (server to client). The browser API handles reconnection automatically. SSE is perfect when you only need server-to-client updates: live feeds, notifications, stock tickers. It is much simpler to implement and scale than WebSockets.\n\nLong Polling is the simplest approach. The client sends a request, and the server holds it open until there is data to send (or a timeout). The client immediately sends another request after receiving a response. It works everywhere and requires no special infrastructure, but it is inefficient — each message costs a full HTTP round trip, and the server must hold many open connections.\n\nThe decision tree is straightforward: if you need bidirectional real-time communication, use WebSockets. If you only need server-to-client updates, use SSE. If you need broad compatibility with minimal infrastructure, use Long Polling.` },
      { type: 'text', heading: 'CDN: Bringing Content Closer to Users', body: `A CDN (Content Delivery Network) is a geographically distributed network of servers that caches content close to users. Without a CDN, every request for an image travels all the way to your origin server. If your server is in Virginia and the user is in India, that adds 250-300ms of latency per request. With a CDN, the same image is served from a nearby edge server in 20-40ms.\n\nHere is how it works: a user requests an image from your app. The request goes to the nearest CDN edge server. If the image is cached there, it is returned immediately (cache hit). If not, the CDN fetches it from your origin server, stores a copy, and returns it. Future users in that region get the image instantly.\n\nModern CDNs like Cloudflare, CloudFront, and Fastly can cache more than just static files — they can also cache API responses and even run edge logic. But the most common and most impactful use is still media delivery: images, videos, JavaScript, CSS, and fonts.\n\nIn system design interviews, introduce a CDN whenever your system serves static media at scale. It is almost always the right answer for images and videos. For dynamic content, caching at the CDN layer is trickier (you need short TTLs and careful invalidation), so mention it only if the problem specifically calls for it.` },
      { type: 'callout', variant: 'tip', heading: 'Latency Numbers to Memorize', body: 'DNS lookup: ~10ms. TCP handshake: ~1 round trip. TLS handshake: ~1-2 round trips. Same datacenter: 0.5ms. Cross-region: 50-100ms. Cross-continent: 150-300ms. Redis read: ~1ms. Database read: ~30-50ms. These numbers help you reason about where latency comes from and whether caching or CDN will help.' },
      { type: 'text', heading: 'HTTPS and TLS: Encryption Is Not Optional', body: `Without HTTPS, anyone on the network can read your data, modify it in transit, or impersonate your server. Every production system must use HTTPS.\n\nHTTPS is simply HTTP wrapped in TLS encryption. When a client connects, a TLS handshake happens after the TCP handshake. During this handshake, the server proves its identity with a certificate, and both sides agree on encryption keys. TLS 1.2 requires two round trips for this, while TLS 1.3 cuts it to one (and can even do zero round trips for returning connections). For a server 100ms away, TLS 1.2 adds 200ms of overhead. TLS 1.3 halves that.\n\nIn most architectures, TLS is terminated at the load balancer. This means the load balancer handles the encryption and decryption, and traffic between the load balancer and your backend servers is unencrypted (but within your private network). This simplifies backend code and reduces CPU usage on application servers. If you need true end-to-end encryption (for compliance reasons, for example), you can terminate TLS at the application level instead, but it costs more CPU.` },
      { type: 'text', heading: 'HTTP Methods and Status Codes', body: `HTTP methods tell the server what action you want to perform. GET retrieves data. POST creates something new. PUT replaces a resource entirely. PATCH updates part of a resource. DELETE removes it. In system design, you will define API endpoints using these methods, so knowing the conventions matters.\n\nStatus codes tell the client what happened. The 2xx range means success: 200 OK (here is your data), 201 Created (the new resource was created), 204 No Content (done, nothing to return). The 3xx range means redirection: 301 Moved Permanently, 302 Found (temporary redirect). The 4xx range means the client made an error: 400 Bad Request (malformed input), 401 Unauthorized (not logged in), 403 Forbidden (logged in but not allowed), 404 Not Found, 429 Too Many Requests (rate limited). The 5xx range means the server failed: 500 Internal Server Error, 502 Bad Gateway (upstream server is broken), 503 Service Unavailable (overloaded or in maintenance).\n\nA common interview detail: for URL shorteners, use 302 (temporary redirect) instead of 301 (permanent). With 301, browsers cache the redirect and never call your server again, which means you lose all analytics tracking.` },
      { type: 'text', heading: 'Connection Pooling: Stop Rebuilding the Road Every Trip', body: `Remember all those steps to establish a connection? DNS lookup, TCP handshake, TLS handshake. For a server 50ms away, that is 150-200ms before any actual data flows. Now imagine doing that for every single request. User clicks a button? 200ms of handshaking. Loads an image? Another 200ms. Your app feels sluggish even though your server responds in 5ms.\n\nThe solution is connection reuse. HTTP/1.1 introduced keep-alive connections: instead of closing the connection after each request, the browser keeps it open and sends multiple requests over the same connection. One handshake, many requests.\n\nFor server-to-server communication, connection pooling takes this further. Instead of opening a new connection for each request to your database or another service, you maintain a pool of pre-established connections. When your code needs a connection, it borrows one from the pool. When it is done, it returns it. This eliminates the handshake overhead entirely for most requests.\n\nWatch out for connection leaks: if your code borrows a connection but forgets to return it (because of an exception, for example), the pool slowly drains until nothing works. Always use try/finally patterns to ensure connections are returned.\n\nAlso be aware of server-side connection limits. PostgreSQL defaults to 100 max connections. MySQL defaults to 151. If your connection pool is too large, or you have too many application servers each with their own pool, you can exhaust the database connection limit. This is a surprisingly common cause of production outages.` },
      { type: 'text', heading: 'Timeouts: Every Network Call Needs One', body: `Without a timeout, a stuck dependency blocks your service forever. A database that hangs, an external API that never responds, a DNS server that is unreachable — without timeouts, your application threads pile up waiting, and eventually your entire service becomes unresponsive.\n\nReasonable timeout values depend on the call: database queries should timeout in 5-30 seconds, internal API calls in 1-5 seconds, external API calls in 5-10 seconds, and user-facing requests should have a total budget of about 30 seconds.\n\nCascading timeouts are critical to get right. If Service A calls Service B, which calls Service C, then A\'s timeout to B must be longer than B\'s timeout to C. Otherwise, A gives up before B even finishes its work. A common pattern is to set each downstream timeout shorter than the upstream one, leaving room for retries.\n\nIn system design interviews, mentioning timeouts shows operational maturity. When you draw a connection between two services, the interviewer appreciates hearing "and this call has a 3-second timeout with one retry."` },
      { type: 'text', heading: 'Retries, Backoff, and Idempotency', body: `When a network call fails, the natural instinct is to try again. Retries are indeed one of the most effective strategies for handling transient failures — a server that was momentarily overloaded, a network blip, a brief DNS hiccup. But naive retries can make things worse.\n\nImagine a server that is struggling under load. If every client immediately retries its failed request, the server now has twice the traffic. The retries fail too, so clients retry again. The server is drowning. This is why retries should always use exponential backoff: wait 1 second, then 2, then 4, then 8. Adding random jitter (a small random delay) prevents all clients from retrying at exactly the same time, which would create a synchronized thundering herd.\n\nBut retries introduce another problem: what if the original request actually succeeded, but the response was lost? If you are retrying a payment request, you might charge the customer twice. This is where idempotency comes in. An idempotent operation produces the same result no matter how many times you execute it. GET requests are naturally idempotent. For non-idempotent operations like payments, the client generates a unique idempotency key for each logical operation. The server checks whether it has already processed a request with that key, and if so, returns the cached result instead of processing it again.\n\nIn interviews, the magic phrase is "retry with exponential backoff and jitter." For senior-level discussions, mentioning idempotency keys for write operations shows real-world experience.` },
      { type: 'text', heading: 'Circuit Breakers: Preventing Cascading Failures', body: `Sometimes a dependency does not just have a transient failure — it is genuinely down and will stay down for a while. If your service keeps retrying requests to a dead database, those retries consume threads, connections, and time. Meanwhile, your own service becomes slow and unresponsive, which causes the services that depend on you to also become slow. This is a cascading failure, and it is one of the most dangerous failure modes in distributed systems.\n\nA circuit breaker is a pattern inspired by electrical circuit breakers. It monitors the failure rate of calls to a dependency. When failures exceed a threshold (say, 50% of requests in the last 30 seconds), the circuit "trips" to an open state. While open, all requests to that dependency immediately fail without even attempting the call. This is called "failing fast" — it is much better to return an error in 1ms than to wait 30 seconds for a timeout.\n\nAfter a cooldown period, the circuit moves to a "half-open" state and allows a single test request through. If it succeeds, the circuit closes and normal traffic resumes. If it fails, the circuit stays open for another cooldown period.\n\nCircuit breakers are especially valuable when an interviewer asks "what happens when this service goes down?" Being able to describe the circuit breaker pattern and where you would apply it — database connections, external API calls, service-to-service communication — demonstrates the kind of operational thinking that comes from real production experience.` },
      { type: 'animation', id: 'circuit-breaker', heading: 'Circuit Breaker: Interactive Demo', body: 'Click to simulate failures and see how the circuit breaker transitions between Closed → Open → Half-Open states.' },
      { type: 'text', heading: 'Load Balancing Basics', body: `When you have multiple servers, you need something to decide which server handles each request. That is a load balancer. In system design, load balancers appear in almost every architecture diagram, sitting between clients and your application servers.\n\nThere are two types that matter for interviews. Layer 4 (L4) load balancers operate at the TCP level. They look at IP addresses and ports but do not inspect the actual content of requests. They are fast and efficient, and they maintain the TCP connection between client and server. This makes them ideal for WebSocket connections and other protocols that need persistent connections.\n\nLayer 7 (L7) load balancers operate at the HTTP level. They can inspect request content — URLs, headers, cookies — and make smarter routing decisions. For example, an L7 load balancer can route /api requests to API servers and /static requests to a CDN. The trade-off is that L7 load balancers terminate the client connection and create a new one to the backend, which adds a small amount of overhead.\n\nFor most HTTP-based systems, use an L7 load balancer. For WebSocket connections, use an L4 load balancer (or an L7 that explicitly supports WebSocket upgrades).\n\nCommon load balancing algorithms include round-robin (requests go to servers in rotation), least connections (send to the server with fewest active connections — good for WebSockets), and IP hash (same client always goes to the same server — useful for session affinity).\n\nLoad balancers also perform health checks, periodically pinging backend servers to verify they are alive. If a server fails its health check, the load balancer stops sending traffic to it until it recovers. This automatic failover is what makes load balancers essential for high availability.` },
      { type: 'animation', id: 'load-balancer-types', heading: 'L4 vs L7 Load Balancers', body: 'Toggle between Layer 4 and Layer 7 to see how they route traffic differently.' },
      { type: 'diagram', variant: 'lb-algorithms', heading: 'Load Balancing Algorithms', caption: 'Five common algorithms — each suited for different workloads.' },
      { type: 'text', heading: 'Vertical vs Horizontal Scaling', body: `When your system needs to handle more traffic, you have two fundamental options.\n\nVertical scaling (scaling up) means getting a bigger machine — more CPU, more RAM, faster disks. It is simple because your code does not change. There is no distributed system complexity, no data consistency issues, no need for load balancers. The downside is that hardware has limits. The biggest server money can buy still has a ceiling, and it is a single point of failure. If it goes down, everything goes down.\n\nHorizontal scaling (scaling out) means adding more machines and distributing the work across them. This is theoretically unlimited — you can always add another server. It also gives you high availability because if one server dies, the others keep running. The downside is complexity: you need load balancers to distribute traffic, you need to handle data consistency across machines, and your application must be designed to be stateless (or use shared state stores like Redis).\n\nIn practice, most systems use both. You vertically scale each individual server to a reasonable size (modern cloud instances are very powerful), and then horizontally scale by adding more of them behind a load balancer. The interview answer is almost always horizontal scaling, because interviewers want to see that you can design distributed systems. But mentioning that you would start with vertical scaling for simplicity and switch to horizontal when needed shows pragmatism.` },
      { type: 'diagram', variant: 'scaling-types', heading: 'Vertical vs Horizontal Scaling', caption: 'One big machine vs many small machines — most production systems use both.' },
      { type: 'text', heading: 'How a Packet Actually Travels', body: `Understanding the full journey of a packet helps you reason about where things can go wrong. When your browser sends a request to a server across the world, here is what happens at each layer:\n\nYour application creates an HTTP request (Layer 7). The operating system wraps it in a TCP segment with source and destination ports (Layer 4). Then it wraps that in an IP packet with source and destination IP addresses (Layer 3). Finally, it wraps that in an Ethernet frame with MAC addresses for the next hop (Layer 2) and sends it as electrical signals over your network cable or WiFi (Layer 1).\n\nThe packet hits your home router, which uses NAT to replace your private IP with its public IP. The router then forwards the packet to your ISP. From there, the packet hops through multiple routers on the internet backbone — each router looks at the destination IP and forwards the packet one hop closer to its destination. This is like a relay race where each runner passes the baton to the next.\n\nWhen the packet arrives at the destination network, it goes through the reverse process: the load balancer receives it, unwraps the layers, reads the HTTP request, and forwards it to the appropriate backend server. The server processes the request and sends a response back through the same chain in reverse.\n\nEach hop adds a small amount of latency. A packet from New York to Mumbai might pass through 15-20 routers, each adding a fraction of a millisecond. Combined with the speed-of-light delay over thousands of kilometers of fiber, this is why cross-continent requests take 150-300ms.` },
      { type: 'text', heading: 'Network Security Essentials', body: `Security is not optional in modern systems. Here are the networking security concepts that come up in system design interviews.\n\nFirewalls filter traffic based on rules. In cloud environments, security groups act as virtual firewalls — you define which ports and IP ranges can access each server. A common setup: your load balancer accepts traffic on ports 80 (HTTP) and 443 (HTTPS) from anywhere, your app servers accept traffic only from the load balancer, and your database accepts traffic only from app servers. This layered approach means even if an attacker compromises your load balancer, they cannot directly access your database.\n\nDDoS (Distributed Denial of Service) attacks flood your servers with so much traffic that legitimate users cannot get through. Defenses include rate limiting at the load balancer or API gateway, using a CDN like Cloudflare that absorbs attack traffic at the edge, and auto-scaling to handle traffic spikes (though this can get expensive).\n\nSSL/TLS certificates prove your server is who it claims to be. Without them, an attacker could set up a fake server and intercept traffic (a man-in-the-middle attack). Certificate management is a real operational concern — expired certificates cause outages. Use automated renewal services like Let\'s Encrypt or AWS Certificate Manager.\n\nIn interviews, you do not need to go deep on security unless asked. But mentioning "HTTPS everywhere, database in a private subnet, security groups restricting access" shows you think about security as part of the design, not as an afterthought.` },
      { type: 'text', heading: 'Reverse Proxy and API Gateway', body: `A reverse proxy is a server that sits in front of your backend servers and forwards client requests to them. From the client's perspective, it looks like they are talking directly to the backend — they have no idea a proxy is involved. This is different from a forward proxy (like a VPN), which sits in front of clients.\n\nReverse proxies serve several purposes in system design. They can distribute load across multiple servers (acting as a load balancer), cache responses to reduce backend load, terminate SSL/TLS so backends do not need to handle encryption, compress responses to save bandwidth, and protect backend servers by hiding their IP addresses from the internet.\n\nAn API Gateway is a specialized reverse proxy designed specifically for APIs. In addition to everything a reverse proxy does, an API Gateway handles authentication and authorization (verifying API keys or JWT tokens), rate limiting (preventing abuse), request transformation (converting between protocols or formats), routing (directing requests to the correct microservice based on the URL path), and analytics (logging and monitoring API usage).\n\nIn system design interviews, you will often draw a load balancer or API gateway at the entry point of your system. Knowing the difference matters: a load balancer distributes traffic, a reverse proxy adds features like caching and SSL termination, and an API gateway adds API-specific features like auth and rate limiting. In practice, tools like Nginx and Kong can serve all three roles.` },
      { type: 'text', heading: 'Rate Limiting', body: `Rate limiting controls how many requests a client can make to your API within a given time window. It protects your system from abuse, prevents denial-of-service attacks, and ensures fair usage across all clients.\n\nThe most common approach is to track requests per client (identified by API key, user ID, or IP address) and reject requests that exceed the limit with a 429 Too Many Requests status code. The response typically includes headers telling the client how many requests they have remaining and when the limit resets.\n\nThere are several algorithms for implementing rate limiting. The token bucket algorithm is the most popular — imagine a bucket that holds N tokens and refills at a steady rate. Each request consumes one token. If the bucket is empty, the request is rejected. This naturally allows short bursts (up to the bucket size) while enforcing an average rate.\n\nIn distributed systems, rate limiting counters need to be shared across all your servers. Redis is the standard choice for this — its atomic INCR command with EXPIRE provides a simple and fast way to track request counts per client per time window.\n\nRate limiting is typically implemented at the API Gateway or load balancer level, not in your application code. This centralizes the logic and applies it consistently across all endpoints.` },
      { type: 'text', heading: 'Throughput vs Latency', body: `Throughput and latency are both measures of system performance, but they measure different things and optimizing for one does not necessarily improve the other.\n\nLatency is the time it takes for a single request to complete — from the moment the client sends it to the moment the response arrives. It is measured in milliseconds. Low latency means fast individual responses.\n\nThroughput is the total number of requests your system can handle per unit of time — typically measured in requests per second (RPS) or queries per second (QPS). High throughput means your system can serve many users simultaneously.\n\nA system can have low latency but low throughput (a single fast server that can only handle one request at a time), or high throughput but high latency (a batch processing system that handles millions of records but takes minutes per batch). The goal in system design is usually to optimize both — fast individual responses AND the ability to handle many concurrent users.\n\nWhen an interviewer asks about performance, clarify whether they mean latency (how fast) or throughput (how many). The solutions are often different: caching and CDNs reduce latency, while horizontal scaling and load balancing increase throughput.` },
      { type: 'text', heading: 'Network Congestion', body: `Network congestion occurs when the volume of data traffic exceeds the network's capacity to handle it. Think of it like a traffic jam on a highway — when too many cars try to use the same road at the same time, everyone slows down.\n\nCongestion manifests as increased latency, packet loss (the network drops packets it cannot handle), and connection timeouts. In severe cases, it can cause a congestive collapse where the network becomes essentially unusable.\n\nTCP has built-in congestion control mechanisms. When TCP detects packet loss (which it interprets as a sign of congestion), it reduces its sending rate. It then gradually increases the rate again until it finds the maximum sustainable throughput. This is called the TCP congestion window and it uses algorithms like slow start and congestion avoidance.\n\nIn system design, congestion is relevant when you are designing systems that generate a lot of network traffic — video streaming, large file transfers, or services with millions of concurrent connections. Solutions include using CDNs to distribute traffic geographically, implementing backpressure mechanisms (where downstream services signal upstream services to slow down), and using message queues to buffer traffic spikes instead of letting them hit your servers directly.` },
      {
        type: 'quiz',
        heading: 'Test Your Knowledge: Networking',
        description: 'See how well you understood the networking concepts above.',
        questions: [
          {
            question: 'What does DNS do?',
            options: ['Encrypts HTTP traffic', 'Translates domain names to IP addresses', 'Compresses HTTP headers', 'Manages TCP connections'],
            correct: 1,
            explanation: 'DNS translates human-readable domain names like google.com into IP addresses like 142.250.80.46 that computers use to find each other.',
          },
          {
            question: 'Which protocol would you choose for a real-time chat application?',
            options: ['HTTP Long Polling', 'Server-Sent Events (SSE)', 'WebSockets', 'UDP'],
            correct: 2,
            explanation: 'WebSockets provide full-duplex, bidirectional communication over a persistent connection — ideal for chat where both client and server need to send messages at any time.',
          },
          {
            question: 'What is the main advantage of HTTP/2 over HTTP/1.1?',
            options: ['Uses UDP instead of TCP', 'Multiplexing — multiple requests over one connection', 'End-to-end encryption', 'Smaller response bodies'],
            correct: 1,
            explanation: 'HTTP/2 multiplexes multiple requests over a single TCP connection, eliminating the head-of-line blocking problem that limited HTTP/1.1 to one request at a time.',
          },
          {
            question: 'A user in India experiences 300ms latency to your Virginia server. What is the best solution?',
            options: ['Increase server bandwidth', 'Add a CDN or deploy a server closer to India', 'Switch from TCP to UDP', 'Use HTTP/3'],
            correct: 1,
            explanation: 'The 300ms latency is caused by physical distance. No amount of bandwidth fixes this. You need to put content or servers closer to the user — either via CDN for static content or a regional deployment for dynamic content.',
          },
          {
            question: 'Why does TCP require a three-way handshake before sending data?',
            options: ['To encrypt the connection', 'To compress the data', 'To ensure both sides are ready and can communicate reliably', 'To resolve the domain name'],
            correct: 2,
            explanation: 'The three-way handshake (SYN, SYN-ACK, ACK) ensures both the client and server are ready to communicate and establishes the parameters for a reliable, ordered connection.',
          },
          {
            question: 'What is the difference between a reverse proxy and a load balancer?',
            options: ['They are the same thing', 'A reverse proxy hides backend servers and adds features like caching/SSL; a load balancer distributes traffic', 'A load balancer encrypts traffic; a reverse proxy does not', 'A reverse proxy is client-side; a load balancer is server-side'],
            correct: 1,
            explanation: 'A reverse proxy sits in front of backends and can cache, terminate SSL, and compress responses. A load balancer specifically distributes traffic across servers. In practice, tools like Nginx do both.',
          },
          {
            question: 'A client sends 150 requests in one minute but the rate limit is 100/min. What HTTP status code should the server return?',
            options: ['403 Forbidden', '429 Too Many Requests', '503 Service Unavailable', '408 Request Timeout'],
            correct: 1,
            explanation: '429 Too Many Requests is the standard status code for rate limiting. It tells the client they have exceeded the allowed request rate and should slow down.',
          },
          {
            question: 'Which load balancing algorithm is best for WebSocket connections?',
            options: ['Round Robin', 'Least Connections', 'Random', 'IP Hash'],
            correct: 1,
            explanation: 'Least Connections routes new requests to the server with fewest active connections. Since WebSocket connections are long-lived, this prevents one server from accumulating all connections over time.',
          },
          {
            question: 'Your service calls a database that is down. After 10 failed requests, all subsequent calls immediately return an error without even trying the database. What pattern is this?',
            options: ['Rate Limiting', 'Circuit Breaker', 'Retry with Backoff', 'Load Balancing'],
            correct: 1,
            explanation: 'A circuit breaker monitors failure rates and "trips open" after a threshold, causing all subsequent calls to fail fast without attempting the actual call. This prevents cascading failures.',
          },
          {
            question: 'What is the key difference between latency and throughput?',
            options: ['They are the same metric measured differently', 'Latency is time per request; throughput is requests per second', 'Latency is for reads; throughput is for writes', 'Throughput is always higher than latency'],
            correct: 1,
            explanation: 'Latency measures how long a single request takes (milliseconds). Throughput measures how many requests the system handles per unit time (requests/second). Optimizing one does not necessarily improve the other.',
          },
          {
            question: 'Why should databases never have public IP addresses?',
            options: ['Public IPs are too expensive', 'Databases cannot use public IPs', 'It exposes the database directly to the internet, creating a security risk', 'Public IPs cause higher latency'],
            correct: 2,
            explanation: 'Databases should sit in a private subnet with no internet access. Only load balancers need public IPs. Traffic flows: Internet → Load Balancer (public) → App Servers (private) → Database (private).',
          },
          {
            question: 'What happens when a popular cache key expires and thousands of requests simultaneously hit the database?',
            options: ['Rate limiting', 'Cache stampede (thundering herd)', 'Circuit breaker trip', 'DNS resolution failure'],
            correct: 1,
            explanation: 'A cache stampede (or thundering herd) occurs when a hot cache key expires and many requests simultaneously miss the cache and flood the database. Solutions include request coalescing and probabilistic early expiration.',
          },
        ],
      },
    ],
  },

  'api-design': {
    slug: 'api-design',
    title: 'API Design',
    subtitle: 'REST, GraphQL, gRPC — designing clean, scalable APIs',
    duration: '35 min', difficulty: 'Intermediate',
    sections: [
      { type: 'text', heading: 'Why API Design Matters', body: `An API (Application Programming Interface) is the contract between your system and the outside world. Every time a mobile app loads your feed, a partner integrates with your platform, or a microservice talks to another, they are calling your API. A poorly designed API creates confusion, bugs, and breaking changes that ripple across every client. A well-designed API is intuitive, predictable, and ages gracefully.\n\nIn system design interviews, API design shows up in two ways. First, the interviewer may ask you to sketch out the API for the system you are designing — what endpoints exist, what data they accept, what they return. Second, you may get a dedicated API design question where the entire interview revolves around designing a clean, scalable interface for a service like Twitter, Stripe, or a URL shortener.\n\nEither way, the interviewer is looking for the same things: do you think in terms of resources? Do you understand idempotency? Can you handle pagination, versioning, and authentication without being told? Let us walk through all of it.` },

      { type: 'text', heading: 'REST — The Default Choice', body: `REST (Representational State Transfer) is the most widely used API paradigm on the web. It is not a protocol or a standard — it is an architectural style built on top of HTTP. When someone says "REST API," they mean an API that uses HTTP methods on URL-addressable resources and returns structured data (usually JSON).\n\nThe core idea is simple: everything is a **resource**. A user is a resource. An order is a resource. A comment on a post is a resource. Each resource has a URL (its address), and you interact with it using standard HTTP methods. The server does not remember anything about previous requests — every request is self-contained. This is what "stateless" means, and it is what makes REST APIs easy to scale horizontally behind load balancers.\n\nREST became dominant because it maps naturally to how the web already works. Browsers make HTTP requests. Servers return responses with status codes. Caches understand HTTP headers. You are not fighting the platform — you are working with it.` },

      { type: 'text', heading: 'Resource Modeling — Think in Nouns', body: `The first step in designing a REST API is identifying your resources. Resources are the nouns of your system — the things your users care about. For an e-commerce platform, your resources might be users, products, orders, and reviews. For a social network: users, posts, comments, likes, followers.\n\nEach resource gets a URL path using plural nouns:\n\n\`\`\`\n/users          → collection of users\n/users/42       → a specific user\n/users/42/posts → posts belonging to user 42\n/posts/99       → a specific post\n\`\`\`\n\nNotice the pattern: collections are plural (/users, /posts), individual items are identified by ID (/users/42), and sub-resources are nested to show ownership (/users/42/posts). This nesting should be shallow — going more than two levels deep (/users/42/posts/99/comments/5/likes) makes URLs unwieldy. If a resource can stand on its own, give it a top-level path. Comments can be accessed as /comments/5 even if they belong to a post.\n\nA common mistake is using verbs in URLs: /getUser, /createOrder, /deletePost. This defeats the purpose of REST. The HTTP method already tells you the action. The URL should only identify the resource.` },

      { type: 'text', heading: 'HTTP Methods and Idempotency', body: `HTTP gives you a small set of methods (verbs) that map to CRUD operations. The beauty of this is that every developer already knows what GET, POST, PUT, and DELETE mean — your API is self-documenting.\n\n**GET** — Read a resource. GET /users/42 returns user 42. GET /users returns a list. GET requests must be safe (no side effects) and idempotent (calling it 10 times gives the same result as calling it once). Never use GET to modify data.\n\n**POST** — Create a new resource. POST /users with a JSON body creates a new user. The server assigns the ID and returns it. POST is neither safe nor idempotent — calling it twice creates two users.\n\n**PUT** — Replace a resource entirely. PUT /users/42 with a complete user object replaces everything about user 42. PUT is idempotent — sending the same PUT request twice results in the same state. If the resource does not exist, PUT can create it (if your API supports that).\n\n**PATCH** — Partially update a resource. PATCH /users/42 with { "name": "Alice" } updates only the name, leaving everything else unchanged. PATCH is technically not guaranteed to be idempotent, but in practice most implementations are.\n\n**DELETE** — Remove a resource. DELETE /users/42 deletes user 42. DELETE is idempotent — deleting something that is already gone should return success (or 404), not an error.` },

      { type: 'callout', variant: 'tip', heading: 'Why Idempotency Matters', body: `Networks are unreliable. A client sends a POST /orders request, the server processes it, but the response gets lost. The client retries. Without idempotency handling, you now have two orders. This is why payment APIs like Stripe require an idempotency key — a unique token the client sends with each request. If the server sees the same key twice, it returns the cached response instead of processing again. In interviews, mentioning idempotency keys for write operations shows awareness of distributed systems.` },

      { type: 'animation', id: 'rest-request-flow', heading: 'REST Request Lifecycle', body: 'A typical REST request flows from client through authentication, routing, business logic, and back.' },

      { type: 'text', heading: 'Passing Data — Path, Query, Headers, Body', body: `There are four places to put data in an HTTP request, and each has a specific purpose. Using the wrong one is a common design mistake.\n\n**Path parameters** identify a specific resource. They are part of the URL itself: /users/42, /posts/99. Use path params for required identifiers that pinpoint which resource you are talking about.\n\n**Query parameters** filter, sort, or paginate collections. They come after the ? in the URL: /users?role=admin&sort=created_at&page=2. Query params are optional and do not change which resource you are addressing — they change how the collection is returned.\n\n**Request headers** carry metadata about the request itself: authentication tokens (Authorization: Bearer xxx), content type (Content-Type: application/json), caching directives, API version, request tracing IDs. Headers are not part of the business data.\n\n**Request body** carries the actual data payload for POST, PUT, and PATCH requests. This is where you send the JSON object representing the resource you are creating or updating. GET and DELETE requests should not have a body (some servers ignore it, others reject it).\n\nHere is a concrete example. To update a user's email:\n\n\`\`\`\nPATCH /users/42\nAuthorization: Bearer eyJhbGciOiJIUzI1NiJ9...\nContent-Type: application/json\n\n{\n  "email": "alice@example.com"\n}\n\`\`\`\n\nPath param (42) identifies the user. Header carries the auth token. Body carries the update payload. Clean separation.` },

      { type: 'text', heading: 'HTTP Status Codes — Speak the Language', body: `Status codes are how your API communicates outcomes. Using them correctly means clients can handle responses programmatically without parsing error messages.\n\n**2xx — Success**\n200 OK — Request succeeded. Used for GET, PUT, PATCH, DELETE.\n201 Created — Resource created. Used for POST. Include a Location header pointing to the new resource.\n204 No Content — Success, but nothing to return. Common for DELETE.\n\n**3xx — Redirection**\n301 Moved Permanently — Resource has a new URL. Clients should update their bookmarks.\n304 Not Modified — Used with caching. The resource has not changed since the client last fetched it.\n\n**4xx — Client Error**\n400 Bad Request — Malformed request. Missing required fields, invalid JSON, wrong data types.\n401 Unauthorized — Authentication required or token is invalid. (Confusingly named — it really means "unauthenticated.")\n403 Forbidden — Authenticated but not authorized. You know who they are, but they do not have permission.\n404 Not Found — Resource does not exist.\n409 Conflict — Request conflicts with current state. Common for duplicate creation attempts.\n429 Too Many Requests — Rate limit exceeded. Include a Retry-After header.\n\n**5xx — Server Error**\n500 Internal Server Error — Something broke on the server. The client did nothing wrong.\n502 Bad Gateway — The server, acting as a proxy, got a bad response from upstream.\n503 Service Unavailable — Server is overloaded or down for maintenance.\n\nA good rule of thumb: if the client made a mistake, return 4xx. If the server made a mistake, return 5xx. Never return 200 with an error message in the body — that breaks every HTTP client library's error handling.` },

      { type: 'diagram', variant: 'status-code-tree', heading: 'HTTP Status Code Decision Tree', caption: 'How to pick the right status code for your API response' },

      { type: 'text', heading: 'GraphQL — Ask for Exactly What You Need', body: `GraphQL was created at Facebook in 2012 to solve a specific problem: their mobile app needed different data than their web app, and both were calling the same REST endpoints. The mobile app was fetching user profiles that included 30 fields when it only needed 5 (over-fetching). The web app needed a user's posts, comments, and friend count in one view but had to make three separate REST calls to get them (under-fetching).\n\nGraphQL flips the model. Instead of the server deciding what data to return, the client sends a query describing exactly what it wants:\n\n\`\`\`\nquery {\n  user(id: "42") {\n    name\n    avatar\n    posts(limit: 5) {\n      title\n      likeCount\n    }\n  }\n}\n\`\`\`\n\nOne request, one response, exactly the shape the client asked for. No extra fields, no extra round trips. The server exposes a typed schema that describes every type, field, and relationship in the system. Clients can introspect this schema to discover what is available — it is self-documenting.\n\nThis sounds perfect, but GraphQL introduces its own complexity. Caching is harder because every query is unique (unlike REST where /users/42 is always the same URL). The N+1 problem is real — a query that asks for 50 users and each user's posts can trigger 51 database queries (1 for users + 50 for posts) unless you use a DataLoader pattern to batch them. And for simple CRUD APIs, GraphQL is overkill — you are adding a query language, a schema, and a resolver layer when REST would have been fine.` },

      { type: 'callout', variant: 'info', heading: 'The N+1 Problem in GraphQL', body: `Imagine a query that fetches 50 users and each user's company. A naive resolver fetches all 50 users (1 query), then for each user, fetches their company (50 queries) = 51 total. The fix is batching: collect all company IDs from the 50 users, then fetch all companies in a single query (2 queries total). Facebook's DataLoader library does this automatically. In interviews, mentioning the N+1 problem and DataLoader shows you understand GraphQL's trade-offs, not just its marketing pitch.` },

      { type: 'animation', id: 'graphql-vs-rest', heading: 'REST vs GraphQL — Data Fetching Comparison', body: 'REST requires multiple round trips for related data. GraphQL gets everything in one request.' },

      { type: 'text', heading: 'gRPC — When Performance is Everything', body: `gRPC (Google Remote Procedure Call) is a high-performance framework for service-to-service communication. While REST sends human-readable JSON over HTTP/1.1, gRPC sends binary-encoded Protocol Buffers over HTTP/2. The result is dramatically faster serialization, smaller payloads, and built-in streaming.\n\nYou define your service contract in a .proto file:\n\n\`\`\`\nservice UserService {\n  rpc GetUser (GetUserRequest) returns (User);\n  rpc ListUsers (ListUsersRequest) returns (stream User);\n}\n\nmessage GetUserRequest {\n  string user_id = 1;\n}\n\nmessage User {\n  string id = 1;\n  string name = 2;\n  string email = 3;\n}\n\`\`\`\n\nFrom this definition, gRPC auto-generates client and server code in any supported language (Go, Java, Python, C++, etc.). The client calls GetUser() like a local function — the framework handles serialization, network transport, and deserialization transparently. This is why it is called a "remote procedure call."\n\ngRPC supports four communication patterns: unary (one request, one response — like REST), server streaming (one request, stream of responses — like a live feed), client streaming (stream of requests, one response — like uploading chunks), and bidirectional streaming (both sides stream simultaneously — like a chat).\n\nThe trade-off is developer experience. Protocol Buffers are not human-readable — you cannot curl a gRPC endpoint and read the response. Browser support is limited (you need gRPC-Web as a proxy). Debugging requires specialized tools. For these reasons, gRPC is almost exclusively used for internal microservice communication where performance matters and both sides are controlled by the same team.` },

      { type: 'diagram', variant: 'api-paradigms-comparison', heading: 'REST vs GraphQL vs gRPC', caption: 'When to use each API paradigm' },

      { type: 'text', heading: 'Pagination — Never Return Everything', body: `Any endpoint that returns a list needs pagination. Returning all 10 million users in a single response is not an option — it would consume massive memory, take forever to serialize, and probably crash the client.\n\nThere are two main approaches, and they have very different trade-offs.\n\n**Offset-based pagination** is the simplest. The client sends a page number and page size: GET /users?page=3&limit=20. The server skips the first 40 rows (offset = (page - 1) * limit) and returns the next 20. This is intuitive and works well for small datasets. But it has a fundamental flaw: if a new user is inserted while someone is paginating, they might see duplicates or miss items. And for large offsets, the database has to scan and discard all the skipped rows — OFFSET 1000000 is slow even if you only want 20 rows.\n\n**Cursor-based pagination** solves both problems. Instead of a page number, the client sends a cursor — an opaque token that points to the last item they saw: GET /users?after=eyJpZCI6NDJ9&limit=20. The server decodes the cursor (usually a base64-encoded ID or timestamp), queries for items after that point, and returns the next page along with a new cursor. There is no offset to skip, so performance is consistent regardless of how deep you are in the list. And because the cursor is tied to a specific item, insertions and deletions do not cause duplicates or gaps.\n\nThe response should include pagination metadata so the client knows if there are more pages:\n\n\`\`\`\n{\n  "data": [...],\n  "pagination": {\n    "next_cursor": "eyJpZCI6NjJ9",\n    "has_more": true\n  }\n}\n\`\`\`\n\nUse offset pagination for admin dashboards and simple lists where the dataset is small and users jump to specific pages. Use cursor pagination for feeds, timelines, and any list that is large, frequently updated, or infinite-scrolling.` },

      { type: 'diagram', variant: 'pagination-types', heading: 'Offset vs Cursor Pagination', caption: 'How each pagination strategy handles data retrieval' },

      { type: 'text', heading: 'API Versioning — Evolving Without Breaking', body: `APIs evolve. You will need to rename fields, change response formats, deprecate endpoints, and add new features. The question is: how do you do this without breaking existing clients?\n\n**URL versioning** puts the version in the path: /v1/users, /v2/users. This is the most visible and widely used approach. It is easy to understand, easy to route, and easy to test. The downside is that it creates entirely separate URL namespaces — if only one endpoint changed between v1 and v2, you still need to maintain both complete versions.\n\n**Header versioning** uses a custom header: Accept: application/vnd.myapi.v2+json. The URL stays the same (/users), and the version is negotiated through content negotiation. This is more "RESTful" in theory, but harder to test (you cannot just paste a URL in a browser) and less discoverable.\n\n**Query parameter versioning** adds the version as a param: /users?version=2. Simple to implement, but pollutes the query string and can conflict with other parameters.\n\nIn practice, URL versioning wins for most teams. It is the easiest to understand, the easiest to document, and the easiest to deprecate. The key rule is: once you publish a version, never make breaking changes to it. Additive changes (new optional fields, new endpoints) are fine. Removing fields, renaming fields, or changing types are breaking changes that require a new version.\n\nA good deprecation strategy: announce the deprecation, set a sunset date, return a Deprecation header on old version responses, and give clients at least 6-12 months to migrate.` },

      { type: 'text', heading: 'Authentication and Authorization', body: `Authentication answers "who are you?" Authorization answers "what are you allowed to do?" Every API needs both.\n\n**API Keys** are the simplest form of authentication. The client includes a key in a header (X-API-Key: abc123) or query parameter. API keys identify the calling application, not the user. They are good for server-to-server communication, rate limiting per client, and usage tracking. They are bad for user-specific access control because they do not carry user identity.\n\n**JWT (JSON Web Tokens)** are the standard for user authentication in modern APIs. After a user logs in, the server creates a signed token containing the user's ID, roles, and an expiration time. The client sends this token in the Authorization header with every request: Authorization: Bearer eyJhbGciOiJIUzI1NiJ9...\n\nThe server verifies the signature without hitting a database — the token is self-contained. This is what makes JWTs great for distributed systems: any server can validate the token independently. The trade-off is that you cannot revoke a JWT before it expires (unless you maintain a blacklist, which defeats the "no database lookup" benefit). Keep JWT expiration short (15-60 minutes) and use refresh tokens for long-lived sessions.\n\n**OAuth 2.0** is the framework for delegated authorization — "let this app access my data without giving it my password." When you click "Sign in with Google," that is OAuth. The user authenticates with Google, Google gives your app a token, and your app uses that token to access the user's data. OAuth is complex (multiple grant types, redirect flows, token exchange), but it is the standard for third-party integrations.\n\n**RBAC (Role-Based Access Control)** is the most common authorization model. Users are assigned roles (admin, editor, viewer), and each role has a set of permissions. When a request comes in, the server checks: does this user's role have permission to perform this action on this resource? RBAC is simple to understand and implement, but can become unwieldy with many fine-grained permissions. For complex cases, consider ABAC (Attribute-Based Access Control) which evaluates policies based on user attributes, resource attributes, and environmental conditions.` },

      { type: 'diagram', variant: 'jwt-auth-flow', heading: 'JWT Authentication Flow', caption: 'How token-based authentication works in a typical API' },

      { type: 'text', heading: 'Rate Limiting — Protecting Your API', body: `Rate limiting controls how many requests a client can make in a given time window. Without it, a single misbehaving client (or an attacker) can overwhelm your servers and degrade the experience for everyone else.\n\nThere are four common algorithms, each with different characteristics:\n\n**Token Bucket** is the most widely used. Imagine a bucket that holds tokens. Each request consumes one token. Tokens are added at a fixed rate (say, 10 per second). If the bucket is empty, the request is rejected. If the bucket is full, excess tokens are discarded. The bucket has a maximum capacity, which allows short bursts — a client that has been idle accumulates tokens and can briefly exceed the steady-state rate. This is how most production rate limiters work because it handles bursty traffic gracefully.\n\n**Leaky Bucket** processes requests at a fixed rate, like water leaking from a bucket at a constant drip. Incoming requests fill the bucket; if it overflows, they are rejected. Unlike token bucket, leaky bucket smooths out bursts entirely — requests are always processed at the same rate. Good for APIs that need predictable throughput.\n\n**Fixed Window** divides time into fixed intervals (e.g., 1-minute windows) and counts requests per window. Simple to implement, but has an edge case: a client can send 100 requests at 11:59:59 and another 100 at 12:00:01, effectively getting 200 requests in 2 seconds while the limit is 100 per minute.\n\n**Sliding Window** fixes the edge case by using a rolling time window. It combines the simplicity of fixed windows with the accuracy of tracking individual request timestamps. Most production systems use this or token bucket.\n\nIn a distributed system, rate limit counters need to be shared across all servers. Redis is the standard choice — its atomic INCR and EXPIRE commands make it perfect for this. Include rate limit headers in your responses so clients know their limits:\n\n\`\`\`\nX-RateLimit-Limit: 100\nX-RateLimit-Remaining: 73\nX-RateLimit-Reset: 1625097600\nRetry-After: 30\n\`\`\`` },

      { type: 'text', heading: 'API Security Best Practices', body: `Security is not a feature you add later — it is baked into every design decision. Here are the practices that matter most for API design:\n\n**Always use HTTPS.** There is no excuse for serving an API over plain HTTP. TLS encrypts data in transit, preventing eavesdropping and man-in-the-middle attacks. In interviews, this is table stakes — mention it early and move on.\n\n**Validate all input.** Never trust data from the client. Validate types, lengths, ranges, and formats on every request. A missing validation on a single field is how SQL injection and XSS attacks happen. Use a schema validation library (Joi, Zod, Pydantic) rather than writing validation logic by hand.\n\n**Use parameterized queries.** Never concatenate user input into SQL strings. This is the number one cause of SQL injection. Every modern ORM and database driver supports parameterized queries — use them.\n\n**Implement CORS properly.** Cross-Origin Resource Sharing controls which domains can call your API from a browser. Do not set Access-Control-Allow-Origin: * on authenticated endpoints. Whitelist specific origins.\n\n**Return minimal error information.** In production, never expose stack traces, database errors, or internal paths in API responses. Return a generic error message and a unique error ID that your team can use to look up the details in logs.\n\n**Use short-lived tokens.** JWTs should expire in 15-60 minutes. Use refresh tokens (stored securely, rotated on use) for long-lived sessions. This limits the damage if a token is compromised.\n\n**Log everything, expose nothing.** Log every request with its authentication context, IP, and timestamp. This is essential for debugging, auditing, and incident response. But never log sensitive data like passwords, tokens, or credit card numbers.` },

      { type: 'callout', variant: 'example', heading: 'API Design in Interviews', body: `When an interviewer asks you to design an API, follow this structure:\n\n1. Identify the resources (nouns) in the system\n2. Define the endpoints using REST conventions (method + path)\n3. Specify request/response formats with concrete JSON examples\n4. Address pagination for list endpoints\n5. Discuss authentication and authorization\n6. Mention rate limiting and error handling\n7. Consider whether REST, GraphQL, or gRPC is the right fit\n\nDo not just list endpoints — explain your reasoning. "I chose cursor pagination here because the feed is frequently updated and offset pagination would cause duplicates" shows much more depth than "I will add pagination."` },

      { type: 'quiz', heading: 'API Design Quiz', description: 'Test your understanding of API design concepts', questions: [
        { question: 'Which HTTP method is idempotent?', options: ['POST', 'PUT', 'PATCH (in theory)', 'All of the above'], correct: 1, explanation: 'PUT is guaranteed idempotent — sending the same PUT request multiple times results in the same state. POST creates a new resource each time (not idempotent). PATCH is not guaranteed idempotent by the HTTP spec, though most implementations are.' },
        { question: 'A client sends POST /orders but the response is lost due to a network error. The client retries. What prevents a duplicate order?', options: ['HTTP status codes', 'Idempotency keys', 'Rate limiting', 'CORS headers'], correct: 1, explanation: 'An idempotency key is a unique token sent with the request. If the server sees the same key again, it returns the cached response instead of creating a duplicate. This is standard practice for payment and order APIs.' },
        { question: 'What is the main advantage of cursor-based pagination over offset-based?', options: ['Simpler to implement', 'Allows jumping to arbitrary pages', 'Consistent performance regardless of dataset size', 'Works without a database'], correct: 2, explanation: 'Cursor pagination uses a pointer to the last seen item, so the database does not need to scan and skip rows. Performance is O(1) regardless of how deep you are in the list. Offset pagination degrades as the offset grows because the DB must scan all skipped rows.' },
        { question: 'When should you choose gRPC over REST?', options: ['For public-facing web APIs', 'For internal microservice communication where performance matters', 'When you need browser support', 'For simple CRUD applications'], correct: 1, explanation: 'gRPC uses binary Protocol Buffers over HTTP/2, making it 5-10x faster than JSON/REST. It is ideal for internal service-to-service calls. However, it lacks browser support and is not human-readable, making it unsuitable for public APIs.' },
        { question: 'What problem does GraphQL solve that REST does not?', options: ['Authentication', 'Rate limiting', 'Over-fetching and under-fetching of data', 'Database indexing'], correct: 2, explanation: 'REST endpoints return fixed data shapes — the server decides what to include. GraphQL lets the client specify exactly which fields it needs, eliminating over-fetching (getting too much data) and under-fetching (needing multiple requests to get all required data).' },
        { question: 'What HTTP status code should you return when a user is authenticated but does not have permission to access a resource?', options: ['401 Unauthorized', '403 Forbidden', '404 Not Found', '400 Bad Request'], correct: 1, explanation: '403 Forbidden means the server knows who you are but you do not have permission. 401 Unauthorized (confusingly named) means the server does not know who you are — authentication is missing or invalid.' },
        { question: 'Which rate limiting algorithm allows short bursts of traffic above the steady-state rate?', options: ['Fixed Window', 'Leaky Bucket', 'Token Bucket', 'Sliding Window'], correct: 2, explanation: 'Token Bucket allows bursts because tokens accumulate when the client is idle. A client that has not made requests for a while has a full bucket and can temporarily exceed the per-second rate. Leaky Bucket, by contrast, enforces a strict constant rate.' },
        { question: 'Why should JWT expiration times be kept short (15-60 minutes)?', options: ['To reduce server memory usage', 'Because JWTs cannot be revoked before expiration', 'To improve network performance', 'Because browsers delete long-lived tokens'], correct: 1, explanation: 'JWTs are self-contained — the server does not track them. If a token is compromised, there is no way to invalidate it until it expires (unless you maintain a blacklist, which defeats the purpose). Short expiration limits the window of vulnerability.' },
      ]},
    ],
  },

  'data-modeling': {
    slug: 'data-modeling',
    title: 'Data Modeling',
    subtitle: 'SQL vs NoSQL, schema design, and choosing the right database',
    duration: '35 min', difficulty: 'Intermediate',
    sections: [
      { type: 'text', heading: 'Why Data Modeling Matters', body: `Every system design interview eventually comes down to one question: where does the data live and how is it structured? You can have the most elegant architecture in the world, but if your data model does not match your access patterns, the system will be slow, expensive, or both.\n\nData modeling is the process of deciding how to organize, store, and relate your data. It determines which queries are fast and which are slow. It determines whether your system can scale horizontally or is stuck on a single machine. It determines whether your writes are consistent or eventually consistent.\n\nThe biggest mistake candidates make is jumping straight to "I will use MongoDB" or "I will use PostgreSQL" without explaining why. The interviewer wants to see your reasoning — what are the access patterns? What are the consistency requirements? What is the read/write ratio? The database choice should follow naturally from those answers.` },

      { type: 'text', heading: 'Relational Databases (SQL)', body: `Relational databases store data in tables with rows and columns. Every row has the same structure (schema). Tables are linked through foreign keys, and you query them using SQL — a declarative language that has been around since the 1970s and is not going anywhere.\n\nThe defining feature of relational databases is ACID transactions:\n\n**Atomicity** — A transaction either completes entirely or not at all. If you transfer money from account A to account B, both the debit and credit happen, or neither does. There is no state where the money has left A but not arrived at B.\n\n**Consistency** — The database moves from one valid state to another. Constraints (unique keys, foreign keys, check constraints) are enforced. You cannot insert an order referencing a customer that does not exist.\n\n**Isolation** — Concurrent transactions do not interfere with each other. Two users buying the last item in stock will not both succeed — one will see the updated inventory.\n\n**Durability** — Once a transaction is committed, it survives crashes. The data is written to disk (WAL — write-ahead log) before the commit is acknowledged.\n\nRelational databases excel when your data has strong relationships, you need complex queries with JOINs, or you need transactional guarantees. Think financial systems, e-commerce orders, user account management, inventory tracking — anything where correctness matters more than raw speed.\n\nThe most common choices are **PostgreSQL** (the default for most applications — feature-rich, extensible, excellent JSON support) and **MySQL** (simpler, widely deployed, powers most of the web including Wikipedia and Shopify).` },

      { type: 'text', heading: 'NoSQL Databases', body: `NoSQL is not a single technology — it is an umbrella term for databases that do not use the relational model. They emerged because relational databases struggle with certain workloads: massive write throughput, flexible schemas, horizontal scaling across hundreds of nodes, and data that does not fit neatly into tables.\n\n**Document Databases (MongoDB, Firestore, CouchDB)**\nStore data as JSON-like documents. Each document can have a different structure. Documents can be nested — a user document might contain an array of addresses and an embedded preferences object. This maps naturally to how applications think about data. No JOINs needed if you embed related data in the same document.\n\nBest for: content management, user profiles, product catalogs, any data that is naturally hierarchical.\n\n**Key-Value Stores (Redis, DynamoDB, Memcached)**\nThe simplest model: a key maps to a value. The database does not care what the value contains — it could be a string, a JSON blob, or a binary object. Lookups by key are O(1) and extremely fast. But you cannot query by value — if you need to find all users in a city, you need a secondary index or a different database.\n\nBest for: caching, session storage, shopping carts, feature flags, rate limiting counters.\n\n**Wide-Column Stores (Cassandra, HBase, ScyllaDB)**\nRows have a key, but each row can have different columns. Think of it as a two-dimensional key-value store. Designed for massive write throughput and horizontal scaling. Cassandra can handle millions of writes per second across hundreds of nodes.\n\nBest for: time-series data (IoT sensor readings, logs), messaging systems, write-heavy workloads at massive scale.\n\n**Graph Databases (Neo4j, Amazon Neptune)**\nStore data as nodes (entities) and edges (relationships). Traversing relationships is O(1) per hop, regardless of dataset size. In a relational database, finding friends-of-friends requires expensive recursive JOINs. In a graph database, it is a simple traversal.\n\nBest for: social networks, recommendation engines, fraud detection, knowledge graphs.` },

      { type: 'diagram', variant: 'db-types-comparison', heading: 'Database Types at a Glance', caption: 'Choosing the right database for your access patterns' },

      { type: 'text', heading: 'Normalization vs Denormalization', body: `These are two opposing strategies for organizing data, and understanding the trade-off is essential for interviews.\n\n**Normalization** means eliminating data redundancy. Each piece of information is stored exactly once. A user's name lives in the users table. An order references the user by ID (foreign key). If the user changes their name, you update one row and every query that JOINs to users automatically sees the new name.\n\nNormalization is great for write consistency — there is one source of truth. But it is expensive for reads because every query that needs related data must perform JOINs. A product page that shows the product, its reviews, the reviewer names, and the seller info might require 4-5 JOINs.\n\n**Denormalization** means intentionally duplicating data to avoid JOINs. Instead of JOINing to the users table every time you display a review, you store the reviewer's name directly in the review document. Reads become a single lookup — no JOINs, no latency.\n\nThe cost is write complexity. When a user changes their name, you need to update it everywhere it was copied. This is called a "fan-out on write" and can be expensive if the data is copied to many places.\n\n**The rule of thumb:** Start normalized. Denormalize when you have a proven read performance problem. In interviews, explain this reasoning: "I will start with a normalized schema for consistency. If read latency becomes an issue, I can denormalize the hot paths — for example, embedding the author name in each post to avoid the JOIN on the feed query."` },

      { type: 'animation', id: 'db-replication', heading: 'Database Replication', body: 'Primary handles writes. Replicas handle reads. Replication lag is the trade-off.' },

      { type: 'text', heading: 'Schema Design for SQL', body: `Good schema design starts with identifying your entities and their relationships.\n\n**One-to-Many:** A user has many orders. The orders table has a user_id foreign key pointing to users.\n\n**Many-to-Many:** A student can enroll in many courses, and a course has many students. You need a junction table (enrollments) with student_id and course_id.\n\n**One-to-One:** A user has one profile. You can either embed the profile columns in the users table or create a separate profiles table with a user_id foreign key. Separate table is better when the profile data is large or rarely accessed.\n\nPractical tips:\n\nAlways have a primary key. Use auto-incrementing integers or UUIDs. UUIDs are better for distributed systems (no coordination needed) but are larger (16 bytes vs 4-8 bytes) and cause B-tree fragmentation.\n\nAdd created_at and updated_at timestamps to every table. You will need them for debugging, auditing, and cache invalidation.\n\nUse appropriate data types. Do not store dates as strings. Do not store monetary values as floats (use DECIMAL or integer cents). Do not store booleans as integers.\n\nIndex your foreign keys. PostgreSQL does not automatically index foreign keys (MySQL InnoDB does). Without an index, JOINs on that column will be full table scans.` },

      { type: 'text', heading: 'Schema Design for NoSQL', body: `NoSQL schema design is fundamentally different from SQL. Instead of modeling your data relationships first and figuring out queries later, you start with your access patterns and design the schema to serve them.\n\nThis is called **query-driven design**. You ask: what queries will my application run? Then you structure the data so each query can be answered by a single read — no JOINs, no secondary lookups.\n\n**Example — E-commerce product page:**\nIn SQL, you might have separate tables for products, reviews, sellers, and categories, then JOIN them.\nIn MongoDB, you would embed everything the product page needs into a single document:\n\n\`\`\`\n{\n  _id: "prod_123",\n  name: "Wireless Headphones",\n  price: 2999,\n  seller: { name: "AudioTech", rating: 4.8 },\n  reviews: [\n    { user: "Alice", rating: 5, text: "Great sound" },\n    { user: "Bob", rating: 4, text: "Good value" }\n  ],\n  categories: ["electronics", "audio"]\n}\n\`\`\`\n\nOne read, one document, all the data the page needs. The trade-off: if the seller changes their name, you need to update it in every product document they sell.\n\n**When to embed vs reference:**\nEmbed when the data is always accessed together and the embedded array will not grow unboundedly.\nReference (store an ID) when the data is accessed independently, updated frequently, or can grow very large.` },

      { type: 'diagram', variant: 'sql-vs-nosql', heading: 'SQL vs NoSQL Decision Framework', caption: 'When to choose relational vs non-relational databases' },

      { type: 'text', heading: 'ACID vs BASE', body: `These are two consistency models that represent different trade-offs.\n\n**ACID** (used by SQL databases) guarantees that transactions are atomic, consistent, isolated, and durable. After a transaction commits, the data is guaranteed to be correct and persisted. This is essential for financial systems, inventory management, and any domain where incorrect data causes real harm.\n\nThe cost of ACID is performance. Ensuring isolation requires locks or multi-version concurrency control (MVCC). Ensuring durability requires writing to disk before acknowledging. These add latency.\n\n**BASE** (used by many NoSQL databases) stands for Basically Available, Soft state, Eventually consistent. The system prioritizes availability and partition tolerance over immediate consistency. After a write, different replicas may temporarily return different values, but they will eventually converge.\n\nBASE is not "broken consistency" — it is a deliberate trade-off. A social media feed showing a post 500ms late is acceptable. A bank account showing the wrong balance is not.\n\nIn interviews, frame it as a spectrum, not a binary choice. Many systems use ACID for critical paths (payments, inventory) and BASE for non-critical paths (feeds, analytics, recommendations) within the same application.` },

      { type: 'text', heading: 'How to Choose a Database in Interviews', body: `Do not overthink the database choice. The interviewer cares about your reasoning, not the specific technology. Here is a framework:\n\n**Start with PostgreSQL** as your default. It handles 90% of use cases well — relational data, JSON support, full-text search, geospatial queries. Unless you have a specific reason to choose something else, PostgreSQL is a safe and defensible choice.\n\n**Choose a document database (MongoDB)** when your data is naturally hierarchical, your schema evolves frequently, or you need to store heterogeneous objects (different product types with different attributes).\n\n**Choose a key-value store (Redis/DynamoDB)** when you need sub-millisecond latency for simple lookups, caching, or session storage.\n\n**Choose a wide-column store (Cassandra)** when you need massive write throughput (millions of writes/sec), time-series data, or multi-region replication with tunable consistency.\n\n**Choose a graph database (Neo4j)** when your core queries involve traversing relationships — social graphs, recommendation engines, fraud detection.\n\nThe key phrase to use: "I am choosing PostgreSQL because our data has strong relationships (users, orders, products) and we need ACID transactions for payment processing. For the activity feed, I would use Redis as a cache to avoid expensive fan-out queries."` },

      { type: 'quiz', heading: 'Data Modeling Quiz', description: 'Test your understanding of data modeling concepts', questions: [
        { question: 'What does the "I" in ACID stand for?', options: ['Integrity', 'Isolation', 'Indexing', 'Immutability'], correct: 1, explanation: 'Isolation means concurrent transactions do not interfere with each other. Each transaction sees a consistent snapshot of the database, as if it were the only transaction running.' },
        { question: 'When should you denormalize your schema?', options: ['Always, for better performance', 'When you have a proven read performance problem that JOINs are causing', 'Never, normalization is always better', 'Only when using NoSQL databases'], correct: 1, explanation: 'Start normalized for consistency. Denormalize specific hot paths when you have measured that JOINs are causing unacceptable read latency. Premature denormalization creates write complexity without proven benefit.' },
        { question: 'Which database type is best for storing time-series data at massive scale?', options: ['Document database (MongoDB)', 'Graph database (Neo4j)', 'Wide-column store (Cassandra)', 'Relational database (PostgreSQL)'], correct: 2, explanation: 'Wide-column stores like Cassandra are designed for massive write throughput with time-based partitioning. They can handle millions of writes per second across hundreds of nodes, making them ideal for IoT sensor data, logs, and metrics.' },
        { question: 'In NoSQL schema design, what should you start with?', options: ['Entity-relationship diagrams', 'Your access patterns (queries)', 'Normalization rules', 'The database documentation'], correct: 1, explanation: 'NoSQL schema design is query-driven. You identify your access patterns first, then structure the data so each query can be answered by a single read. This is the opposite of SQL where you model relationships first.' },
        { question: 'Why should you avoid storing monetary values as floating-point numbers?', options: ['Floats are too slow', 'Floats cannot represent all decimal values exactly, causing rounding errors', 'Floats take too much storage', 'Floats are not supported in SQL'], correct: 1, explanation: 'Floating-point arithmetic has precision issues. 0.1 + 0.2 = 0.30000000000000004 in most languages. For money, use DECIMAL types in SQL or store values as integer cents (e.g., $19.99 = 1999 cents).' },
        { question: 'What is the main trade-off of eventual consistency (BASE)?', options: ['Data may be temporarily stale across replicas', 'Data is never persisted to disk', 'Writes are slower than ACID', 'You cannot use indexes'], correct: 0, explanation: 'In an eventually consistent system, after a write, different replicas may temporarily return different values. They will converge given enough time. This is acceptable for feeds and recommendations but not for financial transactions.' },
        { question: 'When should you embed data in a MongoDB document vs store a reference?', options: ['Always embed for performance', 'Embed when data is always accessed together and the array will not grow unboundedly', 'Always reference for consistency', 'Embed only for read-heavy workloads'], correct: 1, explanation: 'Embed when the data is always accessed together (e.g., a product and its top 5 reviews). Reference when the data is accessed independently, updated frequently, or can grow without bound (e.g., all comments on a viral post).' },
      ]},
    ],
  },

  'caching': {
    slug: 'caching',
    title: 'Caching',
    subtitle: 'Speed up reads and reduce database load with caching',
    duration: '30 min', difficulty: 'Intermediate',
    sections: [
      { type: 'text', heading: 'Why Cache?', body: `In system design interviews, caching comes up almost every time you need to handle high read traffic. Your database becomes the bottleneck, latency starts creeping up, and the interviewer is waiting for you to say the word: cache.\n\nReading a user profile from PostgreSQL may take 30-50ms, but reading from Redis takes just 1ms. That's a 30-50x improvement. Databases store data on disk; caches store data in memory, which sits much closer to the CPU.\n\n**Cache hit** — Data found in cache → fast response (~1ms)\n**Cache miss** — Data not in cache → fetch from DB (~50ms), store in cache\n\nTypical cache hit rates in production: 80-99%` },
      { type: 'animation', id: 'cache-flow', heading: 'Cache Hit vs Cache Miss', body: 'Cache hit returns data instantly. Cache miss fetches from DB and populates the cache.' },
      { type: 'text', heading: 'Where to Cache', body: `Caching shows up at multiple layers. In interviews, external caching (Redis) is the default, but knowing the other layers shows depth.\n\n**External Cache (Redis/Memcached)** — Standalone cache service between your app and DB. Every app server shares the same cache. This is what interviewers expect you to discuss first.\n\n**CDN (Content Delivery Network)** — Geographically distributed edge servers that cache static content (images, JS, CSS) close to users. Without CDN: 250-300ms latency from Virginia to India. With CDN: 20-40ms from nearest edge. Introduce CDN when your system serves static media at scale.\n\n**Client-Side Cache** — Browser cache (HTTP cache, localStorage), mobile app local storage. You have limited control from the backend. Data can go stale. Useful for reducing network calls.\n\n**In-Process Cache** — Data cached directly in the application's memory. Even faster than Redis (no network call). Good for small, frequently accessed data that rarely changes: config values, feature flags, hot keys. Each server has its own copy — not shared across instances.` },
      { type: 'text', heading: 'Cache Architectures', body: `**Cache-Aside (Lazy Loading)** — The most common pattern. Default to this in interviews.\n1. App checks cache\n2. If hit → return data\n3. If miss → fetch from DB, store in cache, return\n\nOnly caches data when needed, keeping the cache lean. Downside: first request for any key always hits DB.\n\n**Write-Through** — App writes to cache, cache synchronously writes to DB. Cache is always fresh, but writes are slower (must wait for both). Still has dual-write consistency issues.\n\n**Write-Behind (Write-Back)** — App writes to cache, cache batches and writes to DB asynchronously. Very fast writes, but risk of data loss if cache crashes before flushing. Good for analytics/metrics.\n\n**Read-Through** — Cache acts as a proxy. On miss, cache itself fetches from DB. CDNs are a form of read-through cache. Rarely used for application-level caching.\n\nIf you only remember one pattern: **cache-aside**.` },
      { type: 'text', heading: 'Cache Eviction Policies', body: `Caches have limited memory. When full, they need a strategy for what to remove.\n\n**LRU (Least Recently Used)** — Evict the item not accessed for the longest time. Default in most systems. Adapts well to workloads where recently used data is likely to be used again.\n\n**LFU (Least Frequently Used)** — Evict the item accessed least often. Works well when certain keys are consistently popular (trending videos, top playlists).\n\n**FIFO (First In First Out)** — Evict the oldest item. Ignores usage patterns. Rarely used in real systems.\n\n**TTL (Time To Live)** — Not an eviction policy by itself. Sets an expiration time per key. Often combined with LRU. Essential when data must eventually refresh (API responses, sessions).` },
      { type: 'text', heading: 'Common Caching Problems', body: `Caching makes systems faster but introduces new failure modes. Interviewers use these to test whether you understand the trade-offs.\n\n**Cache Stampede (Thundering Herd)**\nA popular cache entry expires and hundreds of requests simultaneously try to rebuild it, overwhelming the DB.\nSolutions: Request coalescing (only one request rebuilds, others wait), cache warming (refresh before expiry), probabilistic early expiration.\n\n**Cache Consistency**\nCache and DB return different values. Happens because you write to DB first but cache still holds old data.\nSolutions: Invalidate cache on writes (delete the key after DB update), short TTLs for stale tolerance, accept eventual consistency for feeds/metrics.\n\n**Hot Keys**\nOne cache key gets millions of requests/sec (e.g., Taylor Swift's profile on Twitter). Even with caching, that one key can overload a single Redis node.\nSolutions: Replicate hot keys across multiple cache nodes, add in-process cache as fallback, rate limit abusive patterns.` },
      { type: 'text', heading: 'Redis vs Memcached', body: `**Redis:**\n- Rich data structures (strings, lists, sets, sorted sets, hashes)\n- Persistence (RDB snapshots + AOF append-only file)\n- Pub/Sub messaging, Lua scripting\n- Single-threaded but ~100K ops/sec\n- Use for: sessions, leaderboards, rate limiting, queues, distributed locks\n\n**Memcached:**\n- Simple key-value only\n- Multi-threaded (better raw throughput for simple gets/sets)\n- No persistence — pure cache\n- Use for: simple object caching\n\n**Default choice:** Redis. It does everything Memcached does and more.` },
      { type: 'text', heading: 'How to Talk About Caching in Interviews', body: `Don't jump straight to caching. Establish why it's necessary first.\n\n**1. Identify the bottleneck**\n"User profile queries are hitting the DB 500 times/sec during peak. Each query takes 30ms. That's our bottleneck."\n\n**2. Decide what to cache**\nFocus on data that is read frequently, doesn't change often, and is expensive to fetch.\n"We'll cache user profiles — read on every page load, only updated when users edit settings."\n\n**3. Choose your architecture**\n"I'll use cache-aside with Redis. On read: check Redis first. If miss, query DB, store in Redis, return."\n\n**4. Set eviction policy**\n"LRU eviction with 10-minute TTL. If a user updates their profile, we invalidate the cache entry immediately."\n\n**5. Address the downsides**\nPick 1-2 relevant problems:\n- "On write, we delete the cache key so next read gets fresh data."\n- "If Redis goes down, requests fall back to DB with circuit breakers."\n- "For hot keys, we can use request coalescing."` },
      { type: 'callout', variant: 'tip', heading: 'Don\'t Cache Everything', body: 'Show you understand when caching is worth the complexity and when a well-indexed database is enough. Not every read needs a cache. If your DB handles the load fine, adding a cache just adds complexity for no benefit.' },
    ],
  },

  'sharding': {
    slug: 'sharding',
    title: 'Sharding',
    subtitle: 'Horizontal partitioning to scale databases beyond a single machine',
    duration: '30 min', difficulty: 'Intermediate',
    sections: [
      { type: 'text', heading: 'Why Shard?', body: `There comes a point in every growing system where a single database server cannot keep up. You have added read replicas to handle read traffic. You have optimized your queries and indexes. You have thrown more CPU and RAM at the machine (vertical scaling). But the writes keep growing, the dataset keeps expanding, and eventually you hit the ceiling of what one machine can do.\n\nSharding is the answer. It splits your data across multiple database servers (shards), each holding a subset of the total data. Instead of one server handling all 100 million users, you have 10 servers each handling 10 million. Writes are distributed. Storage is distributed. No single machine is a bottleneck.\n\nBut sharding is not free. It introduces significant complexity — cross-shard queries become expensive or impossible, transactions across shards require distributed coordination, and rebalancing data when you add or remove shards is operationally painful. This is why sharding should be a last resort, not a first instinct.\n\nIn interviews, the right framing is: "I would start with a single PostgreSQL instance with read replicas. If write throughput exceeds what a single primary can handle, I would introduce sharding on the user_id."` },

      { type: 'text', heading: 'Sharding Strategies', body: `The sharding strategy determines how data is distributed across shards. Each approach has different trade-offs for query patterns, data distribution, and operational complexity.\n\n**Range-Based Sharding**\nDivide data by ranges of the shard key. Users with IDs 1-1M go to shard 1, 1M-2M to shard 2, and so on. This is simple to understand and makes range queries efficient — "give me all users with IDs between 500K and 600K" only hits one shard.\n\nThe problem is hotspots. If your shard key is a timestamp, all new writes go to the latest shard while older shards sit idle. If your shard key is alphabetical, the shard holding "S" names might be much larger than the one holding "X" names. Range-based sharding works well when the key distribution is uniform and you need range queries.\n\n**Hash-Based Sharding**\nApply a hash function to the shard key: shard = hash(user_id) % num_shards. This distributes data evenly regardless of the key distribution — no hotspots. It is the most common strategy.\n\nThe downside is that range queries become expensive. "Give me all users created this week" requires querying every shard because the hash destroys ordering. And when you change the number of shards, almost all keys remap to different shards (this is where consistent hashing helps).\n\n**Directory-Based Sharding**\nA lookup service (directory) maps each key to its shard. This is the most flexible — you can move individual keys between shards without changing the algorithm. But the directory itself becomes a bottleneck and a single point of failure. It must be highly available and fast.\n\n**Geographic Sharding**\nData is partitioned by geographic region. European users go to the EU shard, US users to the US shard. This reduces latency (data is close to users) and can help with data residency regulations (GDPR requires EU data to stay in the EU). The trade-off is that cross-region queries are slow and users who travel may experience inconsistencies.` },

      { type: 'text', heading: 'Choosing a Shard Key', body: `The shard key is the most important decision in sharding. A bad shard key creates hotspots, makes common queries expensive, and is extremely painful to change later.\n\nA good shard key has three properties:\n\n**High cardinality** — Many distinct values. user_id is good (millions of values). country is bad (only ~200 values, leading to uneven shards).\n\n**Even distribution** — Values should be spread uniformly. Auto-incrementing IDs with hash-based sharding give perfect distribution. Timestamps with range-based sharding create hotspots.\n\n**Query alignment** — Most queries should be able to target a single shard. If 90% of your queries filter by user_id, shard by user_id. If you shard by user_id but most queries filter by city, every query hits every shard.\n\nCommon shard keys by domain:\n- Social media: user_id (most queries are user-scoped)\n- E-commerce: order_id or customer_id\n- Multi-tenant SaaS: tenant_id (natural isolation)\n- Messaging: conversation_id (messages in a conversation stay together)\n- Analytics: timestamp + hash (time-range queries with even distribution)` },

      { type: 'text', heading: 'Challenges of Sharding', body: `Sharding solves the scaling problem but creates several new ones. Understanding these trade-offs is what separates a good interview answer from a great one.\n\n**Cross-Shard Queries**\nA query that needs data from multiple shards (e.g., "find the top 10 most active users globally") must be sent to every shard, results collected and merged. This is called scatter-gather and is much slower than a single-shard query. Design your shard key to minimize cross-shard queries.\n\n**Cross-Shard Transactions**\nACID transactions across shards require two-phase commit (2PC), which is slow, complex, and reduces availability. Most sharded systems avoid cross-shard transactions entirely. If an operation must touch multiple shards, use the saga pattern with eventual consistency.\n\n**Rebalancing**\nWhen you add a new shard, data must be redistributed. With simple hash-based sharding (hash % N), changing N remaps almost all keys. Consistent hashing reduces this to K/N keys on average. Even so, rebalancing is an operational event that requires careful planning — you are moving data while the system is live.\n\n**Hotspots (Celebrity Problem)**\nEven with good distribution, some keys get disproportionate traffic. A celebrity's profile on a social network might receive 100x more reads than an average user. Solutions: cache hot keys aggressively, replicate hot shards, or use a dedicated shard for known hot keys.\n\n**Operational Complexity**\nBackups, schema migrations, monitoring — everything is now multiplied by the number of shards. A schema migration that takes 5 minutes on one database takes 5 minutes × 100 shards, and must be coordinated carefully to avoid downtime.` },

      { type: 'callout', variant: 'warning', heading: 'Avoid Premature Sharding', body: `Sharding adds enormous complexity. Most systems do not need it until they have millions of active users and have exhausted simpler scaling options. The progression should be:\n\n1. Optimize queries and indexes\n2. Vertical scaling (bigger machine)\n3. Read replicas for read-heavy workloads\n4. Caching (Redis) for hot data\n5. Sharding — only when writes exceed what a single primary can handle\n\nMany successful companies (Shopify, GitHub) ran on a single database for years before sharding.` },

      { type: 'callout', variant: 'example', heading: 'Sharding in Interviews', body: `When the interviewer asks about scaling the database, do not jump to sharding immediately. Walk through the progression:\n\n"First, I would optimize queries and add indexes. Then add read replicas to handle read traffic. If write throughput is still a bottleneck, I would introduce sharding by user_id using consistent hashing. This keeps all of a user's data on one shard, so most queries (profile, feed, settings) hit a single shard. For global queries like trending topics, I would use a separate aggregation service that periodically computes results from all shards."` },

      { type: 'quiz', heading: 'Sharding Quiz', description: 'Test your understanding of database sharding', questions: [
        { question: 'What is the main problem with range-based sharding?', options: ['It does not support range queries', 'It can create hotspots if the key distribution is uneven', 'It requires a lookup directory', 'It only works with NoSQL databases'], correct: 1, explanation: 'Range-based sharding creates hotspots when the key distribution is skewed. For example, sharding by timestamp puts all new writes on the latest shard while older shards sit idle.' },
        { question: 'Why is sharding considered a last resort for scaling?', options: ['It is too expensive', 'It adds significant operational complexity (cross-shard queries, rebalancing, distributed transactions)', 'It only works with specific databases', 'It reduces read performance'], correct: 1, explanation: 'Sharding introduces cross-shard query overhead, makes transactions across shards extremely complex, requires careful rebalancing when adding nodes, and multiplies operational burden for backups and migrations.' },
        { question: 'What makes a good shard key?', options: ['Low cardinality for simplicity', 'High cardinality, even distribution, and alignment with common query patterns', 'Always use timestamps', 'The primary key of the table'], correct: 1, explanation: 'A good shard key has high cardinality (many distinct values), distributes data evenly across shards, and aligns with your most common queries so most requests hit a single shard.' },
        { question: 'How does consistent hashing help with sharding?', options: ['It eliminates the need for sharding', 'It minimizes data movement when adding or removing shards', 'It makes cross-shard queries faster', 'It provides ACID transactions across shards'], correct: 1, explanation: 'With simple hash % N, changing N remaps almost all keys. Consistent hashing maps keys to a ring, so adding a shard only moves K/N keys on average — a massive reduction in data movement during rebalancing.' },
      ]},
    ],
  },

  'consistent-hashing': {
    slug: 'consistent-hashing',
    title: 'Consistent Hashing',
    subtitle: 'Minimize data movement when adding or removing nodes',
    duration: '25 min', difficulty: 'Intermediate',
    sections: [
      { type: 'text', heading: 'The Problem with Simple Hashing', body: `Imagine you have 4 cache servers and you distribute keys using hash(key) % 4. Key "user:42" hashes to server 2, key "user:99" hashes to server 3, and so on. This works perfectly — until you need to add a fifth server.\n\nWhen you change from hash(key) % 4 to hash(key) % 5, almost every key maps to a different server. With 1 million cached keys, roughly 750,000 of them (75%) will remap. That means 750,000 cache misses hitting your database simultaneously — a cache stampede that could take down your system.\n\nThe same problem occurs when a server dies. Going from 4 to 3 servers remaps about 75% of keys. In a distributed system where nodes join and leave regularly, this level of disruption is unacceptable.\n\nConsistent hashing solves this by ensuring that when you add or remove a node, only a small fraction of keys need to move — roughly K/N on average, where K is the total number of keys and N is the number of nodes.` },

      { type: 'text', heading: 'How the Hash Ring Works', body: `Picture a circle (ring) with positions numbered from 0 to some large number (typically 0 to 2^32 - 1). Both servers and keys are placed on this ring using a hash function.\n\nTo place a server: hash the server's identifier (e.g., its IP address) to get a position on the ring. Server A might land at position 1000, Server B at position 5000, Server C at position 9000.\n\nTo place a key: hash the key to get a position on the ring. Key "user:42" might land at position 3500.\n\nTo find which server owns a key: walk clockwise from the key's position until you hit a server. Key "user:42" at position 3500 walks clockwise and hits Server B at position 5000. So Server B owns that key.\n\n**Adding a server:** When Server D joins at position 7000, only the keys between Server C (5000) and Server D (7000) need to move — they were previously owned by Server C (the next server clockwise) and now belong to Server D. All other keys stay put.\n\n**Removing a server:** When Server B at position 5000 leaves, its keys (those between Server A at 1000 and Server B at 5000) move to the next server clockwise — Server C at 9000. Again, only a fraction of keys move.\n\nThis is the core insight: adding or removing a node only affects the keys in the arc between the new/removed node and its predecessor on the ring.` },

      { type: 'text', heading: 'Virtual Nodes — Solving Uneven Distribution', body: `There is a problem with the basic hash ring. With only 3-4 physical servers, the positions on the ring are unlikely to be evenly spaced. One server might own 60% of the ring while another owns only 10%. This means one server handles 6x more data and traffic than another.\n\nVirtual nodes fix this. Instead of placing each physical server at one position on the ring, you place it at many positions — say, 150 virtual nodes per physical server. Server A gets positions A-1, A-2, A-3, ..., A-150, each at a different hash position on the ring.\n\nWith 4 physical servers × 150 virtual nodes = 600 points on the ring, the distribution becomes very even. Each physical server owns approximately 25% of the key space, regardless of where the hash function places the virtual nodes.\n\nVirtual nodes also make rebalancing smoother. When a new physical server joins, its 150 virtual nodes are scattered across the ring, taking a small slice of keys from many existing servers rather than a large chunk from one server. The load redistribution is gradual and even.\n\nThe trade-off is memory: you need to store the mapping of all virtual nodes to physical servers. With 150 virtual nodes per server and 100 servers, that is 15,000 entries — trivial for modern systems.` },

      { type: 'text', heading: 'Where Consistent Hashing is Used', body: `Consistent hashing is not just a theoretical concept — it powers some of the most critical distributed systems in production.\n\n**Distributed Caches (Memcached, Redis Cluster)**\nWhen you have a cluster of cache servers, consistent hashing determines which server holds each key. When a cache server dies, only its keys are redistributed — the rest of the cache remains warm.\n\n**Distributed Databases (Cassandra, DynamoDB, Riak)**\nCassandra uses consistent hashing to distribute data across nodes in a cluster. Each node is responsible for a range of the hash ring. When you add a node, it takes over a portion of the ring from its neighbors.\n\n**Load Balancers**\nSome load balancers use consistent hashing for session affinity — ensuring that requests from the same client always go to the same backend server. This is useful when servers maintain in-memory state (WebSocket connections, shopping carts).\n\n**Content Delivery Networks (CDNs)**\nCDNs use consistent hashing to determine which edge server caches which content. When an edge server goes down, its content is served by the next server on the ring, not redistributed across all servers.\n\nIn interviews, consistent hashing typically comes up when you are designing a distributed cache, a sharded database, or any system where you need to distribute data across nodes and handle node additions/removals gracefully.` },

      { type: 'callout', variant: 'example', heading: 'Interview Walkthrough', body: `"For our distributed cache, I will use consistent hashing with virtual nodes. Each of our 10 Redis servers gets 150 virtual nodes on the hash ring, giving us 1,500 points for even distribution. When a key comes in, we hash it and walk clockwise to find the owning server. If we need to add an 11th server, only about 1/11 of the keys move — roughly 9% — instead of the 91% that simple modular hashing would cause. For fault tolerance, I will replicate each key to the next 2 servers clockwise on the ring, so if a server dies, the replicas serve the data while the keys are being redistributed."` },

      { type: 'quiz', heading: 'Consistent Hashing Quiz', description: 'Test your understanding of consistent hashing', questions: [
        { question: 'With simple hash(key) % N, what percentage of keys remap when you add 1 server to a 10-server cluster?', options: ['About 10%', 'About 50%', 'About 91%', 'About 100%'], correct: 2, explanation: 'When N changes from 10 to 11, hash(key) % 10 vs hash(key) % 11 gives different results for about (N-1)/N = 10/11 ≈ 91% of keys. Only ~9% happen to map to the same server.' },
        { question: 'What do virtual nodes solve?', options: ['They make hashing faster', 'They ensure even data distribution across physical servers', 'They eliminate the need for replication', 'They reduce network latency'], correct: 1, explanation: 'With few physical servers, positions on the ring are unevenly spaced. Virtual nodes (many ring positions per physical server) ensure each server owns approximately equal portions of the key space.' },
        { question: 'When a server is removed from a consistent hash ring, where do its keys go?', options: ['They are deleted', 'They are redistributed to all remaining servers equally', 'They move to the next server clockwise on the ring', 'They move to a random server'], correct: 2, explanation: 'Keys owned by the removed server move to the next server clockwise on the ring — the server that would have been the "next stop" if the removed server did not exist. Only that one server is affected.' },
      ]},
    ],
  },

  'cap-theorem': {
    slug: 'cap-theorem',
    title: 'CAP Theorem',
    subtitle: 'Consistency, Availability, Partition Tolerance — pick two',
    duration: '25 min', difficulty: 'Intermediate',
    sections: [
      { type: 'text', heading: 'Understanding the CAP Theorem', body: `The CAP theorem, proposed by Eric Brewer in 2000, states that a distributed data store can only guarantee two of three properties simultaneously:\n\n**Consistency (C)** — Every read receives the most recent write or an error. All nodes see the same data at the same time. If you write a value and immediately read it from any node, you get the value you just wrote.\n\n**Availability (A)** — Every request receives a non-error response, without guarantee that it contains the most recent write. The system always responds, even if the data might be slightly stale.\n\n**Partition Tolerance (P)** — The system continues to operate despite network partitions (messages being lost or delayed between nodes). In any real distributed system, network partitions will happen — cables get cut, switches fail, data centers lose connectivity.\n\nHere is the critical insight that most people miss: since network partitions are inevitable in distributed systems, you are not really choosing between C, A, and P. You are always choosing P (because you have no choice), and then deciding between C and A when a partition occurs. The real question is: when the network splits, do you return an error (choosing consistency) or return potentially stale data (choosing availability)?` },

      { type: 'text', heading: 'CP Systems — Consistency over Availability', body: `A CP system, when faced with a network partition, chooses to return an error or become unavailable rather than return stale data. It guarantees that every successful read reflects the most recent write.\n\nImagine a banking system with two data centers. A network partition splits them. A customer tries to check their balance from the isolated data center. A CP system would return an error ("service temporarily unavailable") rather than show a balance that might not reflect a recent transaction processed by the other data center.\n\n**Examples of CP systems:**\n- **ZooKeeper** — Used for distributed coordination (leader election, configuration management). Correctness is more important than availability.\n- **HBase** — Strongly consistent reads and writes. Used when you need to guarantee that reads always return the latest data.\n- **MongoDB (with majority read/write concern)** — Can be configured for strong consistency at the cost of availability during partitions.\n- **etcd** — Distributed key-value store used by Kubernetes. Configuration data must be consistent.\n\n**Use CP when:** Financial transactions, inventory management (preventing overselling), distributed locks, leader election — any domain where returning stale or incorrect data causes real harm.` },

      { type: 'text', heading: 'AP Systems — Availability over Consistency', body: `An AP system, when faced with a network partition, continues to serve requests with potentially stale data rather than returning errors. It prioritizes being responsive over being perfectly accurate.\n\nImagine a social media feed. A network partition splits two data centers. A user posts a photo, which is written to data center A. Users in data center B do not see the photo immediately — they see a slightly stale feed. But the system is still working. Nobody gets an error. Once the partition heals, the photo propagates to data center B and everyone sees it.\n\n**Examples of AP systems:**\n- **Cassandra** — Tunable consistency, but defaults to eventual consistency for high availability. Used by Netflix, Discord, Apple.\n- **DynamoDB** — Eventually consistent reads by default (strongly consistent reads available at higher cost).\n- **CouchDB** — Designed for availability and partition tolerance with eventual consistency.\n- **DNS** — The Domain Name System is eventually consistent. DNS record changes propagate over hours, but the system never goes down.\n\n**Use AP when:** Social media feeds, product catalogs, recommendation engines, DNS, content delivery — domains where showing slightly stale data is acceptable and downtime is not.` },

      { type: 'text', heading: 'Eventual Consistency — The Reality', body: `Most AP systems use eventual consistency: given enough time without new updates, all replicas will converge to the same value. The "eventually" part is usually fast — milliseconds to seconds in a healthy system. It is only during partitions that staleness can last longer.\n\nBut what happens when two nodes accept conflicting writes during a partition? When the partition heals, the system must resolve the conflict. There are several strategies:\n\n**Last Write Wins (LWW)** — The write with the latest timestamp wins. Simple and widely used (Cassandra, DynamoDB). The problem: clocks are not perfectly synchronized across nodes. A write that happened "later" in real time might have an earlier timestamp due to clock skew, causing data loss.\n\n**Vector Clocks** — Each node maintains a vector of logical timestamps. When a conflict is detected, the system can determine if one write causally follows another (no conflict) or if they are truly concurrent (conflict). Concurrent conflicts are surfaced to the application for resolution. Used by Riak and Amazon's original Dynamo paper.\n\n**CRDTs (Conflict-free Replicated Data Types)** — Data structures that are mathematically guaranteed to merge without conflicts. A G-Counter (grow-only counter) can be incremented on any node and merged by taking the maximum of each node's count. Used in collaborative editing (Figma), distributed counters, and shopping carts.\n\n**Application-Level Resolution** — The system stores all conflicting versions and lets the application decide. Amazon's shopping cart famously uses this — if two versions of your cart conflict, it merges them (you might see a deleted item reappear, but you never lose an added item).` },

      { type: 'text', heading: 'PACELC — Beyond CAP', body: `The CAP theorem only describes behavior during network partitions. But partitions are rare — most of the time, the network is healthy. What trade-offs exist then?\n\nThe PACELC theorem extends CAP: if there is a Partition (P), choose between Availability (A) and Consistency (C). Else (E), when the system is running normally, choose between Latency (L) and Consistency (C).\n\nThis captures a more nuanced reality:\n\n**PA/EL systems** (e.g., Cassandra, DynamoDB) — During partitions, choose availability. During normal operation, choose low latency (eventual consistency). These systems are optimized for speed and uptime.\n\n**PC/EC systems** (e.g., HBase, ZooKeeper) — During partitions, choose consistency. During normal operation, still choose consistency over latency. These systems are optimized for correctness.\n\n**PA/EC systems** (e.g., MongoDB default) — During partitions, choose availability. During normal operation, choose consistency. A pragmatic middle ground.\n\nIn interviews, mentioning PACELC shows you understand that the CAP trade-off is not just about failure scenarios — it affects everyday performance decisions.` },

      { type: 'callout', variant: 'example', heading: 'CAP in Interviews', body: `When discussing database choices, frame it around CAP:\n\n"For the payment service, I need strong consistency — a user should never see a stale balance. I will use PostgreSQL with synchronous replication (CP). For the activity feed, eventual consistency is fine — seeing a post 500ms late is acceptable. I will use Cassandra (AP) with a 1-second consistency window."\n\nThis shows you understand that different parts of the same system can make different CAP trade-offs based on their requirements.` },

      { type: 'quiz', heading: 'CAP Theorem Quiz', description: 'Test your understanding of distributed system trade-offs', questions: [
        { question: 'In the CAP theorem, why can you not choose CA (consistency + availability) without partition tolerance?', options: ['It is too expensive', 'Network partitions are inevitable in distributed systems, so P is always required', 'CA systems are slower', 'CA only works with SQL databases'], correct: 1, explanation: 'Network partitions will happen in any distributed system (hardware failures, network issues). Since you cannot prevent partitions, you must tolerate them (P). The real choice is between C and A when a partition occurs.' },
        { question: 'A social media feed shows a post 2 seconds after it was created. What consistency model is this?', options: ['Strong consistency', 'Eventual consistency', 'No consistency', 'Causal consistency'], correct: 1, explanation: 'Eventual consistency means all replicas will converge to the same value given enough time. A 2-second delay before a post appears on all nodes is a typical example of eventual consistency in action.' },
        { question: 'What does PACELC add to the CAP theorem?', options: ['It adds a fourth property: Latency', 'It describes the trade-off between Latency and Consistency during normal (non-partition) operation', 'It replaces the CAP theorem', 'It only applies to NoSQL databases'], correct: 1, explanation: 'PACELC extends CAP by noting that even without partitions, there is a trade-off between latency and consistency. Systems like Cassandra choose low latency (eventual consistency) even during normal operation.' },
        { question: 'Which conflict resolution strategy is mathematically guaranteed to merge without conflicts?', options: ['Last Write Wins', 'Vector Clocks', 'CRDTs (Conflict-free Replicated Data Types)', 'Two-Phase Commit'], correct: 2, explanation: 'CRDTs are data structures designed so that concurrent updates on different nodes can always be merged automatically without conflicts. Examples include grow-only counters, sets, and registers.' },
      ]},
    ],
  },

  'database-indexing': {
    slug: 'database-indexing',
    title: 'Database Indexing',
    subtitle: 'Speed up queries with the right indexes',
    duration: '25 min', difficulty: 'Intermediate',
    sections: [
      { type: 'text', heading: 'What is an Index?', body: `Think of a database index like the index at the back of a textbook. Without it, finding a topic means scanning every page from start to finish. With it, you look up the topic in the index, get the page number, and jump directly there.\n\nA database index works the same way. Without an index, the database must scan every row in the table to find matching records — this is called a full table scan and is O(N). With an index, the database can jump directly to the matching rows in O(log N) for tree-based indexes or O(1) for hash indexes.\n\nThe trade-off is straightforward: indexes make reads faster but writes slower. Every INSERT, UPDATE, or DELETE must also update all relevant indexes. Indexes also consume additional storage — a table with 5 indexes effectively stores parts of the data 6 times (the table + 5 index structures).\n\nIn interviews, indexing comes up when you are discussing database performance. The interviewer wants to see that you understand when to add indexes, what types exist, and what the trade-offs are.` },

      { type: 'text', heading: 'B-Tree Indexes — The Default', body: `B-Tree (balanced tree) indexes are the default index type in PostgreSQL, MySQL, and most relational databases. When you create an index without specifying a type, you get a B-Tree.\n\nA B-Tree is a self-balancing tree structure where each node can have multiple children. The data is sorted, which means B-Trees support a wide range of query types:\n\n**Equality queries:** WHERE user_id = 42 — traverse the tree to find the exact value. O(log N).\n\n**Range queries:** WHERE created_at > '2024-01-01' — find the start of the range in the tree, then scan sequentially. Efficient because the data is sorted.\n\n**Prefix queries:** WHERE name LIKE 'Ali%' — works because the index is sorted alphabetically. But LIKE '%ice' (suffix search) cannot use the index because it would need to check every entry.\n\n**ORDER BY:** If you have an index on created_at, the database can return results in order without a separate sort step.\n\n**MIN/MAX:** Finding the minimum or maximum value is O(log N) — just go to the leftmost or rightmost leaf.\n\nB-Trees are the workhorse of database indexing. Unless you have a specific reason to use something else, B-Tree is the right choice.` },

      { type: 'text', heading: 'Other Index Types', body: `**Hash Index**\nMaps keys to values using a hash function. O(1) lookups for equality queries (WHERE id = 42). Cannot do range queries, sorting, or prefix matching. PostgreSQL supports hash indexes but they are rarely used because B-Trees handle equality queries almost as fast and support much more.\n\n**GIN (Generalized Inverted Index)**\nDesigned for values that contain multiple elements — arrays, JSON fields, full-text search. If you have a tags column containing ["python", "javascript", "react"], a GIN index lets you efficiently query WHERE tags @> '["python"]'. PostgreSQL uses GIN for its full-text search and JSONB indexing.\n\n**GiST (Generalized Search Tree)**\nFor geometric and spatial data. If you need to find all restaurants within 5km of a location, a GiST index on the coordinates column makes this query fast. Also used for range types and full-text search.\n\n**BRIN (Block Range Index)**\nExtremely compact index for naturally ordered data. If your table is ordered by timestamp (new rows always have later timestamps), a BRIN index stores the min/max timestamp per block of rows. Much smaller than a B-Tree but only useful when the physical order of rows correlates with the indexed column. Great for time-series data and append-only tables.` },

      { type: 'text', heading: 'Composite Indexes and the Leftmost Prefix Rule', body: `A composite index (also called a multi-column index) indexes multiple columns together. The order of columns matters enormously.\n\nConsider an index on (country, city, zip_code). This index can efficiently serve:\n- WHERE country = 'US' (uses first column)\n- WHERE country = 'US' AND city = 'Seattle' (uses first two columns)\n- WHERE country = 'US' AND city = 'Seattle' AND zip_code = '98101' (uses all three)\n\nBut it cannot efficiently serve:\n- WHERE city = 'Seattle' (skips the first column — cannot use the index)\n- WHERE zip_code = '98101' (skips the first two columns)\n\nThis is the **leftmost prefix rule**: a composite index can only be used if the query includes the leftmost column(s) of the index, in order. Think of it like a phone book sorted by last name, then first name. You can look up "Smith" or "Smith, John" efficiently, but you cannot look up "John" without scanning the entire book.\n\n**Column order strategy:** Put the most selective column (highest cardinality) first, followed by columns used in range conditions last. Equality conditions should come before range conditions in the index.` },

      { type: 'text', heading: 'Covering Indexes', body: `A covering index includes all the columns that a query needs, so the database can answer the query entirely from the index without looking up the actual table row. This eliminates the "index scan + table lookup" step, which can be a significant performance win.\n\nExample: You frequently run this query:\n\n\`\`\`\nSELECT name, email FROM users WHERE status = 'active' ORDER BY created_at DESC LIMIT 20\n\`\`\`\n\nA covering index would be: CREATE INDEX idx_active_users ON users(status, created_at DESC) INCLUDE (name, email)\n\nThe database finds matching rows using the index (status = 'active', ordered by created_at), and the name and email are already in the index — no need to read the table. This is called an "index-only scan" and can be 2-10x faster than a regular index scan for wide tables.\n\nIn PostgreSQL, use INCLUDE to add non-key columns to the index. In MySQL, all columns of the primary key are automatically included in secondary indexes (because InnoDB secondary indexes point to the primary key).` },

      { type: 'text', heading: 'When to Index (and When Not To)', body: `**Always index:**\n- Primary keys (automatic in all databases)\n- Foreign keys (automatic in MySQL InnoDB, NOT automatic in PostgreSQL — add them manually)\n- Columns frequently used in WHERE clauses\n- Columns used in JOIN conditions\n- Columns used in ORDER BY with LIMIT (pagination queries)\n\n**Think carefully before indexing:**\n- Columns with very low cardinality (e.g., a boolean is_active column with only 2 values — the index does not help much because the database still needs to scan half the table)\n- Tables with heavy write traffic — each index slows down every write\n- Very wide indexes (many columns) — they consume significant storage and memory\n\n**Do not index:**\n- Small tables (< 1000 rows) — full table scan is fast enough\n- Columns that are rarely queried\n- Columns that change very frequently (the index rebuild cost outweighs the read benefit)\n\nA common mistake is over-indexing. A table with 10 indexes means every INSERT updates 10 index structures. Monitor your slow query log, identify the queries that need optimization, and add indexes surgically.` },

      { type: 'callout', variant: 'tip', heading: 'EXPLAIN — Your Best Friend', body: `Every database has a query planner that decides how to execute a query. Use EXPLAIN (PostgreSQL/MySQL) or EXPLAIN ANALYZE to see the execution plan:\n\n- Is it using your index or doing a full table scan?\n- How many rows is it scanning?\n- Is it doing a sort in memory or using the index order?\n\nIf you see "Seq Scan" on a large table where you expected an index scan, either the index does not exist, the query does not match the index's leftmost prefix, or the planner decided a sequential scan is actually faster (common when the query returns a large percentage of the table).` },

      { type: 'quiz', heading: 'Database Indexing Quiz', description: 'Test your understanding of database indexes', questions: [
        { question: 'You have a composite index on (country, city). Which query can use this index?', options: ['WHERE city = "Seattle"', 'WHERE country = "US" AND city = "Seattle"', 'WHERE city = "Seattle" AND country = "US"', 'Both B and C'], correct: 3, explanation: 'Both B and C can use the index. The query optimizer reorders conditions to match the index regardless of the order you write them in the WHERE clause. What matters is that the leftmost column (country) is present. Query A cannot use the index because it skips the leftmost column.' },
        { question: 'What is the main trade-off of adding more indexes?', options: ['Reads become slower', 'Writes become slower because every index must be updated', 'The database uses less memory', 'Queries become more complex'], correct: 1, explanation: 'Every INSERT, UPDATE, and DELETE must update all relevant indexes. A table with 10 indexes means 10 additional write operations per row change. This is why over-indexing is a real problem for write-heavy workloads.' },
        { question: 'When is a hash index better than a B-Tree index?', options: ['For range queries', 'For exact equality lookups only, when you never need range queries or sorting', 'For full-text search', 'Hash indexes are always better'], correct: 1, explanation: 'Hash indexes provide O(1) lookups for equality queries but cannot handle range queries, sorting, or prefix matching. B-Trees handle all of these at O(log N), which is why B-Trees are the default and hash indexes are rarely used.' },
        { question: 'What is a covering index?', options: ['An index that covers all tables in the database', 'An index that includes all columns needed by a query, avoiding table lookups', 'An index on every column of a table', 'An index that automatically updates'], correct: 1, explanation: 'A covering index contains all the columns a query needs, so the database can answer the query entirely from the index without reading the actual table rows. This eliminates the table lookup step and can be 2-10x faster.' },
      ]},
    ],
  },

  'numbers-to-know': {
    slug: 'numbers-to-know',
    title: 'Scale & Estimation',
    subtitle: 'Latency numbers, storage math, and back-of-envelope calculations',
    duration: '20 min', difficulty: 'Beginner',
    sections: [
      { type: 'text', heading: 'Why Numbers Matter', body: `System design interviews are not just about architecture diagrams — they are about making quantitative arguments. When you say "we need a cache," the interviewer wants to know why. When you say "we need to shard," they want to see the math that proves a single database cannot handle the load.\n\nBack-of-envelope calculations let you estimate whether a design will work before building it. Can a single server handle 10,000 requests per second? How much storage do we need for a year of tweets? Will the data fit in memory? These are the questions that separate hand-wavy answers from convincing ones.\n\nYou do not need to memorize exact numbers. What matters is knowing the order of magnitude — is it milliseconds or microseconds? Gigabytes or terabytes? Being off by 2x is fine. Being off by 1000x means your design is fundamentally wrong.` },

      { type: 'text', heading: 'Latency Numbers Every Engineer Should Know', body: `These numbers come from Jeff Dean's famous talk and have been updated for modern hardware. The exact values change with each hardware generation, but the relative ratios stay remarkably stable.\n\n**L1 cache reference:** ~1 ns\n**L2 cache reference:** ~4 ns\n**Main memory (RAM) access:** ~100 ns\n**SSD random read:** ~100 μs (100,000 ns)\n**HDD seek:** ~10 ms (10,000,000 ns)\n**Send 1 KB over 1 Gbps network:** ~10 μs\n**Read 1 MB sequentially from SSD:** ~1 ms\n**Read 1 MB sequentially from HDD:** ~20 ms\n**Round trip within same datacenter:** ~0.5 ms\n**Round trip cross-region (US East → US West):** ~40 ms\n**Round trip cross-continent (US → Europe):** ~150 ms\n**Round trip cross-world (US → Australia):** ~300 ms\n\nThe key ratios to internalize:\n- RAM is ~1,000x faster than SSD\n- SSD is ~100x faster than HDD\n- Same-datacenter network is ~100x faster than cross-continent\n- Sequential reads are ~100x faster than random reads (on both SSD and HDD)\n\nThese ratios explain most architectural decisions. Why do we cache in memory? Because RAM is 1000x faster than disk. Why do we use CDNs? Because serving from a nearby edge (5ms) is 30x faster than serving from a distant origin (150ms). Why do we batch writes? Because sequential I/O is 100x faster than random I/O.` },

      { type: 'text', heading: 'Storage and Throughput Numbers', body: `**Storage sizes:**\n1 KB = 1,000 bytes ≈ a short email or a JSON API response\n1 MB = 1,000 KB ≈ a compressed photo or a minute of MP3 audio\n1 GB = 1,000 MB ≈ an hour of HD video or 1,000 high-res photos\n1 TB = 1,000 GB ≈ a large database or 500 hours of HD video\n1 PB = 1,000 TB ≈ Facebook stores ~600 PB of data\n\n**Common object sizes:**\n- Tweet / short message: ~300 bytes\n- User profile (JSON): ~1 KB\n- Profile photo thumbnail: ~50 KB\n- Full-size photo: ~2 MB\n- 1 minute of video (720p): ~50 MB\n- 1 hour of video (1080p): ~3 GB\n\n**Server throughput (single machine):**\n- Web server (Nginx): ~50,000 requests/sec for static content\n- Application server (Node.js): ~5,000-10,000 requests/sec\n- Redis: ~100,000 operations/sec\n- PostgreSQL: ~5,000-20,000 queries/sec (depends on query complexity)\n- Kafka: ~1,000,000 messages/sec per broker\n\n**Network bandwidth:**\n- 1 Gbps = ~125 MB/sec (typical server NIC)\n- 10 Gbps = ~1.25 GB/sec (modern datacenter)\n- 100 Gbps = ~12.5 GB/sec (high-end datacenter backbone)` },

      { type: 'text', heading: 'Back-of-Envelope Calculation Framework', body: `Here is a repeatable framework for estimation questions in interviews.\n\n**Step 1: Estimate daily active users (DAU)**\nStart with the total user base and estimate what percentage are active daily. For social media, ~50% DAU/MAU ratio is common. For a niche tool, maybe 10-20%.\n\n**Step 2: Estimate actions per user per day**\nHow many times does a user perform the key action? A Twitter user might read 100 tweets and write 2 per day. An Uber user might take 2 rides per day.\n\n**Step 3: Calculate QPS (Queries Per Second)**\nQPS = DAU × actions_per_day ÷ 86,400 seconds\nPeak QPS is typically 2-3x average QPS.\n\n**Step 4: Calculate storage**\nStorage per year = writes_per_day × record_size × 365\nAlways account for metadata, indexes (add 30-50%), and replication (2-3x for redundancy).\n\n**Step 5: Calculate bandwidth**\nBandwidth = QPS × average_response_size\n\n**Example — Designing a URL Shortener:**\n- 100M DAU, each creates 1 short URL/day, reads 10/day\n- Write QPS = 100M ÷ 86,400 ≈ 1,200/sec (peak: ~3,600/sec)\n- Read QPS = 100M × 10 ÷ 86,400 ≈ 12,000/sec (peak: ~36,000/sec)\n- Storage per URL: ~500 bytes (short code + long URL + metadata)\n- Storage per year: 100M × 500 bytes × 365 = ~18 TB/year\n- With 3x replication: ~54 TB/year\n\nConclusion: A single PostgreSQL instance can handle the write QPS. Read QPS needs caching (Redis) or read replicas. Storage is manageable — no sharding needed for years.` },

      { type: 'text', heading: 'Powers of 2 — Quick Reference', body: `Memorize these for instant mental math:\n\n2^10 = 1,024 ≈ **1 Thousand** (1 KB)\n2^20 = 1,048,576 ≈ **1 Million** (1 MB)\n2^30 = 1,073,741,824 ≈ **1 Billion** (1 GB)\n2^40 ≈ **1 Trillion** (1 TB)\n\n**Useful shortcuts:**\n- 1 million seconds ≈ 11.5 days\n- 1 billion seconds ≈ 31.7 years\n- 86,400 seconds in a day (round to ~100K for quick math)\n- 2.5 million seconds in a month (round to ~2.5M)\n- 31.5 million seconds in a year (round to ~30M)\n\n**Quick QPS estimation:**\n- 1M requests/day ≈ 12 QPS\n- 10M requests/day ≈ 120 QPS\n- 100M requests/day ≈ 1,200 QPS\n- 1B requests/day ≈ 12,000 QPS\n\nWhen doing math in an interview, round aggressively. 86,400 becomes 100,000. 365 becomes 400. The interviewer cares about the order of magnitude, not the exact number.` },

      { type: 'callout', variant: 'example', heading: 'Practice Problem', body: `"Estimate the storage needed for Instagram's photo service."\n\n- 2B total users, 500M DAU\n- 20% of DAU upload a photo daily = 100M photos/day\n- Average photo size: 2 MB (after compression)\n- Storage per day: 100M × 2 MB = 200 TB/day\n- Storage per year: 200 TB × 365 = 73 PB/year\n- With 3x replication: ~220 PB/year\n- Plus thumbnails (3 sizes × 50 KB each): add ~15 TB/day\n\nConclusion: This is massive — you need a distributed object storage system (like S3), not a database. Photos are stored in object storage, only metadata (URLs, user IDs, timestamps) goes in the database.` },

      { type: 'quiz', heading: 'Numbers Quiz', description: 'Test your estimation skills', questions: [
        { question: 'RAM is approximately how much faster than SSD for random reads?', options: ['10x', '100x', '1,000x', '10,000x'], correct: 2, explanation: 'RAM access is ~100 ns. SSD random read is ~100 μs (100,000 ns). That is a 1,000x difference. This is why caching in memory (Redis) provides such dramatic speedups.' },
        { question: 'A service has 50M DAU, each making 20 requests/day. What is the approximate average QPS?', options: ['~120', '~1,200', '~12,000', '~120,000'], correct: 2, explanation: '50M × 20 = 1B requests/day. 1B ÷ 86,400 ≈ 11,574 QPS. Rounding: ~12,000 QPS. Peak would be 2-3x this, so ~24,000-36,000 QPS.' },
        { question: 'How many seconds are in a day (approximately)?', options: ['~10,000', '~86,000', '~860,000', '~8,600,000'], correct: 1, explanation: '24 hours × 60 minutes × 60 seconds = 86,400 seconds. For quick estimation, round to ~100,000 (10^5). This makes QPS calculations easy: divide daily requests by 10^5.' },
        { question: 'A single Redis instance can handle approximately how many operations per second?', options: ['~1,000', '~10,000', '~100,000', '~1,000,000'], correct: 2, explanation: 'Redis is single-threaded but extremely efficient. A single instance can handle ~100,000 operations per second for simple GET/SET operations. This is why Redis is the go-to for caching and rate limiting.' },
      ]},
    ],
  },
};

