Object.assign(TOPICS, {

  'pattern-realtime-updates': {
    slug: 'pattern-realtime-updates', title: 'Real-time Updates', subtitle: 'WebSockets, SSE, long polling — when to use what', duration: '25 min', difficulty: 'Intermediate',
    sections: [
      { type: 'text', heading: 'The Problem', body: `HTTP was designed as a request-response protocol. The client asks, the server answers. But many modern features need the server to push data to clients without being asked: chat messages, live sports scores, stock prices, collaborative editing, notifications, live dashboards.\n\nThe fundamental question is: how does the server tell the client that something changed? There are three main approaches, each with different trade-offs. Understanding when to use which one is a common interview topic — it comes up in almost every system that needs "live" data.` },
      { type: 'text', heading: 'Long Polling', body: `Long polling is the simplest approach and works everywhere. The client sends a normal HTTP request, but instead of responding immediately, the server holds the connection open until new data is available (or a timeout, typically 30 seconds). When the client gets a response, it immediately sends another request.\n\nThis creates the illusion of real-time push, but it is really just the client asking "anything new?" over and over. Each response requires a new HTTP connection, which adds latency (one round trip per message).\n\nLong polling works well for low-frequency updates like notifications ("you have 3 new messages") where a 1-2 second delay is acceptable. It is also the safest fallback when WebSockets are blocked by corporate firewalls or proxies.\n\nThe downside is resource usage: each waiting client holds a server connection open. With 100K concurrent users, that is 100K open connections doing nothing most of the time.` },
      { type: 'text', heading: 'Server-Sent Events (SSE)', body: `SSE uses a single persistent HTTP connection where the server can push data to the client at any time. The client opens the connection once, and the server sends events as they happen. It is one-directional — server to client only.\n\nSSE has built-in reconnection (the browser automatically reconnects if the connection drops), event IDs for resuming where you left off, and works over standard HTTP/2 (no special protocol). It is simpler than WebSockets and sufficient for many use cases.\n\nUse SSE for: live feeds, notification streams, dashboards, stock tickers — anything where the client only needs to receive data, not send it. Twitter's live timeline updates and many analytics dashboards use SSE.\n\nThe limitation is that SSE is one-directional. If the client needs to send data back (like chat messages), you need a separate mechanism or should use WebSockets instead.` },
      { type: 'text', heading: 'WebSockets', body: `WebSockets provide a full-duplex, persistent connection between client and server. After an initial HTTP handshake that "upgrades" the connection, both sides can send messages at any time with minimal overhead (just 2-6 bytes of framing per message, compared to hundreds of bytes for HTTP headers).\n\nThis makes WebSockets ideal for bidirectional, high-frequency communication: chat applications, collaborative editing, multiplayer games, live trading platforms.\n\nThe trade-off is complexity. WebSocket connections are stateful — a user is connected to a specific server. If that server goes down, the connection is lost. You cannot simply put a load balancer in front and forget about it. You need a pub/sub system (Redis Pub/Sub, Kafka) so that any server can publish messages to any connected client, regardless of which server they are connected to.\n\nScaling WebSockets also requires sticky sessions or a connection registry (Redis mapping user_id → server_id) so you know where to route messages.` },
      { type: 'callout', variant: 'tip', heading: 'Which One to Choose?', body: `In interviews, the decision tree is simple:\n\nDo you need bidirectional communication? → WebSockets (chat, collaborative editing)\nDo you only need server → client push? → SSE (notifications, live feeds, dashboards)\nDo you need maximum compatibility with no special infrastructure? → Long Polling (fallback, simple notifications)\n\nMany production systems use a hybrid: WebSockets as the primary channel with long polling as a fallback for environments where WebSockets are blocked.` },
      { type: 'text', heading: 'Scaling Real-time Systems', body: `The biggest challenge with real-time systems is fan-out: when an event happens, how do you notify all interested clients?\n\nIf User A sends a message in a group chat with 100 members, and those 100 members are connected to 20 different WebSocket servers, you need a way to route the message to all 20 servers. This is where pub/sub comes in.\n\nThe pattern: WebSocket servers subscribe to relevant channels in Redis Pub/Sub (or Kafka). When a message arrives, it is published to the channel. All subscribed servers receive it and push to their connected clients.\n\nFor very large fan-out (a celebrity posting to 10M followers), you may need to tier the fan-out: publish to a Kafka topic, have multiple consumer groups process it in parallel, each responsible for a subset of followers.` },
      {
        type: 'architecture', heading: 'Real-time Push Architecture',
        caption: 'WebSocket servers subscribe to Redis Pub/Sub channels. Any server can publish to any connected client.',
        config: {
          width: 750, height: 300,
          nodes: [
            { id: 'producer', label: 'Event Source', sublabel: 'New message / update', icon: '📤', color: 'blue', x: 20, y: 110, w: 120, h: 56 },
            { id: 'pubsub', label: 'Redis Pub/Sub', sublabel: 'Channel routing', icon: '⚡', color: 'red', x: 220, y: 110, w: 130, h: 56 },
            { id: 'ws1', label: 'WS Server 1', sublabel: '50K connections', color: 'green', x: 430, y: 30, w: 120, h: 50 },
            { id: 'ws2', label: 'WS Server 2', sublabel: '50K connections', color: 'green', x: 430, y: 110, w: 120, h: 50 },
            { id: 'ws3', label: 'WS Server N', sublabel: '50K connections', color: 'green', x: 430, y: 190, w: 120, h: 50 },
            { id: 'clients', label: 'Connected Clients', icon: '📱', color: 'blue', x: 620, y: 110, w: 110, h: 56 },
          ],
          edges: [
            { from: 'producer', to: 'pubsub', label: 'PUBLISH', color: 'accent' },
            { from: 'pubsub', to: 'ws1', label: 'fan-out' },
            { from: 'pubsub', to: 'ws2', label: '' },
            { from: 'pubsub', to: 'ws3', label: '' },
            { from: 'ws1', to: 'clients', label: 'push', dashed: true },
            { from: 'ws2', to: 'clients', label: '', dashed: true },
          ],
        },
      },
      {
        type: 'faq', heading: 'Common Interview Questions',
        questions: [
          { q: 'When would you choose SSE over WebSockets?', a: 'When you only need server-to-client push (no bidirectional communication). SSE is simpler, has built-in reconnection, and works over standard HTTP. Use it for live feeds, notifications, and dashboards. Use WebSockets only when the client also needs to send data (chat, collaborative editing).' },
          { q: 'How do you scale WebSocket connections to millions of users?', a: 'Each WebSocket server handles ~50K-100K connections. Use a load balancer with sticky sessions (or a connection registry in Redis mapping user_id → server_id). For message routing across servers, use Redis Pub/Sub or Kafka so any server can publish to any connected client.' },
          { q: 'What happens when a WebSocket server crashes?', a: 'All connections on that server are lost. Clients must reconnect (to a different server). On reconnect, the client should fetch any missed messages using a "last seen" timestamp or sequence number. This is why you need a persistent message store (database) alongside the real-time push — WebSocket is for delivery, not storage.' },
        ],
      },
    ],
  },

  'pattern-scaling-reads': {
    slug: 'pattern-scaling-reads', title: 'Scaling Reads', subtitle: 'Read replicas, caching, CDN, and CQRS', duration: '25 min', difficulty: 'Intermediate',
    sections: [
      { type: 'text', heading: 'Why Reads Are Usually the Bottleneck', body: `Most web applications have a 10:1 to 100:1 read-to-write ratio. Think about Instagram: for every photo uploaded (1 write), it is viewed thousands of times (thousands of reads). For every tweet posted, it appears in millions of feeds.\n\nThis means scaling reads is usually the first challenge you face as traffic grows. The good news is that reads are easier to scale than writes because you can serve stale data (caching), duplicate data (replicas), and distribute data geographically (CDN) without worrying about consistency as much.\n\nHere are the techniques in order of complexity. Start with the simplest and add more as needed.` },
      { type: 'text', heading: 'Level 1: Caching', body: `The highest-impact, lowest-effort optimization. Put a cache (Redis, Memcached) between your application and database. On read: check cache first. If hit, return immediately (~1ms). If miss, query DB (~10ms), store result in cache, return.\n\nCaching works because of the Pareto principle: 20% of your data serves 80% of requests. A user's profile, a product page, a feed — these are read thousands of times but change rarely.\n\nKey decisions: what to cache (frequently read, rarely changed data), TTL (how long before the cache entry expires), and eviction policy (LRU is the default). Invalidate the cache on writes — either delete the key (lazy) or update it (eager).` },
      { type: 'text', heading: 'Level 2: Read Replicas', body: `Add replica databases that receive a copy of all writes from the primary. Route all read queries to replicas, keeping the primary for writes only.\n\nWith 3 read replicas, you can handle 4x the read throughput of a single database. Replicas can also be in different geographic regions — a replica in Mumbai serves Indian users with lower latency than a primary in Virginia.\n\nThe trade-off is replication lag. Replicas may be a few hundred milliseconds behind the primary. For most reads (feed, product page, search results), this is fine. For reads that must see the latest write (checking your own profile after editing it), route to the primary.\n\nThis pattern is called "read-after-write consistency" — after a write, read from the primary for a short window (e.g., 5 seconds), then fall back to replicas.` },
      { type: 'text', heading: 'Level 3: CDN for Static Content', body: `A CDN (Content Delivery Network) caches static content at edge servers close to users. Images, CSS, JavaScript, videos — anything that does not change per-user should be served from CDN.\n\nWithout CDN: a user in India requests an image from your server in Virginia. Round trip: ~300ms. With CDN: the image is cached at an edge server in Mumbai. Round trip: ~20ms.\n\nFor dynamic content that varies per-user (like a personalized feed), CDN does not help directly. But you can cache the API response at the CDN edge with a short TTL (e.g., 60 seconds) for content that is the same for many users (trending topics, popular products).` },
      { type: 'text', heading: 'Level 4: CQRS', body: `Command Query Responsibility Segregation separates the write model from the read model entirely. The write side uses a normalized database optimized for consistency. The read side uses a denormalized, pre-computed view optimized for fast queries.\n\nExample: an e-commerce product page needs data from 5 tables (product, reviews, seller, inventory, pricing). With CQRS, a background process listens to write events (via Kafka) and pre-computes a single "product page" document in a read-optimized store (Elasticsearch, MongoDB, or even Redis). The read path is a single lookup — no JOINs.\n\nCQRS adds significant complexity (eventual consistency between write and read models, event processing pipeline). Use it only when simpler approaches are not enough.` },
      {
        type: 'faq', heading: 'Common Interview Questions',
        questions: [
          { q: 'How do you handle cache invalidation?', a: 'The two main strategies: **delete on write** (delete the cache key when the data changes — next read will miss and repopulate) or **update on write** (update the cache when the data changes). Delete is simpler and safer. Update is faster for reads but risks inconsistency if the cache update fails. For most systems, delete + TTL is the right approach.' },
          { q: 'What is read-after-write consistency?', a: 'After a user writes data (e.g., updates their profile), they should immediately see their own changes. Route the user\'s reads to the primary database for a short window (5-10 seconds) after a write, then fall back to replicas. This gives the replica time to catch up.' },
          { q: 'When should I use a CDN vs Redis cache?', a: 'CDN caches static content (images, CSS, JS) at edge locations close to users — great for reducing latency globally. Redis caches dynamic data (API responses, database query results) at the application layer — great for reducing database load. Most systems use both: CDN for static assets, Redis for dynamic data.' },
        ],
      },
    ],
  },

  'pattern-scaling-writes': {
    slug: 'pattern-scaling-writes', title: 'Scaling Writes', subtitle: 'Sharding, write-ahead logs, and async processing', duration: '25 min', difficulty: 'Advanced',
    sections: [
      { type: 'text', heading: 'Why Writes Are Hard to Scale', body: `Writes are fundamentally harder to scale than reads. With reads, you can serve stale data, cache aggressively, and add replicas. With writes, every write must be consistent — you cannot "cache" a write or serve a "stale" write. Every write must eventually reach the source of truth.\n\nThe common bottlenecks are: a single primary database that handles all writes, synchronous writes to multiple systems (write to DB + update cache + send notification), and hot partitions where all writes go to the same shard (e.g., a trending hashtag).` },
      { type: 'text', heading: 'Technique 1: Async Write Processing', body: `The most impactful technique: do not make users wait for slow operations. Accept the write into a fast store (Redis, Kafka) and return success immediately. A background worker processes the write and persists it to the database.\n\nExamples:\n\nLike/view counts: increment a Redis counter immediately (fast), batch-write to PostgreSQL every minute (durable). The user sees the count update instantly, but the database is only written to 60x less frequently.\n\nEmail sending: write the email to a Kafka topic, return success to the user. A consumer picks it up and sends it asynchronously. The user does not wait for SMTP.\n\nAnalytics events: write to Kafka, process in batch with Flink or Spark. Never write analytics synchronously on the hot path.` },
      { type: 'text', heading: 'Technique 2: Write-Ahead Log (WAL)', body: `Before writing to the main data structure (B-tree, hash table), write to an append-only log file. Sequential writes to a log are much faster than random writes to a B-tree because disk drives (even SSDs) are optimized for sequential I/O.\n\nThe WAL serves three purposes: fast writes (sequential append), crash recovery (replay the log after a crash to reconstruct the state), and replication (send the log to replicas so they can apply the same writes).\n\nEvery major database uses WAL: PostgreSQL, MySQL, SQLite. Kafka is essentially a distributed WAL — it is an append-only log that consumers read from.\n\nLSM Trees (Log-Structured Merge Trees) take this further: all writes go to an in-memory buffer (memtable). When the buffer is full, it is flushed to disk as a sorted file (SSTable). Background compaction merges SSTables. This gives excellent write throughput at the cost of slower reads (must check multiple SSTables). Used by Cassandra, RocksDB, LevelDB.` },
      { type: 'text', heading: 'Technique 3: Sharding', body: `When a single database cannot handle the write throughput, split the data across multiple databases (shards). Each shard handles a portion of the writes.\n\nThe key decision is the shard key — it determines which shard receives each write. A good shard key distributes writes evenly and aligns with your query patterns. For a social media app, shard by user_id so all of a user's data is on one shard.\n\nSharding adds enormous complexity: cross-shard queries require scatter-gather, transactions across shards need distributed coordination, and rebalancing when adding shards requires careful data migration. This is why sharding should be a last resort after exhausting caching, replicas, and async processing.` },
      { type: 'callout', variant: 'example', heading: 'Scaling Writes in Practice', body: `A typical progression for a growing application:\n\n1. Single PostgreSQL instance (handles up to ~10K writes/sec)\n2. Add async processing for non-critical writes (analytics, notifications)\n3. Add read replicas to offload reads from the primary\n4. Shard the database when write throughput exceeds what a single primary can handle\n\nMost applications never need to go past step 2 or 3. Sharding is for systems handling millions of writes per second (Uber location updates, Twitter likes, IoT sensor data).` },
      {
        type: 'faq', heading: 'Common Interview Questions',
        questions: [
          { q: 'How do you handle a hot partition?', a: 'A hot partition occurs when one shard receives disproportionate write traffic (e.g., a trending hashtag). Solutions: add a random suffix to the shard key to spread writes across multiple shards (then aggregate on read), use a write-back cache (Redis) to absorb bursts, or use a dedicated shard for known hot keys.' },
          { q: 'What is the difference between WAL and LSM trees?', a: 'WAL (Write-Ahead Log) is a technique: write to a log before updating the main data structure. LSM trees are a data structure that uses WAL as a foundation: writes go to an in-memory buffer, which is periodically flushed to disk as sorted files. WAL is used by PostgreSQL/MySQL (B-tree based). LSM trees are used by Cassandra/RocksDB (optimized for write-heavy workloads).' },
          { q: 'When should I use Kafka vs a simple message queue (SQS)?', a: 'Use Kafka when you need: message replay (consumers can re-read old messages), multiple consumers reading the same messages (fan-out), ordering guarantees within a partition, or very high throughput (millions of messages/sec). Use SQS when you need simple job queuing with at-least-once delivery and do not need replay or ordering.' },
        ],
      },
    ],
  },

  'pattern-large-blobs': {
    slug: 'pattern-large-blobs', title: 'Handling Large Blobs', subtitle: 'Storing and serving images, videos, and files', duration: '20 min', difficulty: 'Intermediate',
    sections: [
      { type: 'text', heading: 'Never Store Blobs in Your Database', body: `This is one of the most common mistakes in system design interviews. A candidate says "I will store the image in the database" and the interviewer immediately knows they lack production experience.\n\nStoring large files (images, videos, PDFs) in a relational database causes several problems: it bloats the database size (a 10MB image per user × 100M users = 1PB), slows down backups dramatically, prevents you from using a CDN for delivery, and is far more expensive than object storage.\n\nThe rule is simple: store files in object storage (S3, GCS, Azure Blob Storage). Store only the URL or key in your database. This separation of concerns is fundamental to every production system.` },
      { type: 'text', heading: 'The Upload Pattern: Pre-Signed URLs', body: `The naive approach is: client uploads file to your API server, API server uploads to S3. This means the file travels twice and your API server becomes a bandwidth bottleneck.\n\nThe correct approach uses pre-signed URLs:\n\n1. Client asks your server: "I want to upload a 5MB image"\n2. Your server generates a pre-signed S3 URL — a temporary, authenticated URL that allows the client to upload directly to S3. This is just a cryptographic signature, no S3 call needed. Takes microseconds.\n3. Client uploads the file directly to S3 using the pre-signed URL. Your server is not involved.\n4. S3 sends a notification (via S3 Event or webhook) to your server: "upload complete"\n5. Your server saves the metadata (filename, size, S3 key) to the database\n\nThis pattern is used by every major platform: Dropbox, Instagram, YouTube, Slack. Your servers handle metadata only — S3 handles the heavy lifting of storing and serving files.` },
      { type: 'text', heading: 'Serving Files: CDN', body: `After upload, serve files through a CDN (Content Delivery Network). CDN edge servers cache files at locations close to users around the world.\n\nWithout CDN: a user in India requests an image from your S3 bucket in Virginia. Latency: ~300ms. With CDN: the image is cached at an edge server in Mumbai. Latency: ~20ms. After the first request, subsequent requests from the same region are served from the edge cache.\n\nSet appropriate Cache-Control headers:\n\nFor immutable files (images with a content hash in the URL, like profile-abc123.jpg): set max-age=31536000 (1 year). The URL changes when the content changes, so the old cached version is never served.\n\nFor mutable files (a user's profile photo at a fixed URL): set max-age=3600 (1 hour) with ETag for validation. The CDN checks with the origin if the file has changed before serving the cached version.` },
      { type: 'text', heading: 'Chunked Upload for Large Files', body: `For files larger than 10MB, single-request uploads are unreliable — any network interruption means starting over. The solution is chunked multipart upload.\n\nBreak the file into 5-10MB chunks on the client. Upload each chunk independently with its own pre-signed URL. If a chunk fails, retry just that chunk. Track progress per-chunk for a smooth progress bar.\n\nAfter all chunks are uploaded, call S3 CompleteMultipartUpload to assemble them into a single object. This is how S3 Multipart Upload works natively — you are not building this from scratch.\n\nThis pattern is essential for any system that handles large files: Dropbox (50GB files), YouTube (256GB videos), Google Drive.` },
      {
        type: 'architecture', heading: 'Pre-Signed URL Upload Flow',
        caption: 'Files go directly to S3. Your servers only handle metadata — never touch the file bytes.',
        config: {
          width: 700, height: 250,
          nodes: [
            { id: 'client', label: 'Client', icon: '📱', color: 'blue', x: 20, y: 80, w: 100, h: 56 },
            { id: 'api', label: 'API Server', sublabel: 'Metadata only', color: 'green', x: 190, y: 80, w: 120, h: 56 },
            { id: 's3', label: 'S3', sublabel: 'File storage', icon: '📦', color: 'orange', x: 390, y: 80, w: 110, h: 56 },
            { id: 'cdn', label: 'CDN', sublabel: 'Edge delivery', icon: '🌍', color: 'cyan', x: 560, y: 80, w: 110, h: 56 },
          ],
          edges: [
            { from: 'client', to: 'api', label: '1. request URL' },
            { from: 'api', to: 'client', label: '2. pre-signed URL', dashed: true },
            { from: 'client', to: 's3', label: '3. upload direct', color: 'accent' },
            { from: 's3', to: 'cdn', label: 'origin' },
          ],
        },
      },
      {
        type: 'faq', heading: 'Common Interview Questions',
        questions: [
          { q: 'Why not upload through your API server?', a: 'If the file goes Client → API Server → S3, the file travels twice and your API server becomes a bandwidth bottleneck. A 1GB video upload would consume your server\'s bandwidth for minutes. With pre-signed URLs, the client uploads directly to S3 — your server just generates a signed URL (microseconds) and handles metadata.' },
          { q: 'How do you handle resumable uploads?', a: 'Use chunked multipart upload. Each chunk has its own pre-signed URL. If the upload is interrupted, the client only needs to retry the failed chunks. Track which chunks are complete using fingerprints (SHA-256 hash of each chunk). On resume, skip chunks that are already uploaded.' },
          { q: 'How do you prevent unauthorized access to uploaded files?', a: 'Use signed URLs for downloads with short expiry (5-15 minutes). The server checks authorization before generating the signed URL. Even if the URL is shared, it expires quickly. For extra security, use CloudFront signed cookies for CDN access.' },
        ],
      },
    ],
  },

  'pattern-long-running-tasks': {
    slug: 'pattern-long-running-tasks', title: 'Managing Long Running Tasks', subtitle: 'Async job processing, progress tracking, and retries', duration: '20 min', difficulty: 'Intermediate',
    sections: [
      { type: 'text', heading: 'The Problem', body: `Some operations take too long for a synchronous HTTP request. Video transcoding takes minutes. Report generation takes seconds to minutes. Sending bulk emails to 100K users takes hours. ML model inference can take 10-30 seconds.\n\nHTTP timeouts are typically 30-60 seconds. If your operation takes longer, the connection drops and the user sees an error. Even if it finishes within the timeout, making a user stare at a spinner for 30 seconds is terrible UX.\n\nThe solution: accept the job immediately, process it in the background, and let the user check on progress.` },
      { type: 'text', heading: 'The Async Job Pattern', body: `This is one of the most common patterns in system design and comes up in almost every interview that involves processing.\n\n1. Client submits the job (e.g., "transcode this video"). Server validates the request, creates a job record in the database with status PENDING, adds the job to a message queue (Kafka, SQS, Redis), and returns immediately with the job_id and HTTP 202 Accepted.\n\n2. A pool of worker processes consumes jobs from the queue. When a worker picks up a job, it updates the status to RUNNING and begins processing.\n\n3. The worker updates progress periodically (e.g., "50% complete") in the database or Redis.\n\n4. When done, the worker updates the status to COMPLETED (or FAILED) and stores the result.\n\n5. The client either polls GET /jobs/{job_id} every few seconds to check status, or receives a push notification via WebSocket/SSE when the job completes.\n\nThis decouples the request from the processing. The API server is free immediately. Workers can be scaled independently based on queue depth.` },
      { type: 'text', heading: 'Retry Logic and Failure Handling', body: `Jobs fail. Networks drop. Servers crash. Your system must handle this gracefully.\n\n**Exponential backoff with jitter:** If a job fails, retry after 1 second, then 2, then 4, then 8, then 16... Add random jitter (±30%) to prevent all retried jobs from hitting the system at the same time (thundering herd).\n\n**Dead letter queue (DLQ):** After N retries (typically 3-5), stop retrying and move the job to a dead letter queue. An engineer reviews the DLQ to understand why jobs are failing. This prevents a poison message from blocking the queue forever.\n\n**Idempotency:** Jobs must be safe to run multiple times. If a worker crashes after processing but before acknowledging the message, the queue will redeliver it. The worker must detect "I already processed this" and skip it. Use unique job IDs and check for existing results before processing.\n\n**Timeouts:** Set a maximum processing time per job. If a worker takes longer than the timeout, assume it crashed and make the job available for another worker.` },
      { type: 'callout', variant: 'example', heading: 'Where This Pattern Shows Up', body: `Video transcoding (YouTube): upload → queue → transcode to 360p/720p/1080p/4K → notify user\nReport generation (analytics dashboard): request → queue → query data, build PDF → email to user\nImage processing (Instagram): upload → queue → resize, apply filters, generate thumbnails → update metadata\nEmail campaigns (marketing): create campaign → queue 100K individual emails → send via SES → track delivery\n\nIn interviews, whenever you see an operation that takes more than a few seconds, reach for this pattern.` },
      {
        type: 'faq', heading: 'Common Interview Questions',
        questions: [
          { q: 'How does the client know when the job is done?', a: 'Two approaches: **polling** (client calls GET /jobs/{id} every few seconds — simple but wastes requests) or **push** (server sends a WebSocket/SSE event when the job completes — efficient but requires persistent connection). For most systems, polling every 3-5 seconds is fine. For real-time UX, use WebSocket.' },
          { q: 'What if a worker crashes mid-processing?', a: 'The message queue handles this. Most queues have a "visibility timeout" — if the worker does not acknowledge the message within the timeout, the queue makes it available for another worker. The job must be idempotent so re-processing is safe.' },
          { q: 'How do you prioritize jobs?', a: 'Use separate queues for different priorities: a "high" queue for user-facing jobs (video transcoding) and a "low" queue for background jobs (analytics). Workers process the high queue first. Alternatively, use a priority queue (Redis sorted set with priority as score).' },
        ],
      },
    ],
  },

  'pattern-multi-step-processes': {
    slug: 'pattern-multi-step-processes', title: 'Multi-step Processes', subtitle: 'Sagas, distributed transactions, and workflow orchestration', duration: '25 min', difficulty: 'Advanced',
    sections: [
      { type: 'text', heading: 'The Problem', body: `Some operations span multiple services and must either all succeed or all be rolled back. Consider an e-commerce order:\n\n1. Reserve inventory (Inventory Service)\n2. Charge payment (Payment Service)\n3. Send confirmation email (Notification Service)\n4. Update order status (Order Service)\n\nWhat happens if step 2 fails after step 1 succeeded? The inventory is reserved but the payment failed. You need to "undo" the reservation. In a monolithic application with a single database, you would use a database transaction (BEGIN, ROLLBACK). But in a microservices architecture, each service has its own database. There is no single transaction that spans all of them.\n\nDistributed transactions (two-phase commit / 2PC) exist but are slow, complex, and reduce availability — if any participant is down, the entire transaction blocks. This is why most modern systems use the Saga pattern instead.` },
      { type: 'text', heading: 'The Saga Pattern', body: `A saga breaks a distributed transaction into a sequence of local transactions. Each step is a local transaction within a single service. If a step fails, compensating transactions undo the previous steps.\n\nFor the e-commerce order:\n1. Order Service: create order (status: PENDING)\n2. Inventory Service: reserve items → success → continue / fail → cancel order\n3. Payment Service: charge card → success → continue / fail → release inventory, cancel order\n4. Notification Service: send confirmation → mark order COMPLETED\n\nIf payment fails at step 3, the saga executes compensating transactions: release the inventory reservation (undo step 2) and cancel the order (undo step 1). Each compensation is itself a local transaction.\n\nThere are two ways to coordinate a saga:` },
      { type: 'text', heading: 'Choreography vs Orchestration', body: `**Choreography:** Each service listens for events and decides what to do next. Order Service publishes "OrderCreated" → Inventory Service hears it and reserves items, publishes "InventoryReserved" → Payment Service hears it and charges the card, publishes "PaymentCompleted" → and so on.\n\nPros: no central coordinator, services are loosely coupled. Cons: hard to understand the full flow (it is spread across services), hard to debug, hard to add new steps.\n\n**Orchestration:** A central Saga Orchestrator tells each service what to do and when. It maintains the state of the saga and handles failures.\n\nPros: the flow is in one place, easy to understand and modify, centralized error handling. Cons: the orchestrator is a single point of failure and can become a bottleneck.\n\nFor simple sagas (2-3 steps), choreography works fine. For complex sagas (5+ steps with branching logic), orchestration is much easier to manage. Tools like Temporal, Cadence (created by Uber), and AWS Step Functions implement the orchestration pattern.` },
      { type: 'callout', variant: 'warning', heading: 'Eventual Consistency', body: `Sagas are eventually consistent, not immediately consistent. Between step 1 and step 4, the system is in an intermediate state — the order exists but is not yet confirmed. Your UI must handle this gracefully: show "Processing your order..." instead of pretending it is instant. Design your APIs to return the current state (PENDING, PROCESSING, COMPLETED, FAILED) so the client can display appropriate feedback.` },
      { type: 'text', heading: 'Idempotency in Sagas', body: `Every step in a saga — both the forward action and the compensating action — must be idempotent. Why? Because messages can be delivered more than once (at-least-once delivery). If the "charge payment" message is delivered twice, you must not charge the customer twice.\n\nThe standard approach: include a unique saga_id and step_id with every message. Before processing, check if this step was already completed for this saga. If yes, skip it and return the cached result. This is the same idempotency key pattern used in payment systems.` },
      {
        type: 'faq', heading: 'Common Interview Questions',
        questions: [
          { q: 'When should I use choreography vs orchestration?', a: 'Choreography (event-driven) for simple sagas with 2-3 steps where services are loosely coupled. Orchestration (central coordinator) for complex sagas with 5+ steps, branching logic, or when you need clear visibility into the saga state. Most production systems with complex workflows use orchestration (Temporal, AWS Step Functions).' },
          { q: 'How do you handle a compensating transaction that fails?', a: 'Retry with exponential backoff. If it still fails after N retries, move to a dead letter queue for manual intervention. This is a rare edge case but must be handled. Some systems use a "compensation log" that records all compensations needed, and a background job retries failed compensations periodically.' },
          { q: 'What is the difference between a saga and a distributed transaction (2PC)?', a: '2PC (two-phase commit) locks all participants until the transaction completes — strong consistency but blocks on any failure. Sagas use compensating transactions — eventually consistent but never block. Sagas are preferred in microservices because 2PC reduces availability (if any participant is down, the entire transaction fails).' },
        ],
      },
    ],
  },
  'pattern-microservices': {
    slug: 'pattern-microservices', title: 'Microservices vs Monolith', subtitle: 'When to split, when to stay together, and how to migrate', duration: '30 min', difficulty: 'Intermediate',
    sections: [
      { type: 'text', heading: 'The Monolith — Where Every Application Starts', body: `A monolith is a single deployable unit that contains all of your application's code. The user service, order service, payment service, notification service — all in one codebase, one deployment, one database.\n\nMonoliths get a bad reputation, but they are the right choice for most early-stage applications. They are simple to develop (one codebase), simple to deploy (one artifact), simple to test (one test suite), and simple to debug (one log stream). Instagram ran as a Django monolith serving 400 million users. Shopify is still largely a monolith.\n\nThe problems emerge at scale — not user scale, but team scale. When 50 engineers are working on the same codebase, deployments become risky (one bug in the payment module takes down the entire application), development slows down (merge conflicts, long CI pipelines), and teams cannot deploy independently.` },

      { type: 'text', heading: 'Microservices — Independent, Deployable Services', body: `Microservices split the monolith into small, independent services, each owning a specific business domain. The user service is its own deployable with its own database. The order service is separate. The payment service is separate.\n\nEach service communicates via APIs (REST, gRPC) or events (Kafka). Each team owns their service end-to-end: development, testing, deployment, monitoring.\n\nBenefits:\n\n**Independent deployment** — Deploy the payment service without touching the user service. If the payment deploy has a bug, only payments are affected.\n\n**Technology flexibility** — The recommendation service can use Python + ML libraries while the payment service uses Java + strict typing. Each team picks the best tool for their domain.\n\n**Independent scaling** — The search service needs 20 instances during Black Friday. The user service needs 2. Scale each independently.\n\n**Team autonomy** — Each team owns their service. No merge conflicts with other teams. Faster development cycles.` },

      { type: 'text', heading: 'The Trade-offs — Microservices Are Not Free', body: `Microservices solve organizational scaling problems but introduce distributed systems complexity:\n\n**Network calls instead of function calls.** A function call takes nanoseconds. A network call takes milliseconds and can fail. Every service-to-service call needs timeout handling, retries, and circuit breakers.\n\n**Distributed transactions.** In a monolith, transferring money is one database transaction. In microservices, it spans the account service and the ledger service — you need sagas or eventual consistency.\n\n**Operational overhead.** Instead of monitoring one application, you monitor 50. Each needs its own CI/CD pipeline, logging, alerting, and on-call rotation. You need service discovery, distributed tracing (Jaeger, Zipkin), and centralized logging (ELK stack).\n\n**Data consistency.** Each service owns its database. No JOINs across services. If the order service needs user data, it must call the user service API or maintain a local copy (eventual consistency).\n\nThe rule of thumb: start with a monolith. Split into microservices only when team size and deployment frequency demand it — typically when you have 20+ engineers working on the same codebase.` },

      {
        type: 'architecture', heading: 'Monolith vs Microservices',
        caption: 'Monolith: one deployment, one database. Microservices: independent services with their own databases.',
        config: {
          width: 800, height: 320,
          nodes: [
            { id: 'mono-client', label: 'Client', icon: '📱', color: 'blue', x: 20, y: 60, w: 80, h: 50 },
            { id: 'mono-app', label: 'Monolith', sublabel: 'All code in one app', color: 'orange', x: 140, y: 40, w: 130, h: 80 },
            { id: 'mono-db', label: 'Single DB', icon: '🗄️', color: 'blue', x: 310, y: 60, w: 90, h: 50 },
            { id: 'ms-client', label: 'Client', icon: '📱', color: 'blue', x: 20, y: 210, w: 80, h: 50 },
            { id: 'ms-gw', label: 'API Gateway', color: 'cyan', x: 140, y: 210, w: 100, h: 50 },
            { id: 'ms-user', label: 'User Svc', color: 'green', x: 290, y: 170, w: 90, h: 40 },
            { id: 'ms-order', label: 'Order Svc', color: 'green', x: 290, y: 220, w: 90, h: 40 },
            { id: 'ms-pay', label: 'Payment Svc', color: 'green', x: 290, y: 270, w: 90, h: 40 },
            { id: 'ms-db1', label: 'User DB', color: 'blue', x: 430, y: 170, w: 70, h: 35 },
            { id: 'ms-db2', label: 'Order DB', color: 'blue', x: 430, y: 220, w: 70, h: 35 },
            { id: 'ms-db3', label: 'Pay DB', color: 'blue', x: 430, y: 270, w: 70, h: 35 },
          ],
          edges: [
            { from: 'mono-client', to: 'mono-app', label: '' },
            { from: 'mono-app', to: 'mono-db', label: '' },
            { from: 'ms-client', to: 'ms-gw', label: '' },
            { from: 'ms-gw', to: 'ms-user', label: '' },
            { from: 'ms-gw', to: 'ms-order', label: '' },
            { from: 'ms-gw', to: 'ms-pay', label: '' },
            { from: 'ms-user', to: 'ms-db1', label: '' },
            { from: 'ms-order', to: 'ms-db2', label: '' },
            { from: 'ms-pay', to: 'ms-db3', label: '' },
          ],
        },
      },

      { type: 'text', heading: 'How to Talk About This in Interviews', body: `In most system design interviews, you are implicitly designing a microservices architecture — each "service" you draw (Ride Service, Location Service, Matching Service) is a microservice. You do not need to explicitly discuss monolith vs microservices unless the interviewer asks.\n\nBut if asked: "I would start with a modular monolith — clear module boundaries but one deployment. As the team grows beyond 15-20 engineers, I would extract the highest-traffic or most-independently-deployed modules into microservices, starting with the ones that have the clearest domain boundaries."` },

      {
        type: 'faq', heading: 'Frequently Asked Interview Questions',
        questions: [
          { q: 'When should you move from monolith to microservices?', a: 'When team size makes independent deployment necessary (typically 20+ engineers), when different parts of the system need to scale independently, or when deployment risk is too high (one bug takes down everything). Do not split for technical reasons alone — split for organizational reasons.' },
          { q: 'How do microservices communicate?', a: 'Synchronous: REST or gRPC for request-response (user service calls payment service). Asynchronous: Kafka or message queues for event-driven communication (order service publishes "OrderCreated", notification service consumes it). Prefer async for decoupling.' },
          { q: 'What is a service mesh?', a: 'A dedicated infrastructure layer (Istio, Linkerd) that handles service-to-service communication: load balancing, retries, circuit breakers, mutual TLS, observability. It runs as a sidecar proxy alongside each service, so the application code does not need to implement these concerns.' },
        ],
      },
    ],
  },

  'pattern-availability': {
    slug: 'pattern-availability', title: 'Availability & Failover', subtitle: 'Designing systems that survive failures — redundancy, replication, and failover', duration: '30 min', difficulty: 'Intermediate',
    sections: [
      { type: 'text', heading: 'What is Availability?', body: `Availability is the percentage of time a system is operational and serving requests. It is measured in "nines":\n\n**99% (two nines)** — 3.65 days of downtime per year. Unacceptable for most production systems.\n**99.9% (three nines)** — 8.77 hours of downtime per year. Acceptable for internal tools.\n**99.99% (four nines)** — 52.6 minutes of downtime per year. Standard for production web applications.\n**99.999% (five nines)** — 5.26 minutes of downtime per year. Required for critical infrastructure (payment systems, emergency services).\n\nEach additional nine is exponentially harder and more expensive to achieve. Going from 99.9% to 99.99% requires fundamentally different architecture — redundancy at every layer, automated failover, and no single points of failure.` },

      { type: 'text', heading: 'Eliminating Single Points of Failure', body: `A single point of failure (SPOF) is any component whose failure takes down the entire system. In a system design interview, the interviewer will often ask "what happens if X goes down?" Your answer should always be: "we have redundancy."\n\n**Load Balancer** — Run multiple instances. Use DNS round-robin or a cloud provider's managed load balancer (AWS ALB) which is inherently redundant.\n\n**Application Servers** — Run multiple stateless instances behind the load balancer. If one dies, the load balancer routes to the others. This is why servers must be stateless — no user data stored on the server.\n\n**Database** — Run a primary with one or more replicas. If the primary fails, promote a replica (failover). PostgreSQL streaming replication, MySQL replication, or managed services (AWS RDS Multi-AZ) handle this.\n\n**Cache (Redis)** — Run Redis Sentinel or Redis Cluster with replicas. If the primary Redis node fails, Sentinel promotes a replica automatically.\n\n**Message Queue (Kafka)** — Each partition has replicas on different brokers. If a broker fails, the replica takes over as partition leader.` },

      { type: 'text', heading: 'Failover Patterns', body: `**Active-Passive (Hot Standby)**\nThe active server handles all traffic. The passive server is idle but receives replicated data. If the active fails, the passive takes over (failover). Downtime during failover: seconds to minutes.\n\nUsed for: databases (primary-replica), Redis (Sentinel), single-leader systems.\n\nTrade-off: the passive server is wasted capacity during normal operation.\n\n**Active-Active**\nMultiple servers handle traffic simultaneously. If one fails, the others absorb its load. No failover delay — traffic is already distributed.\n\nUsed for: stateless application servers (behind a load balancer), multi-region deployments (each region serves its local users).\n\nTrade-off: more complex — need to handle data consistency across active nodes. For databases, this means multi-master replication with conflict resolution.\n\n**Circuit Breaker**\nWhen a downstream service is failing, stop calling it instead of waiting for timeouts. The circuit breaker "opens" after N consecutive failures, returning errors immediately. Periodically, it allows one test request through ("half-open"). If it succeeds, the circuit closes and normal traffic resumes.\n\nThis prevents cascade failures — if the payment service is down, the order service fails fast instead of queuing up thousands of timeout requests that consume threads and memory.` },

      { type: 'animation', id: 'circuit-breaker', heading: 'Circuit Breaker Pattern', body: 'Closed (normal) → Open (failing, reject all) → Half-Open (test one request) → Closed or Open.' },

      { type: 'text', heading: 'Replication Strategies', body: `**Synchronous replication:** The primary waits for the replica to acknowledge the write before confirming to the client. Guarantees no data loss on failover. Trade-off: higher write latency (must wait for replica).\n\n**Asynchronous replication:** The primary confirms the write immediately, then sends it to the replica in the background. Lower latency, but the replica may be slightly behind. If the primary fails before replicating, those writes are lost.\n\n**Semi-synchronous:** The primary waits for at least one replica to acknowledge, but not all. Balances durability and latency. Used by MySQL semi-sync replication.\n\nFor most systems: use asynchronous replication for read replicas (slight staleness is acceptable) and synchronous replication for the failover replica (no data loss on primary failure).` },

      { type: 'text', heading: 'Multi-Region Availability', body: `For global applications, a single region is a SPOF — if the entire AWS us-east-1 region goes down (it has happened), your application is offline.\n\nMulti-region deployment runs your application in 2+ geographic regions. Each region has its own application servers, caches, and database replicas. Users are routed to the nearest region via GeoDNS or a global load balancer.\n\nThe hard part is data: how do you keep databases in sync across regions? Options:\n\n**Read replicas in each region:** Writes go to the primary region, replicas in other regions serve reads. Cross-region replication lag: 50-200ms. Good for read-heavy workloads.\n\n**Multi-master (active-active):** Each region can accept writes. Conflicts resolved by last-write-wins, vector clocks, or CRDTs. Used by DynamoDB Global Tables, Cassandra.\n\n**Region-specific data:** Users in EU are served by the EU region, their data lives there. No cross-region replication needed for user-specific data. Good for compliance (GDPR).` },

      {
        type: 'faq', heading: 'Frequently Asked Interview Questions',
        questions: [
          { q: 'What is the difference between availability and reliability?', a: 'Availability is the percentage of time the system is operational. Reliability is the probability that the system performs correctly for a given period. A system can be available (responding to requests) but unreliable (returning wrong results). Both matter, but availability is what interviewers usually ask about.' },
          { q: 'How do you achieve 99.99% availability?', a: 'Redundancy at every layer (no SPOF), automated failover (no human intervention needed), health checks with fast detection (<10 seconds), graceful degradation (serve cached data if DB is down), multi-AZ deployment (survive datacenter failure), and comprehensive monitoring with alerting.' },
          { q: 'What happens to in-flight requests during failover?', a: 'They fail. The client must retry. This is why idempotency matters — retried requests must be safe to execute multiple times. For critical operations (payments), use idempotency keys so retries do not cause duplicate charges.' },
          { q: 'What is graceful degradation?', a: 'When a component fails, the system continues operating with reduced functionality instead of failing completely. Example: if the recommendation service is down, show trending items instead of personalized recommendations. If Redis is down, fall back to database queries (slower but functional).' },
        ],
      },
    ],
  },

  'pattern-security': {
    slug: 'pattern-security', title: 'Security Basics', subtitle: 'Authentication, authorization, encryption, and common vulnerabilities', duration: '25 min', difficulty: 'Intermediate',
    sections: [
      { type: 'text', heading: 'Why Security Matters in System Design', body: `Security is not a feature you add later — it is baked into every design decision. In interviews, mentioning security proactively shows engineering maturity. You do not need to be a security expert, but you should know the basics: how authentication works, how to protect data in transit and at rest, and how to prevent the most common attacks.\n\nThe interviewer is not expecting a security deep dive, but they will notice if you say "we store passwords in plain text" or "the API accepts any request without authentication." These are red flags.` },

      { type: 'text', heading: 'Authentication — Who Are You?', body: `Authentication verifies the identity of the user or service making a request.\n\n**JWT (JSON Web Tokens)** — The standard for modern APIs. After login, the server creates a signed token containing the user's ID, roles, and expiration time. The client sends this token in the Authorization header with every request. The server verifies the signature without hitting a database — the token is self-contained.\n\nKeep JWTs short-lived (15-60 minutes). Use refresh tokens for long-lived sessions. JWTs cannot be revoked before expiration (unless you maintain a blacklist, which defeats the purpose of stateless tokens).\n\n**OAuth 2.0** — The framework for "Sign in with Google/GitHub." The user authenticates with the identity provider (Google), which gives your app a token to access the user's data. Complex but standard for third-party integrations.\n\n**API Keys** — Simple tokens for server-to-server communication. Identify the calling application, not the user. Good for rate limiting per client. Not suitable for user-specific access control.` },

      { type: 'text', heading: 'Authorization — What Are You Allowed to Do?', body: `Authorization determines what an authenticated user can access.\n\n**RBAC (Role-Based Access Control)** — Users are assigned roles (admin, editor, viewer). Each role has permissions. When a request comes in, check: does this user's role have permission for this action on this resource? Simple and widely used.\n\n**ABAC (Attribute-Based Access Control)** — Policies based on user attributes (department, location), resource attributes (classification, owner), and environment (time of day, IP address). More flexible than RBAC but more complex.\n\nIn system design: always check authorization at the API layer. Never trust the client to enforce access control. "The frontend hides the delete button" is not security — the API must reject unauthorized requests regardless of what the frontend shows.` },

      { type: 'text', heading: 'Encryption — Protecting Data', body: `**In transit (HTTPS/TLS):** All communication between client and server must use HTTPS. TLS encrypts data so eavesdroppers cannot read it. There is no excuse for serving an API over plain HTTP in production. In interviews, mention this early and move on — it is table stakes.\n\n**At rest:** Sensitive data stored in databases or object storage should be encrypted. AWS services (RDS, S3) support encryption at rest with managed keys (KMS). This protects against physical theft of storage media.\n\n**Passwords:** Never store passwords in plain text. Use bcrypt, scrypt, or Argon2 to hash passwords with a salt. These algorithms are intentionally slow, making brute-force attacks impractical. When a user logs in, hash the provided password and compare with the stored hash.\n\n**Sensitive data (credit cards):** Never store raw credit card numbers. Use tokenization — the payment provider (Stripe, Razorpay) stores the card and gives you a token. You store the token, not the card. This is required by PCI DSS compliance.` },

      { type: 'text', heading: 'Common Vulnerabilities to Mention', body: `**SQL Injection:** Never concatenate user input into SQL queries. Use parameterized queries or an ORM. This is the #1 web vulnerability and interviewers expect you to know it.\n\n**XSS (Cross-Site Scripting):** Sanitize all user-generated content before rendering in HTML. Use Content Security Policy headers. Frameworks like React auto-escape by default.\n\n**CSRF (Cross-Site Request Forgery):** Use CSRF tokens for state-changing requests from browsers. SameSite cookies help prevent this.\n\n**Rate Limiting:** Protect APIs from brute-force attacks and DDoS. Limit requests per user/IP per time window. Return 429 Too Many Requests.\n\n**CORS (Cross-Origin Resource Sharing):** Do not set Access-Control-Allow-Origin: * on authenticated endpoints. Whitelist specific origins.\n\n**Input Validation:** Validate all input on the server. Check types, lengths, ranges, and formats. Never trust data from the client.` },

      {
        type: 'faq', heading: 'Frequently Asked Interview Questions',
        questions: [
          { q: 'How do you store passwords securely?', a: 'Hash with bcrypt (or Argon2) with a unique salt per password. Never store plain text. Never use MD5 or SHA-256 for passwords (too fast, vulnerable to brute force). bcrypt is intentionally slow (~100ms per hash), making brute-force attacks impractical.' },
          { q: 'What is the difference between authentication and authorization?', a: 'Authentication: "Who are you?" (verify identity — login, JWT, OAuth). Authorization: "What can you do?" (check permissions — RBAC, ABAC). Authentication happens first, then authorization.' },
          { q: 'How do you handle API security in microservices?', a: 'External requests: authenticate at the API Gateway (validate JWT). Internal service-to-service: use mutual TLS (mTLS) or service mesh (Istio) for encrypted, authenticated communication. Never expose internal services to the public internet.' },
          { q: 'What is the principle of least privilege?', a: 'Give each component only the minimum permissions it needs. The search service should not have write access to the payment database. Database users should have only the permissions their service requires. This limits the blast radius of a security breach.' },
        ],
      },
    ],
  },
});

// ── KEY TECHNOLOGIES ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────
Object.assign(TOPICS, {

  'tech-redis': {
    slug: 'tech-redis', title: 'Redis', subtitle: 'In-memory data store — caching, queues, pub/sub, and more', duration: '35 min', difficulty: 'Intermediate',
    sections: [
      { type: 'text', heading: 'What is Redis and Why Does It Matter?', body: `Redis (Remote Dictionary Server) is an open-source, in-memory data structure store. It keeps all data in RAM, which is why a Redis GET takes about 0.1 milliseconds compared to 5-10ms for a PostgreSQL query — a 50-100x speedup. That difference is the reason Redis appears in virtually every system design interview.\n\nBut Redis is not just a cache. It is a Swiss Army knife for distributed systems. It provides data structures (strings, lists, sets, sorted sets, hashes), pub/sub messaging, Lua scripting, geospatial indexes, streams, and more — all with atomic operations and sub-millisecond latency.\n\nRedis is single-threaded by design. This sounds like a limitation, but it is actually what makes Redis simple and fast: every command executes atomically without locks. No race conditions, no deadlocks. A single Redis instance handles approximately 100,000 operations per second. When you need more, Redis Cluster shards data across multiple nodes.\n\nCompanies using Redis in production: Twitter (timeline caching), GitHub (job queues), Snapchat (rate limiting), Stack Overflow (caching), Pinterest (social graph), Uber (geospatial), Discord (presence).` },

      { type: 'text', heading: 'Data Structures — The Real Power of Redis', body: `What sets Redis apart from simple key-value stores like Memcached is its rich data structures. Each one maps to specific system design use cases. Understanding which structure to use for which problem is what interviewers look for.\n\n**String** — The simplest type. A key maps to a value (up to 512MB). But strings are more powerful than they look: INCR atomically increments a counter, SETNX (set-if-not-exists) implements distributed locks, and SETEX sets a value with an expiration time.\n\nUse for: cached API responses, session tokens, counters (page views, rate limiting), distributed locks.\n\n**Hash** — A key maps to a set of field-value pairs. Think of it as a mini-object or a row in a table. HGET retrieves a single field, HGETALL retrieves all fields. More memory-efficient than storing each field as a separate string key.\n\nUse for: user profiles (HSET user:42 name "Alice" email "alice@example.com"), shopping carts, configuration objects.\n\n**List** — An ordered collection of strings with O(1) push/pop from both ends. LPUSH adds to the head, RPOP removes from the tail. BRPOP blocks until an element is available — this turns a list into a message queue.\n\nUse for: message queues (LPUSH + BRPOP), activity feeds (latest N items with LTRIM), task queues.\n\n**Set** — An unordered collection of unique strings. SADD adds, SISMEMBER checks membership in O(1), SINTER computes intersection of two sets.\n\nUse for: tags, unique visitors (SADD visitors:2024-04-17 user_id), mutual friends (SINTER friends:alice friends:bob), deduplication.\n\n**Sorted Set (ZSet)** — Like a Set, but each member has a numeric score. Members are automatically sorted by score. ZADD adds with score, ZRANGE returns members by rank, ZRANGEBYSCORE returns members within a score range.\n\nThis is the most versatile data structure in Redis. Use for: leaderboards (score = points), rate limiting (score = timestamp), feed ranking (score = relevance), priority queues (score = priority), sliding window counters.\n\n**Bitmap** — A string treated as an array of bits. SETBIT sets a single bit, BITCOUNT counts set bits. Extremely memory-efficient: tracking 100 million users' daily activity takes only 12.5MB (1 bit per user).\n\nUse for: feature flags, daily active users, bloom filters.\n\n**HyperLogLog** — A probabilistic data structure that estimates the cardinality (count of unique elements) of a set using only 12KB of memory, regardless of how many elements you add. The trade-off is ~0.81% error rate.\n\nUse for: counting unique visitors, unique search queries, unique events — anywhere you need approximate counts at massive scale.` },

      { type: 'animation', id: 'cache-flow', heading: 'Cache-Aside Pattern with Redis', body: 'The most common caching pattern: check Redis first, fall back to database on miss, populate cache for next time.' },

      { type: 'text', heading: 'Use Case Deep Dive: Caching', body: `Caching is the most common use of Redis in system design. The pattern is called cache-aside (or lazy loading):\n\n1. Application receives a read request\n2. Check Redis: GET user:42\n3. If cache hit → return immediately (~0.1ms)\n4. If cache miss → query PostgreSQL (~5-10ms)\n5. Store result in Redis: SET user:42 {data} EX 3600 (1-hour TTL)\n6. Return result\n\nThis simple pattern reduces database load by 80-95% for read-heavy workloads. The TTL ensures stale data eventually expires. On writes, delete the cache key (DEL user:42) so the next read fetches fresh data.\n\nCache sizing: if your hot dataset is 10GB and each Redis node has 16GB RAM, a single node suffices. For larger datasets, use Redis Cluster. For most applications, 80% of traffic hits 20% of data (Pareto principle), so you only need to cache the hot set.\n\nEviction policy: when Redis reaches maxmemory, it needs to evict keys. allkeys-lru (evict least recently used) is the best default for caching. volatile-lru only evicts keys with a TTL set. noeviction returns errors on writes — use this when Redis is a primary store, not a cache.` },

      { type: 'text', heading: 'Use Case Deep Dive: Distributed Locks', body: `When multiple servers need to coordinate access to a shared resource (e.g., only one server should process a payment at a time), you need a distributed lock. Redis SETNX (Set if Not eXists) is the standard approach:\n\n\`\`\`\nSETNX lock:payment:order123 server_id EX 30\n\`\`\`\n\nIf SETNX returns 1 → you acquired the lock. Process the payment.\nIf SETNX returns 0 → another server holds the lock. Wait or fail.\n\nThe EX 30 (30-second TTL) is critical: if the lock holder crashes, the lock auto-expires and another server can acquire it. Without TTL, a crashed server would hold the lock forever (deadlock).\n\nWhen done, release the lock: DEL lock:payment:order123. But only if you still own it — use a Lua script to atomically check ownership and delete:\n\n\`\`\`\nif redis.call("GET", KEYS[1]) == ARGV[1] then\n  return redis.call("DEL", KEYS[1])\nend\nreturn 0\n\`\`\`\n\nThis pattern appears in Ticketmaster (seat holds), Uber (driver locking), and any system that needs mutual exclusion across distributed servers.` },

      { type: 'text', heading: 'Use Case Deep Dive: Rate Limiting', body: `Redis is the standard backend for distributed rate limiting. The simplest approach uses INCR + EXPIRE (fixed window):\n\n\`\`\`\nkey = "ratelimit:{user_id}:{current_minute}"\ncount = INCR key\nif count == 1:\n    EXPIRE key 60\nif count > 100:\n    return 429 Too Many Requests\n\`\`\`\n\nFor more accuracy, use a sorted set (sliding window):\n\n\`\`\`\nZADD ratelimit:{user_id} {timestamp} {request_id}\nZREMRANGEBYSCORE ratelimit:{user_id} 0 {timestamp - 60}\ncount = ZCARD ratelimit:{user_id}\nif count > 100: reject\n\`\`\`\n\nThe sorted set approach is more accurate (no boundary burst problem) but uses more memory. For most production systems, the INCR approach with a Lua script (to make INCR + EXPIRE atomic) is sufficient.` },

      { type: 'text', heading: 'Pub/Sub and Real-time Communication', body: `Redis Pub/Sub enables real-time message broadcasting. A publisher sends a message to a channel, and all subscribers on that channel receive it instantly.\n\n\`\`\`\nSUBSCRIBE chat:room:42\nPUBLISH chat:room:42 "Hello everyone!"\n\`\`\`\n\nThis is how WebSocket fan-out works in distributed systems: each WebSocket server subscribes to relevant Redis channels. When a chat message arrives at any server, it publishes to the channel. All subscribed servers receive it and push to their connected clients.\n\nLimitation: Pub/Sub is fire-and-forget. If a subscriber is disconnected when a message is published, it misses the message. For durable messaging, use Redis Streams (append-only log, similar to Kafka) or an actual message broker like Kafka.` },

      { type: 'text', heading: 'Persistence: RDB vs AOF', body: `Redis stores everything in memory, but it can persist data to disk for durability.\n\n**RDB (Redis Database Backup)** takes periodic snapshots. Redis forks the process (copy-on-write) and the child writes the entire dataset to a binary file. Recovery is fast (just load the file), but you lose all writes since the last snapshot. Configure with save directives: "save 900 1" means snapshot if at least 1 key changed in 900 seconds.\n\n**AOF (Append-Only File)** logs every write command. On restart, Redis replays the log to reconstruct the state. Three fsync policies: always (safest, slowest — fsync every write), everysec (good balance — fsync every second, lose at most 1 second of data), no (fastest, OS decides when to flush).\n\nFor production caching: disable both (data loss is acceptable, DB is the source of truth). For Redis as a primary store (sessions, locks): use AOF with everysec. For disaster recovery: use both RDB + AOF.` },

      { type: 'text', heading: 'Redis Cluster and Scaling', body: `A single Redis instance is limited by the RAM of one machine (typically 16-64GB) and ~100K ops/sec. Redis Cluster distributes data across multiple nodes using hash slots.\n\nRedis has 16,384 hash slots. Each key is assigned to a slot: CRC16(key) % 16384. Each node owns a range of slots. When you add a node, some slots are migrated to it — only the keys in those slots move.\n\nClients use smart routing: they know which node owns which slots and send commands directly to the right node. No proxy needed.\n\nReplication: each primary node has one or more replicas. If a primary fails, a replica is promoted automatically (Redis Sentinel or Cluster's built-in failover).\n\nFor most applications, 3-6 Redis nodes with replicas provide sufficient capacity and availability. Start with a single instance and scale to Cluster only when you exceed the capacity of one node.` },

      {
        type: 'faq', heading: 'Frequently Asked Interview Questions',
        questions: [
          { q: 'What happens when Redis runs out of memory?', a: 'Behavior depends on the maxmemory-policy setting. noeviction returns errors on new writes (default). allkeys-lru evicts the least recently used key to make room. volatile-lru evicts only keys with a TTL. volatile-ttl evicts keys closest to expiration. For caching, always use allkeys-lru.' },
          { q: 'Redis is single-threaded — how can it handle 100K ops/sec?', a: 'All data is in memory (no disk I/O wait), operations are simple (O(1) hash lookups, O(log N) sorted set ops), and the single thread avoids context switching and lock contention. Network I/O is multiplexed using epoll/kqueue. The bottleneck is usually network bandwidth, not CPU.' },
          { q: 'When should I use Redis vs Memcached?', a: 'Redis when you need: data structures beyond strings (sorted sets, lists, hashes), persistence, pub/sub, Lua scripting, or geospatial. Memcached when you need: pure key-value caching with multi-threaded performance and simpler operations. In practice, Redis is the default choice for almost everything.' },
          { q: 'How do you handle cache stampede (thundering herd)?', a: 'When a popular cache key expires, hundreds of requests simultaneously miss the cache and hit the database. Solutions: request coalescing (only one request rebuilds the cache, others wait), probabilistic early expiration (refresh the key before it actually expires), or locking (acquire a lock before rebuilding, others return stale data).' },
          { q: 'Can Redis be used as a primary database?', a: 'Yes, with persistence enabled (AOF + RDB). Redis is used as a primary store for sessions, leaderboards, real-time analytics, and feature flags. However, it lacks SQL queries, JOINs, and complex transactions. For most applications, use Redis as a cache/auxiliary store alongside PostgreSQL as the primary database.' },
          { q: 'How does Redis handle replication?', a: 'Asynchronous replication: the primary sends write commands to replicas. Replicas apply them in order. Replication lag is typically sub-millisecond within the same datacenter. If the primary fails, a replica can be promoted (manually or via Redis Sentinel). Note: async replication means a write acknowledged by the primary might be lost if it fails before replicating.' },
        ],
      },
    ],
  },

  'tech-kafka': {
    slug: 'tech-kafka', title: 'Kafka', subtitle: 'Distributed event streaming for high-throughput pipelines', duration: '35 min', difficulty: 'Advanced',
    sections: [
      { type: 'text', heading: 'What is Kafka and Why Does It Matter?', body: `Apache Kafka is a distributed event streaming platform — fundamentally, it is a durable, ordered, append-only log that multiple systems can write to and read from independently. Unlike a traditional message queue (SQS, RabbitMQ) where messages are deleted after consumption, Kafka retains messages for a configurable period (hours, days, or weeks), allowing consumers to replay events from any point in time.\n\nKafka was built at LinkedIn in 2011 to handle their activity stream — billions of events per day from page views, clicks, and user actions. It is now the backbone of event-driven architectures at Netflix (1.4 trillion messages/day), Uber (trillions of messages/day), Airbnb, Spotify, and most large-scale systems.\n\nIn system design interviews, Kafka appears whenever you need to: decouple services (the producer does not need to know about consumers), process events asynchronously (analytics, notifications, search indexing), guarantee ordering within a partition, or build a pipeline where multiple downstream systems consume the same events independently.` },

      { type: 'text', heading: 'Core Concepts', body: `Understanding Kafka's building blocks is essential. Each concept maps to a specific design decision.\n\n**Topic** — A named stream of events. Think of it as a category or feed name. Examples: "user-signups", "order-events", "click-stream", "payment-completed". Topics are the logical channels through which data flows.\n\n**Partition** — A topic is split into one or more partitions. Each partition is an ordered, immutable sequence of messages stored on disk. Partitions are the unit of parallelism — more partitions means more consumers can read simultaneously. Messages within a single partition are strictly ordered. Messages across different partitions have no ordering guarantee.\n\n**Offset** — Each message in a partition has a unique, sequential offset number (0, 1, 2, 3...). Consumers track their position by offset. This is how replay works — a consumer can reset its offset to 0 and re-read all messages from the beginning.\n\n**Producer** — Writes events to topics. The producer chooses which partition to write to based on a partition key: hash(key) % num_partitions. If no key is provided, messages are distributed round-robin across partitions.\n\n**Consumer** — Reads events from topics at its own pace. Each consumer tracks its offset per partition. Consumers can be fast or slow — Kafka does not care. Messages are not deleted after reading.\n\n**Consumer Group** — Multiple consumers that share the work of reading a topic. Each partition is assigned to exactly one consumer in the group. If you have 12 partitions and 4 consumers, each consumer reads 3 partitions. If a consumer crashes, its partitions are reassigned to the remaining consumers (rebalancing).` },

      { type: 'animation', id: 'message-queue', heading: 'Kafka Producer-Consumer Flow', body: 'Producers write to topic partitions. Consumer groups share the work. Messages persist on disk for configurable retention.' },

      { type: 'text', heading: 'Why Kafka Over a Simple Message Queue?', body: `A traditional message queue (SQS, RabbitMQ) follows a "consume and delete" model: once a consumer reads a message, it is gone. Kafka follows a "consume and retain" model. This fundamental difference enables several powerful patterns:\n\n**Replay:** A new analytics service can read the entire history of "order-events" from the beginning. With SQS, those messages are gone forever. This is invaluable for debugging, reprocessing after a bug fix, or bootstrapping a new service.\n\n**Multiple independent consumers:** The order service, analytics service, notification service, and search indexer can all read the same "order-events" topic independently, each at their own pace, each with their own offset. With SQS, you would need to duplicate messages to multiple queues.\n\n**Event sourcing:** Store every state change as an event in Kafka. Reconstruct the current state by replaying events. This gives you a complete audit trail and the ability to rebuild any derived view.\n\n**High throughput:** Kafka achieves millions of messages per second by writing sequentially to disk (sequential I/O is as fast as memory on modern SSDs), batching messages, compressing in transit, and using zero-copy transfers from disk to network. A single Kafka broker handles ~1 million messages/sec.` },

      { type: 'text', heading: 'Partitioning Strategy — The Key Design Decision', body: `The partition key determines which partition a message goes to, and therefore which consumer processes it. This is the most important design decision when using Kafka.\n\n**Rule 1: Same key → same partition → ordered processing.** All messages with the same key are guaranteed to be in the same partition and processed in order by the same consumer. Use this for events that must be processed sequentially: all events for order #123 (created → paid → shipped → delivered) should use order_id as the partition key.\n\n**Rule 2: No key → round-robin → maximum throughput.** If ordering does not matter (independent events like page views), omit the key. Messages are distributed evenly across partitions for maximum parallelism.\n\n**Rule 3: Choose partition count carefully.** You cannot reduce the number of partitions later (only increase). Start with 3-6x the number of consumers you expect. Too few partitions limits parallelism. Too many creates overhead (each partition has a leader, replicas, and file handles).\n\nExample: for an e-commerce system with 4 consumer instances, start with 12 partitions (3x consumers). Use order_id as the partition key for order events, user_id for user events.` },

      { type: 'text', heading: 'Delivery Guarantees', body: `Kafka provides three delivery semantics, and understanding them is critical for interviews:\n\n**At-most-once:** The consumer commits the offset before processing the message. If it crashes during processing, the message is skipped on restart. Fast but can lose messages. Use for: metrics, logs where occasional loss is acceptable.\n\n**At-least-once (default):** The consumer processes the message, then commits the offset. If it crashes after processing but before committing, the message is redelivered on restart. No message loss, but duplicates are possible. Use for: most applications. Make your processing idempotent to handle duplicates.\n\n**Exactly-once:** Kafka supports exactly-once semantics (EOS) using transactional producers and idempotent writes. The producer, Kafka broker, and consumer coordinate to ensure each message is processed exactly once. Use for: financial transactions, inventory updates — anywhere duplicates cause real harm. Note: EOS adds latency and complexity.` },

      { type: 'text', heading: 'Common Kafka Patterns in System Design', body: `**Event-driven microservices:** Services communicate through Kafka topics instead of direct API calls. The order service publishes "OrderCreated" to Kafka. The inventory service, payment service, and notification service each consume from this topic independently. Services are decoupled — adding a new consumer requires zero changes to the producer.\n\n**Change Data Capture (CDC):** Capture every INSERT/UPDATE/DELETE from your PostgreSQL database and publish to Kafka using Debezium. Downstream systems (Elasticsearch for search, Redis for caching, data warehouse for analytics) consume these events and stay in sync. This replaces fragile dual-write patterns.\n\n**Stream processing:** Use Kafka Streams or Apache Flink to process events in real-time as they flow through Kafka. Examples: real-time fraud detection (analyze payment events), live dashboards (aggregate click events), recommendation updates (process user behavior events).\n\n**Audit log:** Every action in the system is published to Kafka. Since Kafka retains messages, you have a complete, immutable audit trail. Compliance teams can replay events to reconstruct what happened and when.` },

      {
        type: 'faq', heading: 'Frequently Asked Interview Questions',
        questions: [
          { q: 'How does Kafka guarantee message ordering?', a: 'Ordering is guaranteed within a partition, not across partitions. Use a partition key (e.g., order_id) to ensure all related messages go to the same partition. Within that partition, messages are strictly ordered by offset.' },
          { q: 'What happens if a Kafka consumer crashes?', a: 'The consumer group rebalances — the crashed consumer\'s partitions are reassigned to the remaining consumers in the group. Each new consumer resumes from the last committed offset for its assigned partitions. Messages are not lost because Kafka retains them on disk.' },
          { q: 'When should I use Kafka vs SQS vs RabbitMQ?', a: 'Kafka: event streaming, audit logs, CDC, when you need replay, multiple consumers, or very high throughput. SQS: simple job queues on AWS, serverless, when you do not need ordering or replay. RabbitMQ: complex routing patterns, RPC, when you need flexible message routing and acknowledgment.' },
          { q: 'How does Kafka achieve such high throughput?', a: 'Sequential disk writes (as fast as memory on SSDs), message batching (producers batch messages before sending), compression (gzip/snappy/lz4), zero-copy transfers (data goes from disk to network socket without copying through application memory), and partitioning (parallel writes and reads across partitions).' },
          { q: 'What is a consumer group rebalance and why does it matter?', a: 'When a consumer joins or leaves a group, Kafka reassigns partitions among the remaining consumers. During rebalancing, consumption pauses briefly. Frequent rebalances (from flaky consumers) cause processing delays. Minimize rebalances by using stable consumer instances and appropriate session timeouts.' },
          { q: 'How do you handle poison messages (messages that always fail processing)?', a: 'After N retry attempts, publish the failed message to a dead letter topic (DLT). A separate consumer or manual process reviews the DLT. This prevents a single bad message from blocking the entire partition. Always log the original message, error, and retry count for debugging.' },
        ],
      },
    ],
  },

  'tech-elasticsearch': {
    slug: 'tech-elasticsearch', title: 'Elasticsearch', subtitle: 'Full-text search and analytics at scale', duration: '30 min', difficulty: 'Intermediate',
    sections: [
      { type: 'text', heading: 'What is Elasticsearch and Why Does It Matter?', body: `Elasticsearch is a distributed search and analytics engine built on Apache Lucene. It is designed for one thing: finding data fast. While a relational database can do a LIKE '%search term%' query, it requires a full table scan — O(N) and painfully slow on millions of rows. Elasticsearch uses an inverted index that makes full-text search effectively O(1) regardless of dataset size.\n\nElasticsearch is the "E" in the ELK stack (Elasticsearch, Logstash, Kibana) used for log analytics at companies like Netflix and Uber. But it is also the search engine behind product search (Amazon, eBay), autocomplete (Google search suggestions), geospatial search (find restaurants near me), and time-series analytics.\n\nIn system design interviews, introduce Elasticsearch whenever the problem requires search: "users should be able to search events by name" (Ticketmaster), "users should be able to search videos" (YouTube), "users should be able to search products" (e-commerce). It is never the primary database — always a secondary index synced from your source of truth.` },

      { type: 'text', heading: 'The Inverted Index — How Search Works', body: `A traditional database index (B-tree) maps a key to a row: user_id 42 → row data. This is great for exact lookups but terrible for text search.\n\nAn inverted index flips the relationship: it maps every word to all documents containing that word:\n\n\`\`\`\n"wireless"   → [product_1, product_5, product_12]\n"headphones" → [product_1, product_3, product_5]\n"bluetooth"  → [product_1, product_5, product_8]\n\`\`\`\n\nSearching for "wireless bluetooth headphones" finds the intersection: documents containing all three words → [product_1, product_5]. This is O(1) per term regardless of how many products exist.\n\nBut Elasticsearch does much more than simple word matching:\n\n**Tokenization:** "Running shoes for women" is split into tokens: ["running", "shoes", "for", "women"]. Stop words like "for" can be removed.\n\n**Stemming:** "running" is reduced to its root "run". Now a search for "run" matches documents containing "running", "runs", "runner".\n\n**Synonyms:** "car" can be configured to also match "automobile", "vehicle".\n\n**Fuzzy matching:** "aple" matches "apple" using edit distance (Levenshtein distance). Handles typos gracefully.\n\n**Relevance scoring (BM25):** Documents are ranked by how well they match the query. A product with "wireless headphones" in the title ranks higher than one with "wireless" buried in the description. BM25 considers term frequency (how often the word appears in the document) and inverse document frequency (how rare the word is across all documents).` },

      { type: 'text', heading: 'Architecture: Indexes, Shards, and Replicas', body: `**Index** — A collection of documents, analogous to a database table. Each document is a JSON object with fields. You might have a "products" index, a "users" index, and a "logs" index.\n\n**Shard** — An index is split into shards distributed across nodes. Each shard is a self-contained Lucene index. More shards = more parallelism for both writes and searches. A search query is sent to all shards in parallel, results are merged.\n\n**Replica** — A copy of a shard on a different node. Provides redundancy (if a node dies, replicas serve the data) and read scalability (search queries can hit any replica). Default: 1 replica per shard.\n\n**Near real-time search:** When you index a document, it is not immediately searchable. Elasticsearch buffers writes in memory and "refreshes" every 1 second by default, flushing the buffer to a searchable segment. This 1-second delay is the trade-off for write performance. You can force a refresh for immediate searchability, but it hurts throughput.\n\n**Write path:** Document → primary shard → replicated to replica shards → searchable after next refresh.\n**Search path:** Query → sent to all relevant shards (primary or replica) in parallel → results merged and ranked → returned to client.` },

      { type: 'text', heading: 'Syncing Elasticsearch with Your Database', body: `Elasticsearch is NOT a primary database. It is eventually consistent, does not support ACID transactions, and can lose data during node failures. Your source of truth should always be a relational database (PostgreSQL, MySQL).\n\nThe standard pattern: write to PostgreSQL first (source of truth), then sync changes to Elasticsearch for search. Three approaches:\n\n**1. Application-level dual write:** After writing to PostgreSQL, also write to Elasticsearch. Simple but fragile — if the ES write fails, your search index is out of sync. Not recommended.\n\n**2. Change Data Capture (CDC):** Use Debezium to capture every INSERT/UPDATE/DELETE from PostgreSQL's WAL (write-ahead log) and publish to Kafka. An Elasticsearch consumer reads from Kafka and updates the index. Near-real-time sync (~1-2 seconds), decoupled, reliable. This is the recommended approach.\n\n**3. Periodic batch sync:** A cron job queries PostgreSQL for recently changed records and bulk-indexes them into Elasticsearch. Simple but has higher latency (minutes). Good for non-critical search where freshness is not important.` },

      { type: 'text', heading: 'Common Use Cases in System Design', body: `**Product search (e-commerce):** Index product name, description, category, brand. Support fuzzy matching ("iphne" → "iPhone"), faceted search (filter by price range, brand, rating), and autocomplete.\n\n**Log analytics (ELK stack):** Index application logs with timestamp, level, message, service name. Query: "show me all ERROR logs from the payment service in the last hour." Kibana provides dashboards.\n\n**Autocomplete / typeahead:** Use edge n-gram tokenizer: "apple" is indexed as "a", "ap", "app", "appl", "apple". When the user types "app", it matches instantly. The completion suggester uses an in-memory FST (finite state transducer) for sub-millisecond lookups.\n\n**Geospatial search:** Elasticsearch supports geo_point and geo_shape types. Query: "find all restaurants within 5km of this location." Uses geohashing internally, similar to Redis GEORADIUS.` },

      { type: 'callout', variant: 'warning', heading: 'Elasticsearch is NOT a Primary Database', body: `Never use Elasticsearch as your source of truth. It is eventually consistent (1-second refresh delay), does not support transactions, and can lose acknowledged writes during node failures. Always store data in PostgreSQL first, sync to Elasticsearch for search. If Elasticsearch goes down, your application still works — search is degraded but core functionality is intact.` },

      {
        type: 'faq', heading: 'Frequently Asked Interview Questions',
        questions: [
          { q: 'How do you keep Elasticsearch in sync with the primary database?', a: 'Use CDC (Change Data Capture) with Debezium: capture changes from PostgreSQL\'s WAL, publish to Kafka, consume into Elasticsearch. This gives near-real-time sync (~1-2 seconds) with minimal coupling. Avoid dual writes — they are fragile and can lead to inconsistency.' },
          { q: 'How do you implement autocomplete/typeahead?', a: 'Two approaches: edge n-gram tokenizer (indexes prefixes: "apple" → "a", "ap", "app"...) or the completion suggester (in-memory FST for sub-millisecond lookups). Edge n-grams are more flexible (support fuzzy matching). Completion suggester is faster but less flexible.' },
          { q: 'When should I use Elasticsearch vs PostgreSQL full-text search?', a: 'PostgreSQL\'s tsvector/tsquery works well for simple search on small-to-medium datasets (<1M rows). Use Elasticsearch when you need: complex relevance scoring, fuzzy matching, autocomplete, faceted search, geospatial search, or search across millions of documents.' },
          { q: 'How do you handle Elasticsearch going down?', a: 'Search is degraded but the application still works because PostgreSQL is the source of truth. Show a "search temporarily unavailable" message or fall back to a simpler PostgreSQL LIKE query. When ES recovers, it catches up via the CDC pipeline.' },
          { q: 'How many shards should an index have?', a: 'Rule of thumb: each shard should be 10-50GB. For a 100GB index, use 3-10 shards. Too few shards limits parallelism. Too many shards creates overhead (each shard consumes memory and file handles). You cannot change the shard count after index creation — plan ahead.' },
        ],
      },
    ],
  },

  'tech-postgresql': {
    slug: 'tech-postgresql', title: 'PostgreSQL', subtitle: 'The world\'s most advanced open-source relational database', duration: '30 min', difficulty: 'Intermediate',
    sections: [
      { type: 'text', heading: 'Why PostgreSQL is the Default Choice', body: `In system design interviews, when someone asks "what database should I use?", the safest answer is PostgreSQL. It handles 90% of use cases well: relational data with ACID transactions, JSON documents (JSONB), full-text search, geospatial queries (PostGIS), and time-series data.\n\nPostgreSQL is not the fastest at any single thing — Redis is faster for caching, Cassandra handles more writes, Elasticsearch is better at search. But PostgreSQL is good enough at everything, which means you can start with one database and add specialized stores only when you have a proven need.\n\nInstagram ran on a single PostgreSQL instance for years before sharding. Discord uses PostgreSQL for their core data. Notion, Supabase, and thousands of startups build on PostgreSQL. It is open source, battle-tested for 35+ years, and has the richest ecosystem of extensions.` },

      { type: 'text', heading: 'ACID Transactions — Why They Matter', body: `ACID is the defining feature of relational databases and the reason you choose PostgreSQL over NoSQL for critical data.\n\n**Atomicity:** A transaction either completes entirely or not at all. Transferring money: debit account A and credit account B happen together. If the credit fails, the debit is rolled back. There is no state where money has left A but not arrived at B.\n\n**Consistency:** The database moves from one valid state to another. Constraints (unique keys, foreign keys, check constraints) are enforced. You cannot insert an order referencing a customer that does not exist.\n\n**Isolation:** Concurrent transactions do not interfere with each other. PostgreSQL uses MVCC (Multi-Version Concurrency Control) — each transaction sees a snapshot of the database at the time it started. Writers do not block readers. This gives excellent concurrency for most workloads.\n\n**Durability:** Once a transaction is committed, it survives crashes. PostgreSQL writes to the WAL (write-ahead log) before acknowledging the commit. Even if the server loses power immediately after, the data is safe on disk.\n\nUse PostgreSQL when correctness matters: financial transactions, inventory management, user accounts, orders, anything where losing or corrupting data causes real harm.` },

      { type: 'text', heading: 'Key Features for System Design', body: `**JSONB:** Store and query JSON documents with full indexing support. You can store a user's preferences as a JSONB column and query individual fields: WHERE preferences->>'theme' = 'dark'. GIN indexes make JSONB queries fast. This blurs the line between SQL and document databases — you get the flexibility of MongoDB with the reliability of PostgreSQL.\n\n**Advanced Indexing:** B-tree (default — range queries, equality, ORDER BY), Hash (equality only, rarely used), GiST (geospatial with PostGIS, full-text search), GIN (arrays, JSONB, full-text search — indexes every element), BRIN (Block Range Index — extremely compact for naturally ordered data like timestamps). Choosing the right index type is a common interview topic.\n\n**Table Partitioning:** Split large tables by range (date), list (region), or hash. Built-in since PostgreSQL 10. Essential for tables with billions of rows — queries only scan relevant partitions.\n\n**Logical Replication:** Stream changes (INSERT/UPDATE/DELETE) to other PostgreSQL instances or external systems. This is how you implement CDC to sync with Elasticsearch, build read replicas, or feed a data warehouse.\n\n**Connection Pooling (PgBouncer):** PostgreSQL creates a new OS process per connection (~10MB overhead each). With 1000 concurrent connections, that is 10GB just for connection overhead. PgBouncer multiplexes thousands of application connections onto a smaller pool of database connections (e.g., 100). This is essential for any production deployment.` },

      { type: 'text', heading: 'Performance and Scaling', body: `A single PostgreSQL instance on modern hardware handles:\n\n- 5,000-20,000 simple queries/sec (SELECT by primary key)\n- 5,000-10,000 writes/sec (simple INSERTs)\n- Tables with billions of rows (with proper indexing and partitioning)\n- Databases up to several terabytes\n\nWhen you exceed these limits, scale in this order:\n\n1. **Optimize queries and indexes** — Use EXPLAIN ANALYZE to find slow queries. Add missing indexes. This alone often solves the problem.\n2. **Connection pooling (PgBouncer)** — Reduces connection overhead dramatically.\n3. **Read replicas** — Route read queries to replicas, keep the primary for writes. PostgreSQL supports streaming replication with sub-second lag.\n4. **Caching (Redis)** — Cache hot data to reduce database load by 80-95%.\n5. **Table partitioning** — For very large tables, partition by date or region.\n6. **Sharding** — Last resort. Split data across multiple PostgreSQL instances. Adds enormous complexity (cross-shard queries, distributed transactions). Most applications never need this.\n\nThe key message for interviews: start with a single PostgreSQL instance. It handles more than you think. Add complexity only when you have measured evidence that you need it.` },

      { type: 'callout', variant: 'tip', heading: 'VACUUM — The Hidden Maintenance Task', body: `When PostgreSQL updates or deletes a row, the old version is not immediately removed — other transactions might still need it (MVCC). VACUUM reclaims this dead space. Without regular VACUUM, tables bloat and performance degrades.\n\nPostgreSQL runs autovacuum by default, but write-heavy tables may need tuning: lower the autovacuum threshold so it runs more frequently. Monitor table bloat with pg_stat_user_tables. This is a common production issue that interviewers may ask about.` },

      {
        type: 'faq', heading: 'Frequently Asked Interview Questions',
        questions: [
          { q: 'When should I choose PostgreSQL over MongoDB?', a: 'PostgreSQL when you need: ACID transactions, complex JOINs, strong consistency, relational data with foreign keys. MongoDB when: your data is naturally document-shaped (deeply nested JSON), your schema changes frequently, or you need horizontal write scaling beyond what a single PostgreSQL primary can handle. PostgreSQL\'s JSONB support means you can store documents in PostgreSQL too.' },
          { q: 'How does PostgreSQL handle concurrent writes?', a: 'MVCC (Multi-Version Concurrency Control). Each transaction sees a snapshot of the database. Writers do not block readers. Concurrent writes to the same row are serialized using row-level locks — the second writer waits until the first commits or rolls back. This gives excellent concurrency without explicit locking in most cases.' },
          { q: 'What is the difference between B-tree and GIN indexes?', a: 'B-tree indexes a single value per row — great for equality, range queries, and ORDER BY on scalar columns. GIN (Generalized Inverted Index) indexes every element within a value — great for arrays, JSONB fields, and full-text search where a single row contains multiple searchable elements.' },
          { q: 'How do you handle PostgreSQL connection limits?', a: 'PostgreSQL creates one OS process per connection (~10MB each). Default max_connections is 100. For applications with many concurrent users, use PgBouncer as a connection pooler — it multiplexes thousands of app connections onto a smaller pool of DB connections. This is essential for any production deployment.' },
          { q: 'When should I partition a table?', a: 'When a table exceeds ~100M rows and queries consistently filter by the partition key (usually a date range). Partitioning by month means a query for "last 7 days" only scans 1-2 partitions instead of the entire table. Also useful for data retention — drop old partitions instead of DELETE (instant vs hours).' },
        ],
      },
    ],
  },

  'tech-cassandra': {
    slug: 'tech-cassandra', title: 'Cassandra', subtitle: 'Wide-column store for write-heavy, time-series workloads', duration: '20 min', difficulty: 'Advanced',
    sections: [
      { type: 'text', heading: 'What is Cassandra?', body: `Cassandra is a distributed wide-column store designed for:\n- Write-heavy workloads\n- Time-series data\n- High availability (no single point of failure)\n- Linear horizontal scalability\n\n**Used by:** Netflix, Apple, Instagram, Discord` },
      { type: 'text', heading: 'Data Model', body: `**Keyspace** → **Table** → **Row**\n\nRows identified by **partition key** (determines which node stores the data)\nWithin a partition, rows sorted by **clustering key**\n\n**Design principle:** Model your data around your queries, not your relationships.\nDenormalize aggressively. Joins don't exist.\n\n**Example — Messages:**\nPartition key: conversation_id\nClustering key: timestamp DESC\nQuery: "Get last 50 messages in conversation X" → single partition read` },
      { type: 'text', heading: 'Consistency Levels', body: `Cassandra lets you choose consistency per query:\n\n**ONE** — Fastest, least consistent. One replica responds.\n**QUORUM** — Majority of replicas respond. Good balance.\n**ALL** — All replicas respond. Slowest, most consistent.\n\n**Tunable consistency:** Write QUORUM + Read QUORUM = strong consistency\nWrite ONE + Read ONE = eventual consistency (fastest)` },
      { type: 'callout', variant: 'warning', heading: 'Cassandra Anti-patterns', body: 'No JOINs, no transactions, no secondary indexes at scale. Do not use Cassandra if you need complex queries or ACID. Use it for: time-series, event logs, message storage, IoT data.' },
      {
        type: 'faq', heading: 'Common Interview Questions',
        questions: [
          { q: 'When should I use Cassandra vs PostgreSQL?', a: 'Cassandra when you need massive write throughput (millions/sec), time-series data, multi-region replication, or no single point of failure. PostgreSQL for everything else — complex queries, transactions, JOINs.' },
          { q: 'How does Cassandra achieve high write throughput?', a: 'Writes go to an in-memory buffer (memtable) and a commit log (sequential append). No random disk I/O on writes. Memtables are periodically flushed to disk as SSTables. This LSM-tree approach optimizes for writes at the cost of slower reads.' },
          { q: 'What is a partition key and why does it matter?', a: 'The partition key determines which node stores the data (via consistent hashing). All data with the same partition key is on the same node. Choose a key with high cardinality that matches your query patterns. Bad partition key = hot spots.' },
        ],
      },
    ],
  },

  'tech-dynamodb': {
    slug: 'tech-dynamodb', title: 'DynamoDB', subtitle: 'AWS managed NoSQL — serverless, scalable key-value store', duration: '25 min', difficulty: 'Intermediate',
    sections: [
      { type: 'text', heading: 'What is DynamoDB and When to Use It', body: `DynamoDB is AWS's fully managed NoSQL database. "Fully managed" means AWS handles provisioning, patching, replication, backups, and scaling — you just define your table and start reading/writing. It provides single-digit millisecond latency at any scale, from 10 requests/sec to 10 million requests/sec.\n\nDynamoDB is a key-value and document store. Every item (row) is identified by a primary key and can contain any number of attributes (columns). Unlike relational databases, there is no fixed schema — different items in the same table can have different attributes.\n\nUse DynamoDB when: you are on AWS, need serverless auto-scaling, have simple access patterns (get by key, query by key + sort), or need global distribution (Global Tables replicate across regions). Avoid when: you need complex queries with JOINs, ad-hoc analytics, or SQL.` },
      { type: 'text', heading: 'Data Model and Access Patterns', body: `DynamoDB's data model revolves around the primary key, which comes in two forms:\n\n**Simple primary key (partition key only):** Each item is uniquely identified by one attribute. Example: user_id. You can only GET by exact partition key.\n\n**Composite primary key (partition key + sort key):** Items are grouped by partition key and ordered by sort key within the partition. Example: partition key = user_id, sort key = order_date. You can query all orders for a user, sorted by date.\n\nThe critical design principle: you must know your access patterns before designing the table. DynamoDB does not support ad-hoc queries. Every query must use the primary key or a secondary index.\n\n**Global Secondary Index (GSI):** An alternate primary key for the table. If your table is keyed by user_id but you also need to query by email, create a GSI with email as the partition key. GSIs are eventually consistent.\n\n**Single-table design:** Advanced pattern where you store multiple entity types (users, orders, products) in one table using generic PK/SK attributes. Reduces the number of tables and enables complex access patterns with a single query. Popular in the DynamoDB community but adds complexity.` },
      { type: 'text', heading: 'Capacity and Pricing', body: `DynamoDB offers two capacity modes:\n\n**On-demand:** Pay per request. No capacity planning needed. Great for unpredictable workloads or new applications where you do not know the traffic pattern yet. More expensive per request but zero waste.\n\n**Provisioned:** You specify read and write capacity units (RCU/WCU). Cheaper per request but you pay for provisioned capacity even if unused. Use auto-scaling to adjust capacity based on traffic. Best for predictable workloads.\n\nOne RCU = one strongly consistent read/sec for items up to 4KB. One WCU = one write/sec for items up to 1KB. Larger items consume more units.\n\n**DynamoDB Streams:** A change data capture (CDC) feature. Every INSERT/UPDATE/DELETE is published to a stream that Lambda functions or other consumers can process. Use for: syncing to Elasticsearch, triggering notifications, building materialized views.` },
      {
        type: 'faq', heading: 'Frequently Asked Interview Questions',
        questions: [
          { q: 'When should I use DynamoDB vs PostgreSQL?', a: 'DynamoDB for: serverless applications, simple key-value access patterns, auto-scaling without ops overhead, global distribution. PostgreSQL for: complex queries, JOINs, transactions, ad-hoc analytics, or when you need SQL. If your access patterns are simple and you are on AWS, DynamoDB is excellent.' },
          { q: 'What is a hot partition and how do you avoid it?', a: 'A hot partition occurs when one partition key receives disproportionate traffic. DynamoDB throttles the hot partition even if total table capacity is sufficient. Avoid by choosing a high-cardinality partition key. If unavoidable (e.g., a viral post), add a random suffix to the key to spread writes across partitions, then aggregate on read.' },
          { q: 'What is the difference between strongly consistent and eventually consistent reads?', a: 'Eventually consistent reads (default) may return slightly stale data — the replica might not have the latest write yet. Strongly consistent reads always return the latest data but cost 2x the RCU and have higher latency. Use strong consistency for critical reads (checking inventory before purchase), eventual for everything else.' },
        ],
      },
    ],
  },

  'tech-api-gateway': {
    slug: 'tech-api-gateway', title: 'API Gateway', subtitle: 'Single entry point for all client requests', duration: '25 min', difficulty: 'Intermediate',
    sections: [
      { type: 'text', heading: 'What is an API Gateway and Why Every System Needs One', body: `An API Gateway is a server that sits between clients and your backend services, acting as the single entry point for all requests. Instead of clients calling 10 different microservices directly, they call one API Gateway which routes requests to the right service.\n\nThis is not just a convenience — it is a fundamental architectural pattern. Without an API Gateway, every microservice must independently handle authentication, rate limiting, CORS, SSL termination, logging, and monitoring. With an API Gateway, these cross-cutting concerns are handled in one place.\n\nIn system design interviews, the API Gateway is one of the first components you should draw. It sits right after the load balancer (or is combined with it) and before your application services.` },
      { type: 'text', heading: 'Core Functions', body: `**Request routing:** Route /users/* to the User Service, /orders/* to the Order Service, /search/* to the Search Service. Clients do not need to know the internal service topology.\n\n**Authentication and authorization:** Validate JWT tokens, API keys, or OAuth tokens before forwarding requests. Reject unauthorized requests at the gateway — they never reach your services.\n\n**Rate limiting:** Enforce per-client or per-API rate limits. Return 429 Too Many Requests when limits are exceeded. Protects backend services from abuse.\n\n**SSL termination:** Decrypt HTTPS at the gateway. Internal communication between the gateway and services can use plain HTTP (within a private network), reducing CPU overhead on every service.\n\n**Request/response transformation:** Convert between protocols (REST to gRPC), add/remove headers, transform payloads. Useful when migrating from monolith to microservices.\n\n**Caching:** Cache responses for idempotent GET requests. Reduces load on backend services for frequently requested data.\n\n**Logging and monitoring:** Log every request with timing, status code, and client info. Feed into monitoring dashboards for observability.` },
      { type: 'text', heading: 'Popular API Gateways', body: `**AWS API Gateway** — Fully managed, serverless. Integrates with Lambda, DynamoDB. Good for AWS-native architectures. Supports REST, HTTP, and WebSocket APIs.\n\n**Kong** — Open-source, built on Nginx. Plugin ecosystem for auth, rate limiting, logging. Can run on-premise or in the cloud. Popular for Kubernetes deployments.\n\n**Nginx** — Not a dedicated API gateway, but widely used as one. Reverse proxy with routing, SSL termination, and rate limiting. Extremely fast and battle-tested.\n\n**Envoy** — Modern L7 proxy built for microservices. Used as a sidecar proxy in service mesh architectures (Istio). Supports gRPC, HTTP/2, and advanced load balancing.\n\n**Traefik** — Cloud-native, auto-discovers services in Docker/Kubernetes. Simple configuration with labels/annotations.` },
      { type: 'callout', variant: 'warning', heading: 'The API Gateway is a Single Point of Failure', body: `If the API Gateway goes down, nothing works. Mitigate this by: running multiple gateway instances behind a load balancer, using health checks to remove unhealthy instances, implementing circuit breakers to prevent cascade failures when backend services are slow, and having a fallback mode that returns cached responses or graceful error pages.` },
      {
        type: 'faq', heading: 'Frequently Asked Interview Questions',
        questions: [
          { q: 'What is the difference between an API Gateway and a Load Balancer?', a: 'A load balancer distributes traffic across multiple instances of the same service (Layer 4 or Layer 7). An API Gateway routes requests to different services based on the URL path, handles auth, rate limiting, and protocol translation. In practice, they are often combined — Nginx can do both.' },
          { q: 'Should I use an API Gateway in a monolithic application?', a: 'Not necessarily. API Gateways shine in microservices architectures where you have many services. For a monolith, a simple reverse proxy (Nginx) with SSL termination and rate limiting is sufficient. Adding a full API Gateway to a monolith adds complexity without much benefit.' },
          { q: 'How do you handle API versioning at the gateway?', a: 'Route /v1/* to the old service and /v2/* to the new service. The gateway handles the routing — clients just change the URL prefix. This allows you to run both versions simultaneously during migration. Set a deprecation timeline and sunset the old version.' },
        ],
      },
    ],
  },

  'tech-zookeeper': {
    slug: 'tech-zookeeper', title: 'ZooKeeper', subtitle: 'Distributed coordination service', duration: '25 min', difficulty: 'Advanced',
    sections: [
      { type: 'text', heading: 'What is ZooKeeper and Why Does It Exist?', body: `Distributed systems have a fundamental coordination problem: how do multiple servers agree on who is the leader? How do they share configuration without conflicts? How do they discover each other?\n\nZooKeeper is a centralized service that solves these coordination problems. It provides a simple, reliable, and fast coordination kernel that other distributed systems build upon. Kafka uses ZooKeeper for broker coordination and leader election. HBase uses it for master election. Hadoop uses it for configuration management.\n\nThink of ZooKeeper as a tiny, highly reliable filesystem that all servers in your cluster can read from and write to, with built-in notifications when data changes. It is not designed for storing large amounts of data — each node (znode) should hold at most a few kilobytes. It is designed for coordination metadata.` },
      { type: 'text', heading: 'Core Concepts: Znodes and Watches', body: `ZooKeeper maintains a hierarchical namespace, like a filesystem:\n\n\`\`\`\n/\n├── /services\n│   ├── /services/user-service\n│   │   ├── /services/user-service/instance-1  (data: "10.0.1.5:8080")\n│   │   └── /services/user-service/instance-2  (data: "10.0.1.6:8080")\n│   └── /services/order-service\n├── /config\n│   └── /config/feature-flags  (data: {"dark_mode": true})\n└── /locks\n    └── /locks/payment-lock-order123\n\`\`\`\n\nEach node (znode) can store a small amount of data and have children.\n\n**Persistent znodes:** Survive client disconnects. Used for configuration, service registry.\n\n**Ephemeral znodes:** Automatically deleted when the client that created them disconnects. This is the key feature — if a server crashes, its ephemeral znode disappears, and other servers are notified. Used for service discovery, leader election, distributed locks.\n\n**Sequential znodes:** ZooKeeper appends a monotonically increasing number to the name. Used for ordering (leader election: lowest sequence number wins).\n\n**Watches:** A client can set a watch on a znode. When the znode changes (data modified, children added/removed, znode deleted), ZooKeeper sends a one-time notification to the watching client. This enables event-driven coordination without polling.` },
      { type: 'text', heading: 'Common Patterns', body: `**Leader Election:** All servers create ephemeral sequential znodes under /election. The server with the lowest sequence number is the leader. Others watch the znode just before theirs. If the leader crashes, its ephemeral znode is deleted, the next server is notified and becomes the new leader.\n\n**Service Discovery:** Each service instance creates an ephemeral znode under /services/{service-name} with its address as data. Clients watch the directory — when instances join or leave, they are notified and update their routing table. If a service crashes, its ephemeral znode disappears automatically.\n\n**Distributed Locks:** To acquire a lock, create an ephemeral sequential znode under /locks/{resource}. If your znode has the lowest sequence number, you hold the lock. Otherwise, watch the znode just before yours. When it is deleted (lock released or holder crashed), you are notified and check again.\n\n**Configuration Management:** Store configuration in persistent znodes. All servers watch the config znode. When an admin updates the config, all servers are notified and reload. No need to restart services or deploy new code for config changes.` },
      { type: 'callout', variant: 'info', heading: 'ZooKeeper Alternatives', body: `ZooKeeper is being replaced in some systems by newer alternatives:\n\n**etcd** — Used by Kubernetes for cluster state. Simpler API (key-value with watches), built-in Raft consensus. Preferred for new Kubernetes-native systems.\n\n**Consul** — HashiCorp's service mesh and service discovery tool. Combines service discovery, health checking, and KV store.\n\n**Kafka without ZooKeeper (KRaft)** — Kafka 3.3+ can run without ZooKeeper using its own Raft-based consensus. This simplifies Kafka deployments significantly.` },
      {
        type: 'faq', heading: 'Frequently Asked Interview Questions',
        questions: [
          { q: 'Why does Kafka use ZooKeeper?', a: 'Kafka uses ZooKeeper for: broker registration (which brokers are alive), topic metadata (which partitions exist on which brokers), leader election (which broker is the leader for each partition), and consumer group coordination. Note: Kafka 3.3+ supports KRaft mode which eliminates the ZooKeeper dependency.' },
          { q: 'What happens if ZooKeeper goes down?', a: 'ZooKeeper runs as a cluster (ensemble) of 3 or 5 nodes. It tolerates the failure of a minority of nodes (1 out of 3, or 2 out of 5). If a majority fails, ZooKeeper becomes read-only — existing data can be read but no writes are accepted. Systems that depend on ZooKeeper (like Kafka) continue operating with their last known state but cannot elect new leaders.' },
          { q: 'What is the difference between ZooKeeper and Redis for distributed locks?', a: 'ZooKeeper locks use ephemeral znodes — if the lock holder crashes, the znode is automatically deleted and the lock is released. Redis locks use SETNX with TTL — if the holder crashes, the lock expires after the TTL. ZooKeeper is more reliable (no TTL guessing) but slower. Redis is faster but requires careful TTL management. For critical locks (payment processing), prefer ZooKeeper. For performance-sensitive locks (seat holds), prefer Redis.' },
        ],
      },
    ],
  },
});

